//@version=6
indicator(
     "Earnings Print Detector v10.1",
     shorttitle="EPD v10.1",
     overlay=true,
     max_labels_count=500,
     max_lines_count=500,
     max_bars_back=5000
)

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// EVERYDAY SETTINGS  (v7.6)
//
// Pine renders groups in the order their FIRST member is declared, so a group can
// only be put at the top by declaring its inputs first. These three are MOVED from
// further down, not duplicated - there is no behaviour change whatsoever, and each
// one is gone from its old group so there is exactly one control per setting.
//
// Adding more later is cheap: move the declaration up here and change its group.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
gTop = "★  EVERYDAY SETTINGS"

atrTighten = input.bool(true, "Trailing stop: tighten it as the trade ages", group=gTop,
     tooltip="RETUNED IN v7.3 AND NOW ON BY DEFAULT - HERE IS THE MEASUREMENT.\n\nThe mechanism works like this: it aims at a target a multiple of CURRENT volatility behind price and closes a fraction of the gap each bar, so it tightens as volatility decays overnight and loosens (advances LESS per favourable move) when volatility expands at the open. It is fed through the one-way ratchet, so it can never move the stop backwards.\n\nWHY IT WAS OFF, AND WHY IT IS NOW ON. On the ORIGINAL tuning - 30-bar volatility, quarter-gap steps - it cost [n]R across the acceptance set, so it shipped off rather than ship a default only measured to lose. v7.3 made the response much more dramatic (10-bar volatility, half-gap steps, faster phase-in) and the cost went to ZERO. Re-measured on every trade, ON versus OFF, at the shipped distance of 5 ATRs:\n\n  CRWV 08-11 .......... [n]R -> [n]R   unchanged\n  CSCO 08-12 .......... [n]R -> [n]R   unchanged\n  SPCE 08-12 .......... [n]R -> [n]R   unchanged\n  NBIS all trades ...... [n]   IDENTICAL\n\nNet cost ZERO, down from [n]R. The objection that kept it off is gone.\n\nTHE MECHANISM IS PROVABLY LIVE, not merely inert. Drop the distance below and it bites immediately: on CSCO at 3.0 ATRs the trade exits on a trailed stop at 115.42 for [n]R, and at 1.5 ATRs at 114.94 for [n]R, instead of running to the session-end exit at 113.17. At the shipped 5.0 it is active but is rarely the BINDING constraint, which is exactly why it now costs nothing.\n\nSTILL UNMEASURED, AND WORTH SAYING PLAINLY: the acceptance set contains no trade that REVERSES at the regular-hours open, which is the one case this feature exists to protect. So the cost is now known to be zero and the benefit is still unproven. It ships on because zero-cost-plus-possible-benefit is a better default than zero-cost-plus-nothing, not because the benefit has been demonstrated.\n\nTHE ORIGINAL TUNING'S NUMBERS, kept for the record:\n\n  CRWV 08-11 long ........ [n]R -> [n]R   unchanged\n  SPCE 08-12 short ....... [n]R -> [n]R   unchanged\n  NBIS 08-12 re-entry .... [n]R -> [n]R   GAINS [n]R\n  CSCO 08-12 short ....... [n]R -> [n]R   COSTS [n]R\n  NBIS 08-11 long ........ [n]R -> [n]R   COSTS [n]R\n\nNet cost [n]R across [n] trades. Some untouched, some slightly better, some worse. AND HERE IS THE HONEST CAVEAT: every trade in that set CONTINUED in its original direction. On a sample of winners a tighter stop can only ever look like a cost. The benefit is real but unmeasured, because the sample contains no instance of it.\n\nThat was the correct call at the time. It no longer applies at the v7.3 tuning. Switch it OFF to get the old, calmer behaviour back.\n\nHISTORICAL NOTE ON THE ORIGINAL v6.1 VERSION. Half an hour later that volatility has usually collapsed, and the stop is left absurdly far from price.\n\nThis proposes a stop atrTightMult ATRs from the close on every confirmed bar. It is fed through the SAME one-way ratchet as every other trail mechanism, so it can only ever tighten - it can never hand back profit that has already been locked.\n\nIT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT LOOSEN. Any widening from the current ratchet level would do exactly that, so this implementation only tightens or holds. If volatility expands, the stop simply stops being pulled in.")
useHold = input.bool(false, "Stay in the trade  (override the early exits)", group=gTop,
     tooltip="THE MANUAL OVERRIDE.\n\nFIRST, THE HONEST LIMITATION. There is no button, because Pine Script cannot have one. The panel is drawn with table cells, which are display-only, and the language has no click handler of any kind. An input is the only control a Pine script can offer, so this is a switch in Settings rather than something you press on the chart.\n\nWHAT IT SUPPRESSES: FLAT TAPE, EXHAUSTED and the structural / trailing STOP.\n\nWHAT IT WILL NOT SUPPRESS (v7.4): the SESSION END exit. Flat tape and exhausted are OPINIONS about whether the move is still working, and the override exists to overrule an opinion you disagree with. Session end is not an opinion - it is the DEFINITION of how long an earnings trade lives. Once a full regular session has traded on the release the edge is spent, and what is left is an ordinary swing position carried on an earnings-shaped stop. Staying in the trade means refusing a premature exit, not turning the trade into something it was never meant to be. That boundary always holds.\n\nTHE STOP STILL MARKS. So the X still appears when the stop level is touched, and the tooltip on it says what you WOULD have banked - but the position stays open, the take-profits keep working, the stop keeps trailing and the panel keeps reporting. It marks ONCE per trade, on the first touch - measured on BABA, re-arming on every recovery produced ten X markers on a single trade.\n\nBE AWARE OF WHAT THIS MEANS: with nothing able to close the trade, the R figures the panel quotes are open P/L on a position whose risk is no longer being enforced. That is the point of a manual override, but it is worth knowing.\n\nNOTHING IS RECONSTRUCTED. The exit simply never fires, so the position was never closed. The original entry, R, stop, ladder, targets, add markers and panel all carry straight on. That is why it is NOT a re-entry and does not draw one - it is the same trade, still running, marked with its own cyan HOLD badge.\n\nRELEASES ITSELF when the trade genuinely ends, so the next trade starts clean.")
// v8.1 - MOVED UP FROM THE TRAILING GROUP AND NOW ON BY DEFAULT.
revExit = input.bool(true, "Exit the trade on a reversal candle", group=gTop,
     tooltip="ONE DETECTOR, TWO CONSEQUENCES. The reversal test is the same one that draws the badge when you are flat - same gates, same magnitude, same direction rule. What differs is only what happens next:\n\n  FLAT       - it arms the opposite direction and waits for a confirmation.\n  IN A TRADE - it closes the trade at that bar's close AND arms the opposite direction on that same candle.\n\nv9.1 CORRECTED THIS TEXT. It used to say the arming waited for a later bar once the position was flat. Both consequences now land on the one bar.\n\nThe old build also refused to fire this exit until the trade was already up [n]R, which meant a reversal could never rescue a LOSING trade - LYFT 2026-08-06 is the case. That condition is gone.")

// v8.1 - THE EXHAUSTION VETO.
maxMovePct = input.float(9.0, "Skip the trade once the move is this % of price", minval=0.0, maxval=100.0, step=0.5, group=gTop,
     tooltip="Measured from the OPEN of the print candle to the CLOSE of the confirmation candle - the whole move, wicks excluded - as a percentage of price. At or above this the trade is refused.\n\nWHY A VETO AND NOT JUST A TIGHTER STOP. A stop does not guarantee the fill. You are filled at the candle close. On the backtest LYFT realised [n]% against a stop implying [n]%, and AAOI [n]% against one implying [n]%. Capping the stop relabels that risk; only refusing the trade removes it.\n\nAt 9%, CBRS 2026-08-12 - the validation trade - measures 8.89% and is kept, with 0.11 points to spare.\n\nSet to 0 to switch the veto off.")

// v8.1 - THE STOP CEILING.
stopCapFrac = input.float(0.65, "  Cap the stop at this fraction of the move", minval=0.1, maxval=2.0, step=0.05, group=gTop,
     tooltip="A ceiling on the FINAL risk, as a fraction of the same print-open-to-confirmation-close move the veto above measures.\n\nAAOI 2026-08-06 is the case it exists for: its stop sat essentially where price had been BEFORE the earnings move, so the trade had to hand back the whole thing before the stop could act - and that is also why its re-entry was able to fire at all.\n\nIt bounds the risk AFTER the volatility floor, not before. That ordering matters: v8.1 repointed the floor at recent bars rather than a calm-day average, and recent true range immediately after a print is large, so without a ceiling the floor would hand back an unbounded stop on the next thin re-entry.")

holdTime = input.time(0, "  ...for the trade open at this bar", group=gTop,
     tooltip="WHICH TRADE THE OVERRIDE APPLIES TO.\n\nLEAVE IT AT THE DEFAULT and the switch above is a blanket rule: no trade ever takes a conviction exit.\n\nSET IT TO A BAR and it arms only for the trade that was ALREADY OPEN at that moment, because it requires the chosen time to be at or after that trade entry. A later trade cannot inherit it and an earlier one can never reach it. Anywhere inside the trade works - on the exit bar itself, or a few bars before it.\n\nUse this one when you want to keep a specific trade alive without rewriting how every other trade on the chart behaved.")




// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// EARNINGS PRINT DETECTOR - marks the 1-minute candle where the market starts
// reacting to an earnings release.
//
// ─── PROVENANCE OF THE NUMBERS BELOW - READ THIS ───────────────────────────────
// The detection counts in this header were calibrated on [n] liquid earnings events
// (2026-06-15 .. 2026-08-12), ground truth from the Finnhub earnings calendar, on
// a CONSOLIDATED-TAPE 1-minute feed - NOT on TradingView bars.
//
// TradingView's extended-hours volume is a fraction of the consolidated tape and
// the ratio varies bar to bar (same symbol, same minute: 200 vs 323, 921 vs 1226,
// 15 vs 361). Prices match; VOLUME DOES NOT. This script is volume-gated, so
// those bars are not these bars.
//
// Consequence: the DIRECTION of every finding below still stands - they are
// structural results about bucketed baselines, confirmation and stop placement -
// but no absolute count, win rate or R-expectancy in this file is valid for this
// chart until it is re-measured on TradingView. Where a specific number would
// otherwise read as a supported conclusion, it has been removed rather than
// repeated. Treat what remains as METHOD, not as performance.
//
// Scored for PRECISION, which matters more here than recall: a fire only counts
// if it lands within a few bars of the actual print candle, where the "actual
// print" is defined objectively as the bar maximising volume x range inside the
// earnings window. The first version of this scoring counted a fire as a success
// if it landed anywhere in the four-hour window, which quietly rewarded firing
// early on the wrong candle - the ASTS 2026-08-10 case, where it marked 16:05
// (31k shares) instead of the real 16:30 print (118k shares).
//
// At the shipped defaults, over the [n] test events:
//     on the print candle    [n]
//     early, wrong candle     [n]     (fewer than before the rescore)
//     late                    [n]
//     no signal              [n]
//     fired on a dud event    [n]    (events whose "print" moved <1%)
//
// The misses are deliberate. A weak or ambiguous report produces no tradeable
// move, and a marker on it is worse than silence - it invites a trade that is
// not there. This is tuned to be quiet and right rather than eager.
//
// ─── THE THING THAT MAKES THIS WORK ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A naive relative-volume indicator FAILS on exactly the bar you care about, and
// it fails silently. Measuring a 16:05 bar against the trailing 30 minutes means
// measuring it against 15:35-16:04 - the closing rush and the closing auction,
// the busiest minutes of the entire day. The baseline is inflated precisely when
// the signal arrives. Measured that way, COHR's real print scored 0.8x RVOL and
// ASTS's scored 13x, while meaningless pre-market blips scored 29x.
//
// The fix is to compare like with like. This script keeps FOUR independent
// rolling baselines, each spanning several days:
//
//     pre-market,  first 20 minutes  |  pre-market,  remainder
//     post-market, first 20 minutes  |  post-market, remainder
//
// A 16:05 bar is therefore measured against other 16:00-16:20 bars from previous
// days, never against the auction and never against the dead 18:00 tape. The
// phase split matters because the open of each extended session is structurally
// loud AND is exactly when most after-close reports land - pooling them hides
// the signal under the noise it sits next to.
//
// Same treatment for volatility: ATR is accumulated per bucket, so a bar's range
// is judged against the normal range of that part of that session.
//
// Measured effect of the change: ASTS's print went from 13x to 58x RVOL, and the
// detector went from firing on the wrong bar to firing on the right one.
//
// ─── THE GATES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// 1. RVOL - bar volume / median volume of the matching bucket.      >= 8x
// 2. RANGE - bar range / ATR of the matching bucket.                 >= 5x
// 3. EXCURSION - largest move from the close 3 bars back, in percent.    >= 1.5%
// 4. ABS SIZE - bar volume / a normal regular-session minute.              OFF
//
// Gate 4 scores better on historical data (wrong-candle fires [n] -> [n] across [n]
// events) but is the only gate whose value comes from request.security() rather
// than the chart's own bars, and switching it on is what silently killed the
// arrows for a whole session. It stays OFF until it can be verified live.
//
// Range is 5, not the 8 that scored best. 8 produced fewer wrong-candle fires
// and found no extra real prints - but the owner's standing call is that a spare
// arrow he can ignore beats a missing one.
//
// ─── WHY THE MARKER DOES NOT VANISH ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Every gate is monotonically non-decreasing inside a live bar: volume only
// accumulates, high only rises, low only falls. Excursion is deliberately
// measured to the bar's HIGH and LOW rather than its close for exactly this
// reason. So a marker that appears three seconds into the print candle is still
// there when the candle closes - you can act on it immediately instead of
// waiting up to 60 seconds for confirmation. Set "Signal timing" to bar-close if
// you would rather have the stricter version.
//
// ─── KNOWN LIMITS, MEASURED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// - Requires Extended Hours ON (right-click price scale -> Settings -> Symbol ->
//   Extended trading hours). Without it there are no bars to test.
// - Built for the 1-minute chart. It runs elsewhere; the numbers stop meaning
//   what they were fitted to mean.
// - Needs a few days of extended-hours history in each bucket before it arms.
//   Illiquid names that barely trade after hours may never fill their buckets.
// - The four misses in testing were all quiet reports: FERG and HWM moved on
//   ranges below the gate, PH drifted 8.5% without any single dramatic bar, and
//   MNST's after-hours volume never cleared 4x its own baseline. A drifting
//   reaction with no violent candle is not what this detects.
// - Largest remaining false-positive cluster is 09:20-09:30, the pre-open
//   imbalance surge. "Exclude final minutes before the open" kills it, at the
//   cost of late-morning detections on before-open reports.
// - This detects a REACTION, not the press release. Halts resuming, M&A
//   headlines and index events trip it too. That is usually the same trade.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


// ─── INPUTS: DETECTION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gDet = "Detection thresholds"

// Shown in the status table's top-left cell. It had been left at "v3.2" while the
// indicator() title said v3.4, so the on-chart table and the legend disagreed.
// Keep this in step with the title above.
VERSION = "v10.1"

// The preset selector from v1.4/v1.5 is gone. It added a layer of indirection
// between what the inputs said and what was actually in force, which is the last
// thing this script needed while it was being debugged. The four numbers below
// are the numbers used. Nothing overrides them.

minRvol = input.float(12.0, "Min RVOL vs same session phase", minval=1.0, step=0.5, group=gDet,
     tooltip="Bar volume / median volume of the matching bucket (same session, same phase, previous days).\n\nLooks low compared with a naive RVOL number, and it is — because this denominator is honest. Fitted value: 8. Real prints in testing scored 8x to 335x.")

useRthGate = input.bool(true, "Require real size vs a normal RTH minute", group=gDet,
     tooltip="THE gate that stops pre-release false triggers, and it is built only from this chart's own regular-hours bars — no request.security(), nothing external that can disagree with TradingView's feed.\n\nThe minutes before a release are often loud RELATIVE to a dead after-hours tape while still being tiny in absolute share count. Only the release itself trades real size.\n\nMeasured on 2026-08-12, bar volume / median regular-hours minute:\n   real 16:05 prints ... INFQ 4.2x  CBRS 6.6x  COHR 8.9x  CSCO 30.2x\n   weak later fires ... 2.9x - 4.8x\n\n3.0 keeps every real print and cuts the weak ones.")

minRthMult = input.float(3.0, "  Min volume vs median RTH minute", minval=0.0, step=0.25, group=gDet)

rthSamples = input.int(390, "  RTH volume samples", minval=30, maxval=2000, group=gDet,
     tooltip="How many regular-hours bars form the median. 390 is one full session.")

useAbsGate = input.bool(false, "Also require absolute size (external data)", group=gDet,
     tooltip="OFF by default again, on purpose.\n\nThis gate scores well on historical data, but it is the ONLY gate that depends on request.security() pulling a daily average volume. That is the single most environment-dependent line in the script: it can differ between the feed this was calibrated on and what TradingView serves for the same symbol, especially with extended hours enabled.\n\nArrows were verifiably printing before this gate was switched on, and stopped after. Whether or not it was the cause, a gate that can silently zero out the whole signal has no business being on by default. Turn it on only after you have confirmed arrows appear without it.")

minAdvRatio = input.float(1.5, "  Min volume vs a normal RTH minute", minval=0.0, step=0.05, group=gDet,
     tooltip="Bar volume / (average daily volume / minutes per session).\n\n1.5 means the candle must trade at least 1.5x what this ticker does in an average regular-session minute — after hours, when volume is normally a fraction of that. Almost nothing but a real release clears it.")

minRangeAtr = input.float(5.8, "Min bar range vs session ATR", minval=0.5, step=0.25, group=gDet,
     tooltip="(high - low) / ATR of the matching bucket. Volatility-normalised, so it behaves the same on a $5 stock and a $500 stock.\n\nSet to 5, not the 8 that scored best. 5 is that call.")

minMovePct = input.float(1.5, "Min excursion %", minval=0.0, step=0.05, group=gDet,
     tooltip="Largest move from the close N bars back to this bar's high or low.\n\nFitted value: 1.5. Dropping to 0.75 added [n] detections and multiplied the false positives.")

impulseLen = input.int(3, "Excursion lookback (bars)", minval=1, maxval=20, group=gDet,
     tooltip="Catches prints that arrive over two or three minutes instead of one candle.")

absWaiveMult = input.float(3.0, "  ...unless price action is this many x its own gates", minval=0.0, maxval=20.0, step=0.5, group=gDet,
     tooltip="THE GAP ESCAPE HATCH, added v8.1e after INTU 2026-08-25.\n\nThe gate above compares a candle's VOLUME against a normal regular-hours minute. It exists to stop a thin post-market bar qualifying purely because the post-market baseline is tiny, and it is a good gate. But it can veto the very candle it should be catching.\n\nINTU, the 16:00 release candle:\n     RVOL             1273x   (needed 12)\n     Range / ATR      126.6x  (needed 5.8)\n     Excursion        11.71%  (needed 1.5)\n     vs RTH minute     1.02   (needed 1.5)   <- REJECTED HERE\n\nThe bar opened at 364.00 and closed at 316.00 - a 48-point, 13.19% collapse in sixty seconds - on 9,551 shares against a ~9,350 median. ON A VIOLENT GAP THE VOLUME IS LOW BECAUSE PRICE FALLS THROUGH AN EMPTY BOOK. There is nothing to trade against. A minute later the book refilled, volume reached 1.63x and the print fired there - on a bar less than a fifth the size, which then anchored every downstream calculation to the wrong candle.\n\nSo the volume gate is waived when the PRICE evidence is overwhelming: excursion AND range-vs-ATR both at this multiple of their own requirements. Price measures cannot be faked by a thin tape, which is exactly why they are the right thing to trust here. At 3.0 INTU clears it comfortably (7.8x and 21.8x) while an ordinary qualifying print, which sits nearer 1-2x its gates, does not come close.\n\nSet to 0 to switch the escape hatch off and restore the pre-v8.1e behaviour.")

useBurst = input.bool(false, "Also allow a 3-bar burst trigger", group=gDet,
     tooltip="A second way in, for reports whose reaction arrives as a fast staircase rather than one violent candle. Those never clear the single-bar range gate no matter how it is set.\n\nFires when the last 3 bars together traded >=1.5x normal for this ticker AND covered >=4%. Measured effect: [n] more real detections, [n] more wrong ones, [n] fires on dud events.")

minBurstVol = input.float(1.5, "  Burst: 3-bar volume vs normal", minval=0.1, step=0.1, group=gDet)
minBurstMove = input.float(4.0, "  Burst: 3-bar move %", minval=0.5, step=0.25, group=gDet)


// ─── INPUTS: BASELINES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gBase = "Baselines"

phaseMin = input.int(20, "Session phase split (minutes)", minval=5, maxval=120, group=gBase,
     tooltip="Bars in the first N minutes of an extended session are baselined only against other first-N-minute bars from previous days. This is the single most important setting in the script — see the header.")

baseLen = input.int(40, "Volume baseline size (bars per bucket)", minval=10, maxval=300, group=gBase,
     tooltip="How many prior same-bucket bars form the rolling median. 40 spans several days of extended-hours activity.")

atrLen = input.int(40, "ATR size (bars per bucket)", minval=10, maxval=300, group=gBase)

minSamples = input.int(12, "Min samples before arming", minval=5, maxval=200, group=gBase,
     tooltip="A bucket stays disarmed until it holds this many bars, so the script cannot fire off a two-sample baseline on its first day.")

advDays = input.int(5, "Daily volume average (days)", minval=1, maxval=60, group=gBase)
rthMinutes = input.int(390, "Minutes in a regular session", minval=1, group=gBase,
     tooltip="390 for US equities. Only feeds the absolute-size gate and the diagnostics panel.")


// ─── INPUTS: TIMING ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gTime = "Timing"

sessMode = input.string("Extended hours only", "Active session",
     options=["Extended hours only", "Post-market only", "Pre-market only"], group=gTime,
     tooltip="Regular-hours detection is deliberately not offered: the baselines here are built from extended-hours buckets and mean nothing during the session.")

guardBars = input.int(1, "Session guard (bars)", minval=0, maxval=30, group=gTime,
     tooltip="Ignore this many bars at the very start of each extended session.\n\nWAS 2, AND THAT WAS THE 'PRINT IS ONE CANDLE LATE' BUG. The counter starts at 0 on the session's first bar, so a guard of 2 meant the first bar allowed to fire was the THIRD bar of the session:\n\n   16:00   bucketBars 0   blocked\n   16:01   bucketBars 1   blocked   <- where AMAT and PLUG actually printed\n   16:02   bucketBars 2   allowed   <- where the arrow appeared instead\n\nAMAT 2026-08-13 and PLUG 2026-08-10 both released at 16:01 and both got marked at 16:02 for this reason alone. The old tooltip even said 'many reports land at 16:01-16:03' while the default was quietly discarding 16:01.\n\n1 skips only the session's very first bar — on a US equity that is the thin 16:00 print, which on AMAT traded 1,647 shares against 12,103 on 16:01. Set to 0 to allow even that bar; the volume and range gates still have to pass either way.\n\nSPCE, CRWV and NBIS are unaffected: their prints land well after the session open.")

preOpenGuard = input.int(0, "Exclude final minutes before the open", minval=0, maxval=60, group=gTime,
     tooltip="09:20-09:30 was the largest single source of false positives in testing (the pre-open imbalance surge). Setting this to 10 removes that cluster, but also removes genuine late detections on before-open reports — one of the hits in testing fired at 09:29.")

cooldownBars = input.int(3, "Cooldown after a signal (bars)", minval=0, maxval=390, group=gTime,
     tooltip="Minimum gap between markers. Its only job is to stop the two or three continuation candles right after a print from each printing their own arrow.\n\nKept short deliberately. This is an indicator, not a trade manager — it should keep flagging whatever qualifies rather than rationing signals.")

suppressWhileOpen = input.bool(true, "  ...and never re-fire the same way mid-trade", group=gTime,
     tooltip="Blocks a second print in the SAME direction while a confirmed trade is still open.\n\nThe SPCE 2026-08-14 case: the second signal genuinely beat the first on both volume and move, so the escalation gate let it through — correctly, by its own rule. But you were already short. All it did was stack a duplicate stop and target set on the chart.\n\nA FLIP still prints while a trade is open. That is the reversal warning, and it is the one re-fire worth having.")

useEscalate = input.bool(true, "Re-fire only on a BIGGER move (either direction)", group=gTime,
     tooltip="Stops the same move printing three arrows while the tape is still loud, without ever capping the session.\n\nAfter a signal, the next one only prints if it BEATS the previous signal on volume OR on excursion. Direction is irrelevant - an opposite-direction print has to earn its place exactly like a same-direction one.\n\nv3.11 CHANGED THIS. The old rule exempted flips from the size test entirely, and that is the AMD 2026-08-04 defect: the real print at 16:15 had 95,183 volume and a 7.79% excursion, and a flip at 16:22 with 42,697 volume and a 3.30% excursion fired a long print AND a long confirm in the middle of a live short. Nothing about that bar was more significant than the event it contradicted.\n\nOR rather than AND, deliberately: on ASTS 2026-08-10 the genuine reversal at 16:30 had HIGHER volume (16,970 vs 16,625) but a SMALLER excursion (3.01% vs 4.31%). An AND would have thrown that warning away.\n\nKNOWN COST: this also blocks the CSCO 2026-08-12 short, whose flip at 16:08 was smaller than the 16:05 print on both measures. Measured, not hidden - there is no clean discriminator between that flip and AMD's.")

staleBars = input.int(8, "Forget an unconfirmed print after N bars", minval=0, maxval=200, group=gTime,
     tooltip="How many bars an UNCONFIRMED print may stay the session anchor before it is forgotten.\n\nTHE ASTS 2026-08-10 PROBLEM. A big pre-earnings candle at 16:05 cleared every gate on merit - real volume, real range - and became the print. It never confirmed. But it stayed the anchor, so when the actual release arrived at 16:29 the script judged it against that stale blip instead of on its own merits, and the real earnings move came out classified as a REVERSAL against a false print. Everything downstream inherited the wrong reference.\n\nMEASURED IN AGE, NOT IN CONSECUTIVE QUIET BARS. A quiet-run counter was tried first and does not work: on ASTS the longest unbroken quiet run between the blip and the release is 7 bars, because three middling bars keep restarting it. Age has no such hole.\n\nWHY 8 IS SAFE. The confirm window is only 6 bars, so an unconfirmed print is already dead for confirmation purposes at 6. Forgetting it at 8 discards nothing that could still have become a trade.\n\nA CONFIRMED trade is NEVER forgotten - the reset requires no open position AND no confirm on that print. Real moves that enter a trade stay active regardless of what price does afterwards, exactly as asked.\n\nSet to 0 to disable.")

staleQuietBars = input.int(5, "  ...and only if the last N bars went back to normal", minval=0, maxval=50, group=gTime,
     tooltip="MEASURED AGAINST THE SCRIPT'S OWN DEFINITION OF AN EARNINGS MOVE, not against an absolute 'quiet' number. The median RVOL and the median range/ATR of the last N bars must both be BELOW the print gates (12 and 6). That is exactly 'not itself earnings-grade'.\n\nWHY NOT AN ABSOLUTE QUIET TEST. On ASTS 2026-08-10 the five bars before the reset run RVOL 22, 5, 0.8, 27, 8 - median 8.1. The tape was still BUSY; price simply went nowhere. Any literal 'quiet' threshold (RVOL < 2) blocks the reset on the one session it exists to fix. Median 8.1 < 12 passes, and median range/ATR 1.4 < 6 passes comfortably.\n\nSet 0 to reset on age alone.")
thinFloor = input.float(0.02, "Skip the RTH-size gate on a dead tape (bucket/RTH)", minval=0.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.005, group=gDet,
     tooltip="THE FIX FOR NAMES WHERE NOTHING WAS DRAWN (LLY, NBIS).\n\nThe RTH gate asks for 3x a normal regular-hours MINUTE - a SHARE-COUNT test. Extended-hours tape is structurally thinner than regular hours, and on a high-priced, low-share-count name it is thinner by so much that no real move can ever clear the bar.\n\nMeasured, LLY 2026-08-05 06:45 - the earnings candle, a 56-point range on a 5.4% move:\n     RVOL           345   (needs 12)   PASS\n     range / ATR     22   (needs 6)    PASS\n     excursion     5.44%  (needs 1.5)  PASS\n     vs RTH median  2.76x (needs 3)    BLOCKED\nZero of LLY's 7,675 loaded bars pass, and the ONLY two blocked by this gate alone are both on the earnings morning.\n\nTHIS IS NOT FIXED BY LOWERING minRthMult. INFQ 2026-08-12 16:05 reads 2.84x on a bar that should NOT print - higher than LLY's 2.76x. No threshold on that ratio separates them.\n\nWhat DOES separate them is how thin the bucket is relative to regular hours:\n     LLY  pre-market median      5 shares/min vs RTH 625    = 0.008\n     INFQ post-market median   115 shares/min vs RTH 2082   = 0.055\n     ASTS post-market                                       = 0.065\nA 7x gap. Below this floor the gate is not a filter, it is an impossibility, so it is skipped. Above it, nothing changes - minRthMult stays 3.0 and every symbol that works today is untouched.\n\nSet 0 to always apply the RTH gate (v4.5 behaviour).")
usePoolFallback = input.bool(true, "Pooled baseline when a bucket is cold", group=gBase,
     tooltip="THE SECOND HALF OF THE MISSING-MARKER DEFECT, and a different cause from the one above.\n\nEach bucket needs minSamples bars of ITS OWN phase before any gate can be evaluated. The post-market-first-20-minutes bucket only gains 20 samples per day, so an event in the first 20 minutes of the FIRST loaded session has no baseline at all.\n\nMeasured, PLTR 2026-08-03 16:05-16:07 - excursion 6.3%, 7.6%, 8.5%:\n     bucket sample count at those bars:  5, 6, 7   (needs 12)\n     RVOL and range/ATR:                 unavailable\nThe script is not rejecting the move, it is structurally unable to measure it - and TradingView will not serve 1-minute history any further back, so waiting does not help.\n\nWith this on, a cold bucket falls back to the POOLED extended-hours samples (all four buckets together) rather than going silent. Less precise than the phase-matched baseline, and vastly better than nothing: PLTR has 330+ pooled samples from that morning's pre-market by 16:05.\n\nThe phase-matched bucket is ALWAYS preferred when it is available; this only ever runs where the alternative is no signal at all. Watch 'DIAG: pooled baseline' in the Data Window to see when it is in use.")
oneShot = input.bool(false, "Only one signal per session block", group=gTime,
     tooltip="OFF by default, and it should almost certainly stay off.\n\nA once-per-session cap sounds tidy and behaves badly: the FIRST qualifying bar is not always the real print, so one early fire silently disables the rest of the session. That exact failure hid COHR's genuine 16:05 print on 2026-08-12 behind an 08:35 pre-market blip.\n\nWith it off you may see the occasional extra marker. That is the correct trade — a spurious arrow you can ignore costs nothing, a suppressed real one costs the trade.")

liveMode = input.bool(true, "Signal timing: live (intrabar)", group=gTime,
     tooltip="ON  — fires the moment the thresholds are met, seconds into the print candle. Every gate is monotonic within a bar, so the marker cannot disappear once shown.\n\nOFF — waits for the 1m bar to close.")


// ─── INPUTS: DISPLAY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gDisp = "Display"

colUp = input.color(color.new(#00c853, 0), "Up print", inline="c", group=gDisp)
colDn = input.color(color.new(#ff1744, 0), "Down print", inline="c", group=gDisp)

// ── REVERSAL marker (v3.12) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
showReversal = input.bool(true, "Mark reversals against the earnings move (R)", group=gDisp,
     tooltip="A SECOND CLASS OF SIGNAL, and a different claim from the ▲/▼ print.\n\n▲/▼ says: the earnings release just hit. R says: the release is already out and something is now driving price the OTHER way — repricing, follow-up news, the move being exhausted and unwound.\n\nAn R only appears when all three are true:\n  1. an earnings print is already established this session\n  2. this bar is in the OPPOSITE direction to that print\n  3. NO trade is currently open\n\nIt clears exactly the same detection gates as a print — RVOL, range vs the bucket ATR, excursion — so an ordinary pullback or a consolidation bar can never produce one. The range >= 5x bucket ATR requirement IS the conviction test; it is what separates 'price drifted back' from 'something changed'.\n\nDownstream it behaves identically to a print: it can earn a ✓ confirm, and that opens a normal trade with a stop, T1/T2/T3 and + adds. The only reason it looks different is so you can tell at a glance which claim is being made.\n\nWHY IT IS EXEMPT FROM THE ESCALATION SIZE TEST. Measured on CSCO 2026-08-12: the print was 16:05 at 104,787 volume and 7.74% excursion; the reversal that set up the short was 16:08 at 30,384 and 5.13% — smaller on BOTH. A fade is not a bigger version of the earnings move, so any 'must supersede the print' rule kills every one of them by construction. That is what removed this trade in v3.11.\n\nReversals do escalate against EACH OTHER, so a fade cannot spam the chart.")

revMaxCount = input.int(1, "  Maximum R markers per print", minval=1, maxval=5, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Upper bound on reversals per earnings print, so a choppy session cannot fill the chart with them. Each one after the first must also beat the previous R on both volume and excursion.")

revImpGive = input.float(0.42, "  R: min candle body (x print candle range)", minval=0.1, maxval=3.0, step=0.05, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="THE MAGNITUDE TEST FOR A REVERSAL. It measures THIS CANDLE'S OWN BODY against the print candle's range, and it replaces excursion entirely on this path.\n\nWHY EXCURSION IS WRONG HERE: a move can go a long way up and then all the way back down. That is a huge reversal, but price ends very close to where it started. Excursion measures distance from a reference point a few bars back, so a full round trip scores near zero on it. Correct for a release, useless for a fade.\n\nWHY CUMULATIVE RETRACEMENT IS ALSO WRONG, which is what v4.0 and v4.1 used. Measuring how far price had come back from the print extreme fails in BOTH directions at once:\n\n  * once price drifts past the threshold, every later bar against the trade satisfies it, including tiny ones. SPCX 2026-08-04 fired on the smallest red candle in the middle of a bounce.\n\n  * the FIRST big red candles can FAIL it, because price has not retraced far enough from the print extreme yet. On SPCX the move ran up well past that extreme, so the major red candles that actually began the reversal were rejected.\n\nIt skipped every worthy candle and then picked the worst one.\n\nMeasured on TradingView bars:\n   ASTS 16:30   body 4.32 / print range 1.62 = 2.67x   fires\n   FN   16:16   body 23.77 / 50.89           = 0.47x   fires\n   SMCI 16:28   body 0.40 / 2.73             = 0.15x   blocked\n\n0.35 keeps both genuine reversals and drops the SMCI chop. A small candle can no longer qualify however far price has already fallen, and a large one no longer waits for a retracement threshold to be reached.")

// ── RESTORED 2026-08-18: THIS DECLARATION WAS MISSING AND THE SCRIPT DID NOT ────
// COMPILE. `revGatesOk` further down still reads `revExemptRth`, and the comment
// block above that line still argues for it. Only the input itself had been
// dropped, so the reference was left dangling:
//     Undeclared identifier revExemptRth, line 889.
// Restored verbatim from EPD-v4.1.pine. No reversal RULE was changed here.
revExemptRth = input.bool(true, "  R: skip the RTH-median volume gate", group=gDisp,
     tooltip="RTH means REGULAR TRADING HOURS - the normal 09:30-16:00 session.\n\nThe gate it refers to asks: is this bar's volume at least 3x the MEDIAN MINUTE of a normal trading day? It is an absolute-size sanity check, there so a thin after-hours bar with a flattering RVOL cannot masquerade as an earnings release. RVOL alone can be fooled: if a stock trades almost nothing after hours, the baseline is tiny and any activity looks enormous. That is precisely why CRWV once showed RVOL 493.8x on a spurious mark.\n\nBut it is the wrong test for a REVERSAL. A fade is a REACTION to the release, and it trades less than the release by nature. Demanding 3x a normal regular-session minute is a \"this is a major event\" test - correct for the release, too strict for the response to it.\n\nConcretely: FN 16:16 traded 899 shares against an RTH median near 2,570. That gate alone would veto the correct candle, no matter what else was true about it.\n\nPrints still have to pass it. Only reversals skip it.")

revCooldown = input.int(1, "  R: minimum bars after the print", minval=0, maxval=30, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Reversals use THEIR OWN cooldown, separate from the general one, and this was the other half of the late-R problem.\n\n`cooldownBars` in the Timing group is 3, so after any signal nothing at all could fire for three bars. That is right for prints — it stops one event being marked repeatedly. It is exactly backwards for a reversal, because a reversal happens IMMEDIATELY after the print by definition. On ASTS 2026-08-10 the print is 16:29 and the reversal candle is 16:30, one bar later, so the general cooldown alone guaranteed the R could never land on the right candle no matter how the direction test behaved.\n\n1 lets the very next bar be a reversal, which is the whole shape you have been pointing at. Raise it if reversals start appearing too eagerly.")

revPairMin = input.float(0.30, "  R: two-candle path — each candle at least (x print range)", minval=0.0, maxval=3.0, step=0.05, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="PATH 2 of 3. The floor each of the two adjacent candles must clear on its own before they are allowed to combine.\n\nThis is what stops the pair rule degenerating into 'any two red candles'. Both bars have to be genuinely part of the reversal, not one real candle plus a shrug.\n\nSet revPairSum to 0 to switch the two-candle path off entirely.")
revPairSum = input.float(0.60, "  R: two-candle path — combined at least (x print range)", minval=0.0, maxval=5.0, step=0.05, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="PATH 2 of 3. What the two adjacent candles must add up to.\n\nWHY THIS EXISTS. A reversal does not respect the one-minute grid. If it begins halfway through one candle and finishes halfway through the next, the move is split into two bodies and NEITHER clears a single-candle test - so nothing fires, purely because of where the minute boundary happened to fall. The identical move starting two seconds later, at the top of a minute, fires normally. That is an artefact, not a signal.\n\n0.60 against a per-candle floor of 0.30 means two roughly equal candles that together give back a bit more than half the print candle qualify, while one big candle plus one token candle does not.\n\nSet to 0 to disable this path.")
revPairClose = input.float(0.40, "  R: ...and it must close within X of its adverse end", minval=0.05, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Applies to PATH 2 and PATH 3. Where the candle closed inside its own range, on the reversal side. 0.40 means the close must be in the bottom 40% of the bar when fading an up-print, or the top 40% when fading a down-print.\n\nTHIS IS THE REVERSAL-VERSUS-CONSOLIDATION TEST. A bar that sells off hard and closes on its low is a reversal. A bar with the same range that closes mid-bar is a fight, and a fight is consolidation. Range alone cannot tell them apart; close position can.\n\nDeliberately NOT applied to PATH 1, which already requires the body alone to clear the print candle and has been working.")
revGiveBackMin = input.float(0.40, "  R: ...and must give back this much of the whole move", minval=0.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="THE VETO ON A REVERSAL THAT HAS NOT ACTUALLY REVERSED ANYTHING, added v8.7.\n\nThe three magnitude paths all ask how big THIS CANDLE is - against the move, against its neighbour, or against a normal minute. None of them asks the obvious question: has price actually come back? A fade that gives back 15% of the move is a pause, whatever the candle looks like.\n\nThis measures the close against the whole move - the open of the bar that armed the direction through to the furthest close reached since. It is NOT the size of the reversal candle: it is how far THIS CANDLE CLOSED back from that furthest point. 0.40 means price must have surrendered at least 40% of the move, i.e. it must close beyond the 60% mark on the way home.\n\nRaised from 0.30 to 0.40 on 2026-08-27. 0.40 is the conservative end of the range he gave.\n\nIt applies to ALL THREE paths, so it cannot be routed around by the absolute single-candle path, which has no relationship to the move at all.\n\nSet 0 to switch the veto off.")
revSelfMult = input.float(30.0, "  R: single-candle absolute path — body at least (x normal RTH minute)", minval=0.0, maxval=200.0, step=0.5, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="PATH 3 of 3, and the only path that is not measured against the print candle.\n\nTHE PROBLEM IT SOLVES. Every other measure here is RELATIVE - to the print candle, to a rolling median, to a bucket ATR that the release itself has already inflated. All of them can take a genuinely enormous candle and report a small number.\n\nTHE YARDSTICK IS THE STOCK'S OWN NORMAL MINUTE - the median REGULAR-HOURS one-minute RANGE, built the same way as the RTH volume median. A fixed percentage cannot work: 4% is nothing for a $2 speculative name and unreachable for a bank stock, so it would need retuning per symbol forever. A multiple of the symbol's own normal minute needs no retuning at all.\n\nREGULAR HOURS ON PURPOSE. The session-phase ATR is contaminated by the release - it is why a genuine reversal candle scores 1.4x minutes after an identical candle scored 8.5x. RTH minutes are measured before any of that.\n\nThis path can only ADD reversals, never remove one, because the three paths are OR-ed. It still has to clear the close-position test and the reversal RVOL / range / excursion gates.\n\nSet to 0 to switch it off.")
revMinRvol = input.float(5.0, "  R: min RVOL (own gate)", minval=0.0, step=0.5, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="THE REVERSAL'S OWN VOLUME GATE, separate from the print's 12.\n\nWHY IT HAS TO BE LOWER, measured on TradingView bars. A fade is usually a GRIND, not one violent candle, and by the time it develops the bucket baseline has already swallowed the earnings bars that just printed - so the same candle scores far lower than it would have minutes earlier.\n\n  SPCX 2026-08-04, the collapse:\n     16:15  body 0.75x the print candle,  RVOL 9,  range/ATR 2.5\n     16:16  body 0.41x,                   RVOL 8,  range/ATR 1.4\n     16:17  body 0.35x,                   RVOL 8,  range/ATR 1.6\n\nEvery one of them clears the body test and every one of them failed RVOL 12 and range/ATR 6. Lowering the PRINT gates to catch these would wreck the print, which is why the reversal now has its own pair.\n\nThe body-vs-print-range test (revImpGive) is what actually discriminates here. These two are sanity floors under it, not the primary filter.")
revMinRangeAtr = input.float(1.5, "  R: min range vs session ATR (own gate)", minval=0.0, step=0.25, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Companion to the RVOL gate above, and the more important of the two.\n\nThe session ATR is a ROLLING 40-bar average of the same bucket. The earnings candles push themselves into it within a few minutes, so range/ATR decays fast even while the candles stay large in absolute terms - SPCX reads 8.5 at 16:03 and 1.4 at 16:16 on candles of comparable size. Holding the reversal to the print's 6 therefore measures it against a yardstick the event itself corrupted.\n\n1.5 keeps it a real floor (a dead 0.3x bar still cannot qualify) without asking a fade to out-range the release.")
revExcWaive = input.float(1.5, "  R: ...or waive it when body clears this x the magnitude test", minval=0.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.1, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="THE ESCAPE HATCH ON THE EXCURSION FLOOR, added v8.5.\n\nThe floor exists to stop small chop qualifying once RVOL and range/ATR are relaxed for the reversal path - measured on ASTS 2026-08-10, where two noise bars at 1.02% and 0.67% would otherwise have fired. That job is real and the floor keeps doing it.\n\nBut a candle that has ALREADY cleared the magnitude test by a wide margin has proved it is a fade, and at that point the floor is only standing in the way. 1.5 means the body must be one and a half times the required fraction of the original move - at the shipped 0.42 that is 0.63.\n\nNBIS 2026-08-26 20:23 reads 0.78 and fires. The smaller 20:24 candle reads 0.45, is not waived, and no longer steals the marker.\n\nSet 0 to switch the waiver off and always demand the floor.")
revMinExc = input.float(1.5, "  R: min excursion %", minval=0.0, step=0.05, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="THE TERM THAT KEEPS THE LOOSER GATES ABOVE HONEST, and it is deliberately the SAME 1.5% a print must clear.\n\nWithout it, dropping RVOL to 5 and range/ATR to 1.5 lets small chop qualify. Measured on ASTS 2026-08-10 after the false 16:05 blip:\n     16:09   excursion 1.02%   would have fired\n     16:14   excursion 0.67%   would have fired\nBoth are noise, and both would have re-anchored the print and blocked the stale reset that fixes that session.\n\nAgainst the reversals that SHOULD fire:\n     SPCX 16:08   excursion 3.5%+\n     INFQ 16:10   excursion 2.2%\nExcursion separates them cleanly where RVOL and range/ATR do not.\n\nNOTE this does not contradict the revImpGive reasoning. That argument is about CUMULATIVE round trips scoring near zero. Excursion here is measured over only 3 bars, so it reads the local move, and a genuine fade bar has one.")
revWindowBars = input.int(60, "  R: only within N bars of the print", minval=0, maxval=200, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="A reversal may only fire within this many bars of the print it is reversing. Past that, the release is no longer what is moving the tape and the bar falls through to the normal first-signal path, where it can print as its own event.\n\n20 RATHER THAN 10, and the number is not arbitrary - it is the same phaseMin boundary the session buckets already use. Measured:\n     SPCX  print 16:02, the real fade develops 13-15 bars later   -> 10 would MISS it\n     ASTS  print 16:05, the spurious R sits 23 bars later         -> 20 EXCLUDES it\n20 is the only value in that range that keeps SPCX and drops ASTS.\n\nSet 0 to disable the window entirely (v4.2-v4.5 behaviour).")

revMinRetrace = input.float(0.0, "  R: min travel past the print extreme (x print range)", minval=0.0, maxval=5.0, step=0.25, group=gDisp,
     tooltip="0 = OFF, which is the shipped default.\n\nWhen set, an R also has to have travelled this far beyond the PRINT'S extreme, measured in print-candle ranges. It asks whether the earnings move has actually been UNDONE rather than merely paused — the cleanest separator found between a real reversal and heavy consolidation.\n\nMeasured on TradingView bars:\n\n   ASTS 08-10 16:30   print hi 72.18, bar lo 67.34    2.99x\n   FN   08-17 16:17   print hi 650.89, bar lo 600.00  1.06x\n   SMCI 08-11 16:28   print hi 34.56, bar lo 33.45    0.41x   <- the chop\n\n0.75 keeps both real reversals and drops the SMCI trade, which was heavy consolidation rather than a reversal.\n\nWHY IT IS OFF BY DEFAULT: CSCO 2026-08-12 is a working trade, and its one-minute history would not load on any tab, so this could not be checked against it. A rough read put CSCO near 0.65, which 0.75 would block. Shipping it on would risk silently deleting a trade that works.")

colRev = input.color(color.new(#2979ff, 0), "  Reversal marker", group=gDisp,
     tooltip="BLUE on purpose, so the reversal badge cannot be mistaken for anything else on the chart. Red and green already mean direction on the print markers, and amber already means confirm and add. Blue is the one colour left that says 'a different kind of event happened here' at a glance.\n\nThe ARROW inside the badge still carries direction: red when the reversal turns the move bearish, green when it turns it bullish.")

// ── v4.5: A THICK, TRIANGLE-HEADED U-TURN THAT FINISHES ON THE RIGHT ──────
// Two cosmetic defects, both real:
//
// 1. THE ARROW POINTED BACKWARDS. The old glyphs were ⤺ / ⤻ - hairline arcs
//    whose head finishes on the LEFT. Candles run left to right, so a marker
//    whose head points back into history reads as "it came from there" instead
//    of "it is turning that way".
//
// 2. IT WAS TOO THIN TO SEE THE COLOUR. The badge is blue and the direction
//    lives ENTIRELY in the arrow's colour, and a one-pixel arc is not a colour.
//    Scaling the whole badge was the wrong lever - it just made the marker
//    disagree in size with the ▲ ▼ ✓ ✕ markers around it. The badge stays
//    size.normal; the GLYPH itself is now a heavy triangle-headed one.
//
// The pair shipped:
//     ⮎  U-turn opening to the right, head finishing RIGHT       (bearish)
//     ⮍  U-turn opening upward,       head finishing UP-RIGHT    (bullish)
// Both are U+2B8C..2B8F "triangle-headed u-shaped arrow" - full 180-degree
// about-faces with a filled arrowhead, several times the ink of the old arcs.
//
// Head positions were measured, not guessed: each candidate was rasterised in
// this chart's own font and the densest 6x6 window located, which is the solid
// arrowhead. Every glyph in the anticlockwise u-shaped set whose head lands on
// the right is listed below; the ones not listed finish on the left.
//
// FREE TEXT, not a dropdown. Font coverage is the one thing that can silently
// break a marker, so paste whatever you want straight into the box.
//
// Right-finishing and heavy: ⮎ ⮍ ↪ ⮡ ⮣ ⮥ ⮧ ⭯
// Right-finishing but thin:  ↷ ⤻ ⟳ ⬎ ⬏
revGlyphDn = input.string("↷", "  Reversal glyph — turning DOWN", group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Drawn when a reversal turns the move BEARISH: price ran up on the release and is now handing it back. Rendered in the DOWN colour.\n\nSHAPE: an UPSIDE-DOWN U. The tail starts low on the left, rises, arcs over the top, and the filled arrowhead comes back DOWN on the RIGHT. That is the path price actually took.\n\nGLYPH IS NOW U+2B8F, NOT U+2B8D. Unicode NAMES U+2B8D 'downwards arrow with long tip upwards', but TradingView's font renders it head-UP, so on the chart it read upside down. The names describe the intended semantics; the font decides what you actually see. Chosen by LOOKING at it, which is the only test that counts.\n\nWHAT THIS REPLACED, and why it was wrong. v4.5-v4.7 shipped U+2B8E, a LEFTWARDS arrow with its long tip rightwards - a u-turn lying on its SIDE. It read as a horizontal squiggle rather than a reversal. This is U+2B8D, the same family but the VERTICAL member, so the turn happens in the axis price moves in.\n\nFree text box: paste any character and it renders on the next tick. Other vertical candidates if the weight is not right in your font: ↷ ⤷ ⤵ ⬎. If a glyph shows as an empty box, the font does not have it.")

revGlyphUp = input.string("⮍", "  Reversal glyph — turning UP", group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Drawn when a reversal turns the move BULLISH: price dumped on the release and is now being bought back. Rendered in the UP colour.\n\nSHAPE: a U, the exact mirror of the DOWN glyph. The tail starts high on the left, falls, arcs under the bottom, and the filled arrowhead comes back UP on the RIGHT.\n\nThis is U+2B8D. The pair was swapped in v5.0.1 after a check on a live chart: TradingView renders U+2B8D head-UP and U+2B8F head-DOWN, which is the opposite of what the Unicode names imply.\n\nFree text, same as above. Other vertical candidates: ↶ ⤴ ⤶ ⬏. If the two ever look the wrong way round in your font, just swap what is in the two boxes.")

// Kept as an input only. The badge itself stays size.normal so it matches the
// print, confirm and stop markers - an oversized blue badge was the wrong fix
// for a thin arrow. Raise this only if you want the reversal to shout.
revGlyphSize = input.string("Normal", "  Reversal badge size", options=["Normal", "Large", "Huge"], group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Leave on Normal unless you want the reversal badge deliberately larger than every other marker on the chart. The v4.5 fix for not being able to tell the arrow colour was a thicker GLYPH, not a bigger badge.")
revSize = revGlyphSize == "Huge" ? size.huge : revGlyphSize == "Large" ? size.large : size.normal

showTable = input.bool(true, "Show live diagnostics panel", group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Live values for every gate on the current bar, so you can watch pressure build rather than only finding out afterwards.")
tblPos = input.string("Top right", "Panel position",
     options=["Top right", "Top left", "Bottom right", "Bottom left", "Middle right"], group=gDisp)

// ── v4.2: ONE KNOB SCALES THE WHOLE PANEL ──────────────────────────────────────
// Every cell in the panel was hard-coded to size.tiny. That is fine for a
// screenshot and unreadable on a chart you are actually watching, which is the
// only time the panel matters. This scales the body text, the emphasis text one
// notch above it, the frame weight and the column widths together, so the panel
// grows as one object instead of the text outgrowing the box.
tblSize = input.string("Large", "Panel size",
     options=["Small", "Medium", "Large", "Huge"], group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Every step here is one notch smaller than v4.2 shipped with.\n\nLarge is the new default: body text at normal size, the stage banner one notch above it, and fixed column widths so the numbers stay in the same place instead of the panel twitching every time a value gains a digit.\n\nHuge is for a shared screen or a second monitor.")
showLevels = input.bool(false, "Draw pullback levels after a signal", group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Draws the 50% and 61.8% retracement of the impulse, for the first-pullback continuation entry.")
levelLen = input.int(45, "Pullback level length (bars)", minval=5, maxval=400, group=gDisp)

debugSess = input.bool(false, "DEBUG: show what the script can see", group=gDisp,
     tooltip="Turn this ON if you are getting no arrows. It answers, in one look, which of the three possible failures is happening:\n\nBLUE dots along the bottom  = the script sees these bars as extended hours.\nGREEN dots along the top    = the baseline for that bucket is full and the script is armed.\n\nNo blue dots  -> extended hours is off on the chart, or the session filter is wrong. Nothing can ever fire.\nBlue but no green -> the baselines have not filled; not enough extended-hours history is loaded.\nBoth present but still no arrow -> detection is genuinely rejecting those bars, and the Data Window numbers will say which gate.")


// ─── INPUTS: CONTINUATION CONFIRM ──────────────────────────────────────────────
gConf = "Continuation confirm"

showConfirm = input.bool(true, "Mark continuation confirmation", group=gConf,
     tooltip="A SECOND marker, printed only when the move earns it. This is the entry signal.\n\nWHY IT EXISTS: buying the print candle itself means paying for a move that has not proven anything yet. Requiring the next bar to make a NEW EXTREME while holding most of the impulse makes the move demonstrate continuation first, and it roughly halves the stop distance as a geometric side effect.\n\nNo statistics are quoted. The calibration behind this rule was run on a consolidated-tape feed, not on TradingView bars, so its numbers are void here and repeating them would be quoting a feed you do not trade.")

confirmWindow = input.int(6, "Confirm window (bars after print)", minval=1, maxval=30, group=gConf,
     tooltip="How long the print stays eligible for confirmation. After this it is stale.")

minConfClose = input.float(0.60, "Confirm bar must close in top X of its range", minval=0.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="Where the confirming bar CLOSES inside its own range, measured in the trade's direction. 0.60 means a long confirm must close in the top 40% of that candle.\n\nWHAT IT IS FOR — the INFQ case: the confirm fired long at the very top of a spike that immediately collapsed. A bar that pokes to a new extreme and then closes back down inside its own range is a rejection, not a continuation.\n\nNo win rates are quoted. The ones that set this default came from a consolidated-tape feed rather than TradingView bars, so they are void here. The RULE is a structural one and stands on its own; the number has not been re-swept on TradingView. Set to 0 to disable.")

maxGiveback = input.float(50.0, "Max giveback of impulse (%)", minval=5.0, maxval=95.0, step=5.0, group=gConf,
     tooltip="The confirming bar must close having retained at least this much of the original impulse.\n\nNote a trap this encodes: a shallow pullback ALONE tested NEGATIVE ([n]R). It only helps combined with a new extreme. A pullback by itself tells you nothing about whether the move continues.")

colConf = input.color(color.new(#ffc400, 0), "Confirm marker", group=gConf)

// ─── TRAILING STOP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gTrail = "Trailing stop"

riskAtrMult = input.float(1.80, "Risk floor: at least this many bucket ATRs", minval=0.0, maxval=20.0, step=0.25, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="R IS THE GREATER OF TWO MEASURES: the structural stop (a fraction of the way back toward the print candle's far side) and this multiple of the live session-phase ATR.\n\nWHY. The structural stop is derived entirely from the PRINT candle. On a normal release that is the larger number and nothing changes. On a RE-ENTRY the trigger candle is far smaller than the original release, so structure alone produces a stop with no room - and because T1/T2/T3 are multiples of R, the targets collapse with it.\n\nTaking the MAXIMUM means this floor only binds where structure is unrepresentative. Set to 0 to disable and restore pure structural sizing.")
atrTightMult = input.float(5.0, "  ...to this many ATRs from price", minval=0.5, maxval=20.0, step=0.25, group=gTrail)
atrTightAfter = input.int(45, "  ...starting this many bars after entry", minval=0, maxval=400, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="A fresh trade needs the room the release justified.")
atrTrailLen = input.int(10, "  ...current volatility = the last N bars", minval=2, maxval=400, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="THIS IS THE FIX FOR THE ATR BEING AN AVERAGE OF TWO DIFFERENT WORLDS.\n\nThe baseline ATR the print GATES use is bucketed by session phase, and its rolling window straddles the release - quiet pre-event minutes and violent post-event minutes land in the same array. That is the correct yardstick for asking is this bar unusual, because the whole point there is to compare against normal. It is the WRONG yardstick for a trailing stop, which has to answer a completely different question: how volatile is it RIGHT NOW.\n\nSo the trail uses its own measure - a plain rolling average of true range over the last N bars, with no session buckets at all. Whatever regime the trade is currently in IS the last N bars, by definition. Straight after the release it is large. Overnight it decays. At the regular-hours open it expands again. That is the signal this trail is built to follow.\n\nv7.3 - THIS IS NOW 10, AND IT IS THE MAIN DRAMA CONTROL. This setting is the strongest lever there is, because the whole mechanism is driven by how much this number MOVES. A 30-bar average is deliberately placid - it takes half an hour to notice anything - so the stop crept rather than responded. A 10-bar average swings far harder in BOTH directions: it collapses faster as the overnight tape dies, so the stop pulls in much more decisively, and it spikes faster at the regular-hours open, so the stop backs off and gives the move room much sooner.\n\nRaise it back toward 30 for the older, calmer behaviour. Below about 5 it starts twitching on individual bars.")
atrTrailStep = input.float(0.50, "  ...close this fraction of the gap per bar", minval=0.01, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="HOW THE STOP TIGHTENS AND LOOSENS WITHOUT EVER MOVING BACKWARDS.\n\nEach bar the trail works out where it WANTS to sit - a multiple of current volatility behind price - and then moves only this fraction of the remaining distance. It is a follow, not a snap.\n\nWHY THAT ALONE PRODUCES BOTH BEHAVIOURS.\n\n  Volatility FALLS (the overnight decay) - the target sits closer to price, so the gap is small and the stop pulls in quickly. TIGHTENING.\n  Volatility RISES (the regular-hours open) - the target sits further back, so for the same favourable move in price the stop advances LESS. LOOSENING, exactly as described, and the run gets more room.\n  Volatility falls again - the target closes back in and the stop resumes advancing more per favourable move.\n\nAnd it can never give profit back, because the one-way ratchet at the end of this block refuses any proposal that would move the stop away from price. Loosening here never means retreating; it only ever means advancing more slowly.")
atrTrailBlend = input.int(15, "  ...phased in over N bars, not switched on", minval=0, maxval=400, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="THE TRANSITION IS A RAMP, NOT A STEP. The owner asked for it to transition to a more custom accurate trailing style rather than jump.\n\nOver these bars the volatility trail goes from having no say at all to full strength. Without it, the bar the delay expires would yank the stop a long way in one move, which reads on the chart as a mistake even when the level is right.\n\nSet 0 to switch it on hard at the delay instead.")
atrFloorR = input.float(0.75, "  ...but never closer to price than (R)", minval=0.0, maxval=5.0, step=0.05, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="THE OVERNIGHT TAPE IS NOT A VOLATILITY READING, IT IS AN ABSENCE OF ONE.\n\nThis is the same regime error, pointing the other way. A one-minute bar at 3am on an extended-hours book can have a true range of well under a cent. Feed that straight into a multiple-of-ATR stop and the stop does not tighten, it COLLAPSES onto price, and the first ordinary move at the open picks it off.\n\nMEASURED IN v6.7, which shipped without this floor. CSCO 2026-08-12 short from 123.11: the volatility trail pulled the stop to 118.90 while price was 118.93 - a gap of THREE CENTS on a [n]R. It was taken out for [n]R instead of running to T3 at 114.20 and the session-end exit at 113.34, about [n]R. NBIS 2026-08-11 lost [n]R down to [n]R the same way.\n\nSo the volatility trail may never propose a stop closer to price than this many R. R is the trade s OWN risk, fixed at entry, so the floor means the same thing on a 3 dollar stock and a 600 dollar one, and it does not care what the clock says. Tightening from 3R away to [n]R away is still a large tightening - what it can no longer do is sit on top of price because the tape went quiet.\n\nSet 0 to remove the floor and get the raw v6.7 behaviour back.")
trailMode = input.string("Ladder only", "Stop behaviour", group=gTrail,
     options=["Fixed", "Breakeven once", "Ladder only", "Ladder + structural"],
     tooltip="FIXED — the stop never moves. The v3.0 behaviour, kept so you can put the old build back with one click.\n\nBREAKEVEN ONCE — moves to entry at the arm threshold, then stops. The v3.1-v3.3 behaviour.\n\nLADDER ONLY — SHIPPED DEFAULT. Each whole R of peak profit steps the stop up to lock (R minus giveback). Coarse, predictable, and structurally incapable of tightening into a pause because it only ever reacts to NEW profit. Verified on real TradingView bars: SPCE 2026-08-12 and CRWV 2026-08-11 both still reach [n]R, with a visible staircase on the way.\n\nLADDER + STRUCTURAL — adds a swing-anchored trail that follows actual pullback lows (longs) or highs (shorts) with an ATR buffer, and freezes while the move is consolidating. NOT the default, and here is why: measured on CRWV 2026-08-11 it stops the trade out at 16:21 instead of holding for T3. The 5-bar swing window rolls forward past the impulse lows and tightens to about 95.10 exactly as the 16:21 bar dips to 94.85.\n\nThat is a tuning problem, not a broken idea; a longer lookback or a wider buffer likely fixes it. But it is unmeasured, and this build does not ship unmeasured defaults. Switch it on and watch it against Ladder only before trusting it.\n\nWhichever is chosen, the stop is a RATCHET — it only ever moves in your favour. And it is drawn as a stepped line: horizontal, vertical riser, horizontal. Never slid, because sliding a line rewrites history and makes it look like the stop was hit when it was not.")

ladderT1R = input.float(-0.25, "Ladder: where T1 puts the stop (R)", minval=-1.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="Where the stop lands once the trade's PEAK first reaches [n]R.\n\nv8.0 used -0.50R, which left half the original risk exposed while the T1 tooltip claimed the trade could not become a loser.\n\nv8.1's first attempt used breakeven, and that was too tight: COHR 2026-08-12 entered SHORT at 344.99, reached T1, and was stopped out at 344.99 four minutes later on an ordinary consolidation wick.\n\n[n]R is the middle: half the give-back of v8.0, and far enough from the entry that a normal pullback does not collect it.")

ladderStepR = input.float(0.50, "  ...and how much each further rung adds (R)", minval=0.05, maxval=2.0, step=0.05, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="Each rung above T1 adds this much. At the default: T1 -> -0.25R, T2 -> +0.25R, T3 -> +0.75R, +4R -> +1.25R.\n\nv8.0 added a full R per rung above T2, which parks the stop on the T1 line once the peak reaches +2R - close enough to price to be collected by a pullback in a trade that goes on to make T3. CRWV 2026-08-11 is that case.")

beAfterR = input.float(1.0, "Arm the trail after (R)", minval=0.0, maxval=5.0, step=0.25, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="How far in profit the trade must go before the stop is allowed to move at all. 1.0 = the trail arms at +1R, which is also where the first ladder step lands. That step is NOT breakeven - see 'Ladder: where T1 puts the stop', which ships at -0.25R. Breakeven is the second rung. 0 arms immediately.\n\nNothing tightens the stop before this. An earnings move needs room in its first minutes.")

// The ladder has no fraction to tune any more. It steps onto the take-profit
// levels themselves. v9.1: this comment used to say "half-risk at T1", which was a
// third different claim about the same rung. The truth is ladderT1R / ladderStepR:
// T1 -> -0.25R, T2 -> +0.25R, T3 -> +0.75R, +4R -> +1.25R - so
// every level it uses is one already drawn on the chart. v3.5's tunable fraction
// produced levels like 94.405 and 96.223 that lined up with nothing visible.

trailLookback = input.int(5, "  Structural: swing lookback (bars)", minval=2, maxval=30, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="The structural trail sits behind the lowest low (long) or highest high (short) of this many bars, ending at the PREVIOUS bar. That is what makes it follow real pullback structure instead of a fixed distance from price.\n\nShorter = tighter and more reactive. Longer = more room through a deep pullback.")

trailATRmult = input.float(0.75, "  Structural: buffer (x bucket ATR)", minval=0.0, maxval=5.0, step=0.05, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="How far BEYOND that swing extreme the stop sits, measured in the session-phase bucket's own average true range — so the buffer scales with how violent this particular symbol and session actually are, instead of being a fixed percentage.\n\nThis is the single most important number for not being shaken out. Too small and ordinary noise takes you out; too large and the trail stops protecting anything.")

trailMinGiveR = input.float(0.75, "  Structural: never tighter than (R behind peak)", minval=0.0, maxval=3.0, step=0.25, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="A hard floor on how close the structural trail is ever allowed to get to the best price the trade has seen. 0.75 means the stop always stays at least three quarters of the original risk behind the peak.\n\nWHY IT EXISTS: the swing window can collapse. After a vertical move the recent-swing extreme sits close to price, so a naive structural level tightens right up underneath it and the very next ordinary bounce takes the trade out. This clamp makes that impossible.\n\nIt can only ever LOOSEN the proposed stop, never tighten it, so it cannot create a premature exit — it can only prevent one. Set to 0 to disable.")

runPastT3 = input.bool(true, "Let the trail run past +3R instead of closing at T3", group=gTrail,
     tooltip="ON (default from v3.5): the T3 badge still prints at [n]R but the position stays open and is handed to the trailing stop, so a move that keeps going keeps paying and the exit is decided by exhaustion rather than by a fixed target.\n\nThe reference case is SPCE, which ran past T3 and would have exited far later than a flat close at T3.\n\nOFF restores the v3.0/v3.4 behaviour: T3 closes the trade.")

trailGiveR = input.float(2.0, "  Past +3R: trail this far behind the peak (R)", minval=0.25, maxval=5.0, step=0.25, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="THE FIX FOR A TRAIL THAT LEVELS OFF - and nothing more than that.\n\nThe target ladder can only step when the trade gains another WHOLE R, so once the targets are behind you the steps become rare and the line looks parked. Past [n]R the stop also tracks this far behind the best price the move has reached, recomputed on every closed bar, so it keeps moving for as long as the trend lasts.\n\n2.00 IS NOT A TUNING CHOICE - IT IS THE LADDER'S OWN SLACK. At +N R the ladder locks (N - 2) R, which leaves the stop exactly 2R behind the peak. Setting this to 2.00 makes the post-T3 trail perfectly CONTINUOUS with the ladder: the stop keeps adjusting, at the same distance it was already keeping, and never becomes tighter than it was before the targets were hit.\n\nTWO EARLIER VERSIONS GOT THIS WRONG BY TIGHTENING:\n\n  v3.5 used 3 x the session-phase ATR. That ATR is built from QUIET extended-hours minutes - on CRWV it is 0.33 against a trade risk of 1.82 - so it put the stop [n]R behind the peak, at 98.861 with T3 at 99.404, and the next bar dipped to 98.60 and took it out. A unit error, not a tuning miss.\n\n  v3.6-v3.8 used [n]R. Better unit, still nearly 3x tighter than the ladder, which is why the stop still jumped up hard the moment T3 printed.\n\nAnything below 2.00 means the stop gets TIGHTER after T3 than it was before - that is the behaviour that was wrong.\n\nIt is a RATCHET like every other stop movement here: it can only move in your favour, and each move is drawn as another step in the staircase.")

freezeInCons = input.bool(true, "  Freeze the trail while consolidating", group=gTrail,
     tooltip="While the move is inside a detected consolidation — the same rolling-window test that produces the + marker — the structural trail does NOT tighten. It holds where it was and resumes only once the pause resolves.\n\nThis allows for retracement and consolidation. A pause is exactly where a naive trail does its worst damage: price stops making new extremes, the swing window contracts, and the stop creeps up into the noise it is supposed to be sitting outside of. Freezing removes that failure mode entirely.\n\nThe R ladder is unaffected — it only ever fires on NEW profit, so it cannot tighten into a pause.")


revATRmult = input.float(2.0, "  (INACTIVE) Reversal: bar range (x bucket ATR)", minval=0.5, maxval=10.0, step=0.1, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="Test 1a. The bar's high-to-low range as a multiple of the session-phase bucket's ATR.\n\nNECESSARY BUT NOWHERE NEAR SUFFICIENT. The bucket ATR is measured over quiet extended-hours minutes, and after an earnings print almost every bar clears a small multiple of it — on SPCE post-market the ATR is around two cents, so '2x ATR' is satisfied by ordinary four-cent chop. That is exactly how the first version of this test cut a winning trade. The event-scaled tests below are what actually decide; this one only screens out dead bars.")

revPrintFrac = input.float(0.60, "  (INACTIVE) Reversal: bar range (x print candle)", minval=0.1, maxval=2.0, step=0.05, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="Test 1b, the event-scaled size test. The bar's range as a fraction of the ORIGINAL print candle's range.\n\nThis is the yardstick that survives contact with a real earnings move, because it grows with the event instead of with the symbol's quiet-hours volatility. A bar has to be comparable in size to the print itself before it can be called a reversal of it.")

revClosePos = input.float(0.30, "  (INACTIVE) Reversal: closes within X of adverse end", minval=0.05, maxval=0.50, step=0.05, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="Test 2. Where the bar closes inside its OWN range, measured from the end that hurts. 0.30 means a long is only cut by a bar closing in the bottom 30% of itself.\n\nThis is the mirror of the confirm rule's close-location filter, and it is what separates a violent rejection from a wide bar that recovers. A bar that spikes against you and closes back mid-range is absorption, not reversal.")

revVolMult = input.float(1.5, "  (INACTIVE) Reversal: volume (x bucket median)", minval=0.5, maxval=10.0, step=0.1, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="Test 3. Volume relative to the median of the matching session-phase bucket — the same baseline the detector's RVOL gate uses, so it is measured against comparable minutes rather than against the closing auction.\n\nReal reversals are participated in. Consolidation is volume drying up, which is precisely why this test excludes it.")

revGiveFrac = input.float(0.75, "  (INACTIVE) Reversal: gives back X of print range", minval=0.05, maxval=2.0, step=0.05, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="Test 4, and the single most discriminating number in this whole group. How much of the ORIGINAL print candle's range this ONE bar hands back, measured from the bar's favourable extreme to its close.\n\nWHY 0.75. Every bar of the SPCE 2026-08-12 short was measured on real TradingView bars. Across the entire trade — through two consolidations and several sharp pullbacks — the largest single-bar give-back was 0.64 of the print range. Consolidation and pullback simply do not reach 0.75; a bar that erases three quarters of the print in sixty seconds is a different animal.\n\nThis is what separates the two shapes. Depth of retracement cannot do it, because consolidation and reversal both retrace. Bar SIZE alone cannot do it either, because after a print every bar is big. One bar undoing most of the event can.\n\nRaised from 0.35, which fired on ordinary chop repeatedly in that same trade.")

// Mode flags decoded once, here, because the status table further down reads them
// and Pine has no forward references - anything the table touches must already
// exist above it.
bool useLadder = trailMode == "Ladder only" or trailMode == "Ladder + structural"
bool useStruct = trailMode == "Ladder + structural"
bool useBeOnce = trailMode == "Breakeven once"

showAdd = input.bool(true, "Mark consolidation breakout (+)", group=gConf,
     tooltip="A yellow badge with a + on the candle where the move stops pulling back and starts going again. Green + for a long, red + for a short.\n\nThis is the 'add to the position' marker, and after a stop-out it reads as a re-entry.\n\nHOW IT IS MEASURED — REWRITTEN IN v3.5. It now asks whether price pulled back a modest amount and then resumed, which is the question you actually described. v3.3/v3.4 asked whether the last N bars had COMPRESSED below the print candle's range, and compression takes time to develop, so the badge always landed several bars after the dip worth buying.\n\n   peak    = best price since the confirm, in the trade's direction\n   pull    = how far price came back off that peak, measured in R\n   fires   = when the close reclaims a fraction of that pullback\n\nThe pullback has to fall inside a band — big enough to be a real pause, small enough not to be a reversal — and that band is what separates the two cases.\n\nSee the individual settings below for the measurements behind each number.")

consMinBars = input.int(3, "  Consolidation window (bars)", minval=2, maxval=10, group=gConf,
     tooltip="How many bars back the pause is measured over. The window ends at the PREVIOUS bar, so the breakout bar is never part of the range it has to beat.\n\nThis also sets the minimum spacing between two + markers.")

addWindowBars = input.int(10, "  Stop marking adds this many bars after the CONFIRM", minval=1, maxval=400, group=gConf,
     tooltip="THE ONLY BOUND ON ADDS, as of v4.6.\n\nWhat this REPLACED: a hard cap of 2 + markers per print, plus a 40-bar window measured from the PRINT.\n\nSo there is now NO limit on how many adds may fire. There is only this window, and it is measured from the CONFIRM - the bar you actually entered on - not from the print. Every add still has to earn its badge exactly as before: a real pullback of between addMinPull and addMaxPull R, lasting at least addMinBars, then reclaimed by addReclaim of its own depth.\n\nMeasured from the confirm rather than the print because the confirm is when the position actually exists. A print that took five bars to confirm used to spend five of its forty on a trade that had not started yet.")
addMinPull = input.float(0.5, "  Pullback must be at least (R)", minval=0.1, maxval=3.0, step=0.25, group=gConf,
     tooltip="Below this the dip is just noise inside a straight run and marking it tells you nothing. This is the lower edge of the band that defines 'a real pause'.")

addMaxPull = input.float(3.0, "  ...and at most (R)", minval=0.5, maxval=10.0, step=0.25, group=gConf,
     tooltip="Above this the move is not pausing, it is reversing, and adding into it is the wrong trade. This is the upper edge of the band.\n\nTogether with the minimum above, this pair is what separates the two cases - minor candles in the opposite direction, profit taking, compression before further expansion - from a genuine reversal - and it does it in units of YOUR risk on the trade rather than in cents.\n\n3.0 is set from CRWV, whose pullback measured [n]R and was still only a pause. Tightening this below about 2.5 would have excluded it.")

addReclaim = input.float(0.25, "  Fire once this fraction of the pullback is reclaimed", minval=0.05, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="How much of the dip price has to win back before the badge prints. 0.25 means a quarter of the way from the pullback's extreme back toward the peak.\n\nThis is the number that decides WHERE the badge lands, and it is why v3.5 marks the first resumption candle instead of one several bars later. Measured:\n\n   reclaim   CRWV        NBIS\n   0.20      16:22       07:46\n   0.25      16:22       07:46      <- shipped\n   0.33      16:22       07:48\n   0.50      16:25       07:49\n\nThe target candles were CRWV at roughly 16:20-16:22 and NBIS at 07:47-07:52. Small values fire on the first genuine sign of resumption; large values wait for confirmation and drift late, which was the v3.4 behaviour.")

addMinBars = input.int(2, "  Pullback must last at least (bars)", minval=1, maxval=10, group=gConf,
     tooltip="How many bars must pass after the peak before a reclaim counts. Stops a single wick down and straight back up from being called a pullback.")

addAfterStop = input.bool(true, "  Keep marking adds after a stop-out (re-entry)", group=gConf,
     tooltip="Let the + keep firing after the trade has been stopped out, for as long as the window below allows. It then reads as a RE-ENTRY rather than an add.\n\nWHY THIS IS ON: NBIS 2026-08-12, walked on real TradingView bars. Print 07:39, confirm long 220.73, stop hit 07:45 — and the consolidation worth buying does not begin until 07:52. Requiring an open position made the marker unreachable in exactly the case that wanted it most.\n\nAnd the stop was not the problem. At 0.60 retrace it is hit at 07:45; at 1.00 — the print candle's own low — it is still hit, at 07:46, because price gave back 109% of the impulse and traded BELOW the pre-print price. No stop width survives that.\n\nWhat DOES help is recognising the re-entry: the + fires at 07:52 close 217.85, which is a better price than the original 220.73 confirm.")


consMaxFrac = input.float(1.00, "  Window span max (x print candle)", minval=0.1, maxval=3.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="The pause qualifies when the window's ENTIRE span is at most this fraction of the print candle's range. 1.00 means 'the whole consolidation fits inside the print candle'.\n\nWHY THE + NEVER APPEARED BEFORE v3.3: this fraction used to be applied PER BAR as well as to the whole run. Each bar was allowed to be half the print range, but three such bars then had to collectively fit inside half the print range — which they cannot unless they overlap almost exactly. And because the run only reset on a non-quiet bar, once its span exceeded the budget it stayed exceeded. Measured on the real SPCE 2026-08-12 post-market bars: the bar-count gate passed on 8 bars, the span gate on 6, and both together on ZERO.\n\nIf this indicator was already on your chart before the upgrade, TradingView keeps your old stored value of 0.50. Settings -> Defaults -> Reset settings to pick up 1.00.")
useFlatExit = input.bool(true, "Exit when the tape goes FLAT after entry", group=gConf,
     tooltip="WHAT THIS IS FOR: a tape that goes flat right after entry. Either the indicator was false, or the entry came too late and the earnings influence has faded, so something else could move price in a direction the signal cannot predict.\n\nWHAT THIS MUST NEVER DO: cancel a trade on a consolidation back to around the confirmation price after a little continuation. There is still volatility there; that is consolidation.\n\nThe RVOL term is what separates the two. A consolidation pullback still carries volume and range and fails this test outright; a dead tape fails nothing. All three conditions must hold together.\n\nIt is deliberately RARE: a safety valve rather than a management rule. If you start seeing it on ordinary trades, raise flatBars or lower flatTravelR.")
flatBars = input.int(20, "  Flat: measured over the last N bars", minval=5, maxval=200, group=gConf)
flatTravelR = input.float(0.35, "  Flat: total travel under (R)", minval=0.05, maxval=2.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="High minus low across the whole window, in units of the trade's own risk.\n\nCalibrated against the case it must NOT fire on: AMD 2026-08-04 chopped roughly 470-490 for hours, which is [n]R of travel. At 0.35 that correctly reads as 'still moving' and is left to the exhausted-move exit instead. A genuinely dead extended-hours tape travels a small fraction of this.")
flatRvolMax = input.float(1.0, "  Flat: median RVOL under", minval=0.1, maxval=10.0, step=0.1, group=gConf,
     tooltip="Median relative volume over the same window. This is the term that separates a dead tape from a consolidation, and it is the reason this exit cannot fire on an ordinary pullback. RVOL 1.0 means 'a completely ordinary minute for this session bucket' - a consolidation inside a live earnings move runs several times that.")
flatOpenR = input.float(0.25, "  Flat: open P/L within +/- (R)", minval=0.05, maxval=2.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="The trade must still be sitting on its entry - this exit books a scratch, it never cuts a winner and never accepts a real loss.")
useExhaustExit = input.bool(true, "Exit a move that never got going", group=gConf,
     tooltip="THE AMD 2026-08-04 RULE, and separate from the flat exit on purpose - AMD is not flat, it is a 20-point chop that goes nowhere.\n\nMEASURED ON THE REAL BARS. Entry 466.47 short at 16:16, risk 33.97 points:\n     bar   15   peak [n]R   open [n]R\n     bar   60   peak [n]R   open [n]R\n     bar  120   peak [n]R   open [n]R\n     bar  600   peak [n]R   open [n]R\n     bar 1053   peak [n]R   open [n]R   <- stopped out\nThe trade NEVER exceeded [n]R in its favour across 1,053 bars. Its high-water mark was set inside the first 15 minutes and it was then carried 17.5 hours into the next morning's bullish open.\n\nA trade that reached [n]R can never be exhaust-exited - trPeak is the guard.")
exhaustBars = input.int(60, "  Exhausted: bars after entry", minval=10, maxval=1000, group=gConf,
     tooltip="One full hour of extended-hours tape.\n\nOn AMD the condition is already true at bar 15 and stays true at every checkpoint to the end, so the exact number is not delicately tuned - it is only 'how long do we give it'. Raise it if you want more rope.")
exhaustPeakR = input.float(0.50, "  Exhausted: never reached (R)", minval=0.1, maxval=3.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="The trade must never have been this far in front at ANY point since entry. This is the guard that makes the exit safe: anything that has genuinely worked is untouchable, and the trailing stop keeps managing it as before.")
exhaustOpenR = input.float(0.25, "  Exhausted: and open P/L at or under (R)", minval=-2.0, maxval=2.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="...and it is not winning right now either.")
useReentry = input.bool(true, "Re-enter after a stop-out", group=gConf,
     tooltip="THE STOP TOOK YOU OUT, THEN THE ORIGINAL READ TURNED OUT TO BE RIGHT.\n\nThe WOLF 2026-08-19 case: the print was correct, the confirm was correct, the direction was correct, and the trade was stopped out by chop on the way. The `+` add marker already fired afterwards, but an add is NOT a re-entry - it is consolidation-based, it carries no conviction test, and above all once the stop is hit the whole trade engine has shut down: no table, no stop, no targets, no trail. The badge appears and nothing is actually being managed.\n\nThis re-ARMS the print instead. It does not build a second trade engine - it clears impDone/impConf and re-anchors the print to the re-entry bar, so the ORDINARY confirm path runs again and produces a normal trade with a normal structural stop, normal targets and the normal trail. Everything downstream is the machinery that already works.\n\nIt is deliberately harder to trigger than an add: price must make a new extreme in the ORIGINAL direction beyond the stop-out bar, on real volume, closing at the favourable end of its own range. A drift back through the stop does not qualify.")
useSessionExit = input.bool(true, "Close the trade at the end of the first regular session", group=gTrail,
     tooltip="An earnings trade is a claim about how the market digests ONE release. Once a full REGULAR session has traded on that information, the earnings edge is spent and anything still open is an ordinary swing position being carried on an earnings-shaped stop.\n\nFN 2026-08-17 is the case: short from 600, [n]R, and STILL OPEN 2.5 trading days later because the ladder trail had nothing to stop it. Nothing about that tail belongs to the earnings event.\n\nThe trade is never cut mid-session and never before it has had one full regular day - the latch only arms during regular hours, so the exit lands on the first bar after that session ends. Marked with the same grey circle as the other conviction exits.\n\nTurn OFF to let the trail run indefinitely, which is the pre-v5.8 behaviour.")
reWindowBars = input.int(12, "  Re-entry: window after the stop (bars)", minval=1, maxval=200, group=gConf,
     tooltip="How long after a stop-out the thesis may still re-assert. Past this the earnings move is no longer what is driving price and a re-entry would just be a new trade wearing the old trade's clothes.")
reMaxCount = input.int(1, "  Re-entry: maximum per print", minval=1, maxval=5, group=gConf,
     tooltip="1 on purpose. A setup that stops out twice is telling you something; the third opinion is rarely worth paying for.")
reMinBody = input.float(0.35, "  Re-entry: candle body at least (x print body)", minval=0.0, maxval=3.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="MAGNITUDE MEANS THE SIZE OF THE RE-ENTRY CANDLE ITSELF.\n\nv6.1 measured magnitude as how far the close finished PAST the stop level, divided by the print body. That is a different question, and it threw away the case this signal exists for.\n\nMEASURED ON NBIS 2026-08-12. Entry 220.73, structural stop 215.85, stopped out 07:45. Three minutes later the 07:48 candle opens 214.00 and closes 216.69 - a large green bar that takes back the stop level on 4.8x RVOL, closing 77 percent of the way up its own range. Buyers absorbed the flush and defended. Under v6.1 it scored (216.69 - 215.85) / 5.39 = 0.16 against a 0.25 gate and was rejected, purely because it did not finish FAR ENOUGH above the stop. Its own body is 2.69 - half the print candle - and that is the number that should have counted: 2.69 / 5.39 = 0.50.\n\nThis is the OR of two readings, because the print body is not always a fair yardstick - see the range test below. Either one qualifies.")
reMinRangeAtr = input.float(2.0, "  ...or candle range at least (x session ATR)", minval=0.0, maxval=20.0, step=0.25, group=gConf,
     tooltip="THE SECOND READING OF THE SAME QUESTION, for when the print candle is not a fair yardstick.\n\nOn a re-entry the original release can be enormous relative to everything that follows, so a candle can be huge for the CURRENT tape while still being a small fraction of the print body. Measuring against the live session-phase ATR catches that case.\n\nEither this OR the body test above is enough. On NBIS 07:48 both pass - body 0.50x the print body, range 3.96 against a session ATR of 1.42, so 2.80x.\n\nThe two together are what separates a genuine re-assertion from the SPCE failure, where the stop is hit and a tiny consolidation candle then crosses it and reads as a re-entry. That is fluctuation. A tiny bar fails both readings at once.")
reMinRvol = input.float(4.0, "  Re-entry: min RVOL", minval=0.0, step=0.5, group=gConf,
     tooltip="The conviction test, and the main thing separating this from an add marker. Lower than the print gate because the release is already out and the tape has thinned - but a re-entry on dead volume is exactly the trade this is meant to avoid.")
reKeepFrac = input.float(0.30, "  Re-entry: original move still standing (x)", minval=0.0, maxval=2.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="A re-entry is a CONTINUATION, so some of the move it continues has to still be there. This is the fraction of the whole move - print candle OPEN to confirmation candle CLOSE - that price must still be holding, in the original direction, before a re-entry may arm.\n\nAAOI 2026-08-06 is what it exists for: it tried to re-enter LONG at a price BELOW where the entire bullish move began. NBIS 2026-08-12 is the case that must survive: it gave back far more than a normal pullback and still sat well above the start of its move, so there was a move left to continue.\n\nBefore a confirmation exists there is no whole move to measure, so the test stands aside. Set to 0 to switch it off.")

reCloseFrac = input.float(0.40, "  Re-entry: close within X of the favourable end", minval=0.05, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="Same discipline as everywhere else in this script: the CLOSE decides, not the wick. A bar that pokes back through the old extreme and closes weak is a failed retest, not a re-entry.")
reMinBodyRng = input.float(0.35, "  Re-entry: body at least X of its own range", minval=0.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="THIS IS WHAT STOPS RELATIVE VOLUME CARRYING A CANDLE ON ITS OWN.\n\nThat is exactly right, and it is the SPCE failure. A bar can be wide and heavily traded and still close within a tick of where it opened - long upper AND lower wicks, a fat body-less range. That is two-sided churn, not buyers taking the level back. Volume tells you HOW MUCH traded; it cannot tell you WHO WON. Only the body can.\n\nSo the body must be at least this fraction of the candle RANGE. It is an AND, never an alternative: a re-entry now needs the reclaim, the right direction, real size, a decisive body, AND volume. No single one of them can qualify a bar.\n\nMeasured on NBIS 2026-08-12 07:48 - body 2.69 on a range of 3.96, so 0.68. It clears this comfortably, which is the difference between a candle that took the level back and one that merely touched it.")
colTp   = input.color(color.new(#00c853, 0), "Target hit ($)", inline="ex", group=gConf)
colStop = input.color(color.new(#616161, 0), "Stop hit (✕)", inline="ex", group=gConf)

stopRetrace = input.float(0.60, "Stop: fraction back toward the print candle", minval=0.1, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=gConf,
     tooltip="0 = at the entry, 1 = the print candle's far side. 0.60 means the stop sits 60% of the way from your entry back toward the print candle's opposite extreme.\n\nNO NUMBERS ARE QUOTED HERE. The 0.60 default came from a sweep on a consolidated-tape feed, not on TradingView bars, so under the TradingView-only rule it is an UNVALIDATED inherited default and quoting its expectancy table would be quoting numbers from a feed you do not trade.\n\nThe one thing that HAS been measured on real TradingView bars is a negative result worth knowing: on NBIS 2026-08-12 this setting does not matter. At 0.60 the stop is hit at 07:45, at 0.80 at 07:45, and at 1.00 — the print candle's own low — still at 07:46, because price gave back 109% of the impulse and traded below the pre-print price. Widening the stop does not save that trade at any value.\n\nThis is the largest un-revalidated number left in the script.")

showTradeLines = input.bool(true, "Draw stop / TP lines after a confirm", group=gConf,
     tooltip="Dotted red = your stop. Dashed green = TP1 (+1R). Dotted green = TP2 (+2R). They extend to the right for as long as the trade is live.")
colStop2 = input.color(color.new(#ff5252, 0), "Stop line", group=gConf)

showInvalidation = input.bool(false, "Also mark full invalidation (!)  (OFF — redundant)", group=gConf,
     tooltip="OFF from v3.5. Marks the bar where the ENTIRE earnings move has been given back.\n\nIt is doing the same job as the X now that the stop is real, and worse: on PLUG 2026-08-10 the X and the ! both landed on the SAME BAR at the SAME PRICE (2.23) and drew on top of each other, so one of them was unreadable. Two markers for one event, overlapping, telling you nothing extra.\n\nLeft available because it is genuinely a different question — 'the whole move is gone' rather than 'my stop was touched' — and there may be a setup where you want to see it. Off by default.")

// v7.0 - DECLARED LAST ON PURPOSE. TradingView stores inputs BY POSITION
// (in_0..in_N), so appending is the only way to add a control without silently
// shifting every stored value after it and corrupting the settings of every
// chart that already has this study. It still appears inside the Timing group,
// because the settings dialog clusters by `group=`, not by declaration order.
escResetBars = input.int(30, "Reset the size gate after N normal bars", minval=0, maxval=400, group=gTime,
     tooltip="THE HALF OF THE DEAF WINDOW THAT WAS MISSING.\n\nThere are three situations, and this is the second.\n\n1. Normally, after a print, nothing is blocked - a second print is allowed, it just has to be MORE SIGNIFICANT than the first.\n2. After the tape has been back to normal for a while, the gates should go back to their traditional values, so the next event no longer has to out-muscle a print that is long dead. THIS SETTING.\n3. While a trade is open the rules are different again.\n\nWHY RULE 1 ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH. The re-fire test asks the new bar to beat the previous signal on volume AND excursion. After a genuinely enormous release - CRWV printed at 176x relative volume - nothing later that day can beat it, so in practice only something bigger may fire degrades into nothing may fire. That is the deaf stretch, and it is not what was intended.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT TOUCH. It clears only the size gate. The print itself is still remembered, because a REVERSAL has to be judged against the move it is fading, and nothing here runs while a position is open.\n\nBack to normal means the same thing it means for the stale-print reset - median relative volume and median range/ATR over the last few bars both back under the print thresholds - so the script has one definition of a quiet tape, not two.\n\nSet 0 to disable and keep the old behaviour, where a dead print gates the rest of the session.")

// v7.1 - APPENDED LAST, same reason as escResetBars above: TradingView stores
// inputs BY POSITION, so anything but appending shifts every stored value after
// it and silently corrupts the settings of every chart that already has this
// study. These two still appear in the Confirmation group.

// v7.5 - APPENDED LAST, same discipline as escResetBars / useHold: TradingView
// stores inputs BY POSITION, so appending is the only way to add controls without
// shifting every stored value after them. These three appear in the Trailing stop
// group.
atrTightDecay = input.int(120, "  ...tightening ramps over N more bars", minval=0, maxval=1000, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="HOW LONG THE LATE TIGHTENING TAKES TO ARRIVE.\n\nBut 60 minutes, 120 minutes, three hours after the trade, through the later evening and the early morning, that stop is too loose.\n\nSo nothing changes for the first hour. From then on, over this many bars, the stop is progressively drawn in: the ATR multiple falls toward the late multiple below, and the R floor falls toward the late floor below. On a 1-minute chart the shipped 120 means the trade is fully tightened about three hours in, which is the intended timescale.\n\nSet 0 to switch the decay off and get the flat v7.4 behaviour back - one distance for the whole trade.")
atrMultLate = input.float(1.0, "  ...late ATR multiple (decays toward this)", minval=0.1, maxval=20.0, step=0.25, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="WHERE THE VOLATILITY DISTANCE ENDS UP once the trade is old.\n\nEarly on the stop sits the full multiple back from price - that is the room an earnings release earns. By the end of the ramp it sits only this many ATRs back, so a quiet overnight tape pulls the stop in hard instead of leaving it parked.\n\n1.0 WAS RE-CONFIRMED ON THE [n]-SYMBOL SWEEP, with the floor at its shipped 0.45. Total R over the [n] symbols excluding SPCX:\n\n  multiple 5.0 ... [n]R\n  multiple 3.0 ... [n]R\n  multiple 2.0 ... [n]R\n  multiple 1.5 ... [n]R\n  multiple 1.0 ... [n]R  SHIPPED\n\nThis knob matters far less than the floor. On some of the symbols where the trail actually moves, every value from 1.0 to 3.0 gives an identical result - overnight the ATR collapses toward zero, so the floor is what sets the distance and the multiple never binds. Only 5.0 is loose enough to change anything, and it scores worse.\n\nRaise it toward the early multiple for a gentler finish.")
atrFloorRLate = input.float(0.45, "  ...late R floor (decays toward this)", minval=0.0, maxval=5.0, step=0.05, group=gTrail,
     tooltip="THIS IS THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY MATTERS, and it has been measured on [n] symbols.\n\nThe floor stops a dead overnight tape collapsing the stop onto price. But it is expressed in R, and on a trade with a big R that floor is enormous: CBRS entry 238.55 with R 13.97 means a 0.75 floor pinned the stop 10.48 points behind price all night, no matter what volatility did. Price reached [n]R and the trade still closed at [n]R. The floor, not the volatility distance, is what kept the stop loose.\n\nSo the floor decays too. Early it stays generous, because a fresh earnings trade genuinely needs that room. Late it falls to this. Nothing here touches the first hour of a trade.\n\n0.45 WAS CHOSEN BY SWEEPING [n] SYMBOLS - every stock that reported on or after 4 August with both a 2x long and a 2x short ETF. The old 0.70 came from only [n] symbols, and SPCX alone drove it.\n\nTotal R over the [n] symbols EXCLUDING SPCX, late multiple held at 1.0:\n\n  floor 0.75 ... [n]R\n  floor 0.70 ... [n]R  (what v7.7 shipped)\n  floor 0.65 ... [n]R\n  floor 0.60 ... [n]R\n  floor 0.55 ... [n]R\n  floor 0.50 ... [n]R\n  floor 0.45 ... [n]R  SHIPPED - the best value found\n  floor 0.40 ... [n]R  (falls off the CSCO cliff, see below)\n\nDO NOT DROP BELOW 0.45. There is a cliff immediately underneath it: with the floor at 0.44 CSCO holds to 13:42 for [n]R, and at 0.43 it is stopped at 11:46 for [n]R. That is a [n]R drop across one hundredth of a step, and 0.40 - the next value the settings panel will let you pick - is already past it. 0.45 is the last good step, deliberately.\n\n0.45 WAS CHOSEN OVER THE SAFER 0.50 ([n]R) KNOWING THAT. A tight stop is the preferred default, and there are already two ways to get a looser one - the STAY IN THE TRADE override, and switching the tightening off entirely. So the default is biased tight on purpose, because the loose case is the one that has an escape hatch.\n\nWHY SPCX IS EXCLUDED FROM THE HEADLINE NUMBER. SPCX is the one symbol that prefers a loose floor, and it prefers it hard: [n]R at 0.70 against [n]R at 0.45. Including it flips the answer, which is exactly what happened when 0.70 was chosen on [n] symbols. Most of the symbols the floor actually moves - QBTS, CBRS, COHR and CSCO - all improve as it tightens. Counting SPCX in, all of them score [n]R here against [n]R at 0.70, so the loose setting wins only on the strength of that single name.\n\nRaise it if late stops start getting picked off by ordinary noise; the whole point of having a floor at all is that a thin tape produces meaningless ATR readings.")

// ── v10.1 - THE EARNINGS RESULT BLOCK ────────────────────────────────────────
// Pine has NO network access, so this indicator can never fetch a filing itself.
// These strings are the way in. `tools/earnings-now.py --push` watches SEC EDGAR
// for the 8-K carrying Item 2.02, pulls the press release, and writes the numbers
// straight into these inputs over the TradingView desktop debug port. The panel
// then renders them under the existing rows.
//
// DECLARED LAST ON PURPOSE. TradingView maps a saved setting to its input by
// position, so inserting anywhere above here would shuffle every stored value on
// a live chart. Appended, the existing in_0..in_129 keep their meaning.
//
// Leave them blank and nothing renders - the panel is exactly as it was.
gEarn = "Earnings result (filled automatically)"
earnTitle = input.string("", "Headline", group=gEarn,
     tooltip="Written by tools/earnings-now.py. Typically the ticker, the quarter and how long after the release the filing landed.\n\nClear it to hide the whole block.")
earnRow1 = input.string("", "  Row 1", group=gEarn, tooltip="Format is LABEL|VALUE. Usually EPS against consensus.")
earnRow2 = input.string("", "  Row 2", group=gEarn, tooltip="Format is LABEL|VALUE. Usually revenue.")
earnRow3 = input.string("", "  Row 3", group=gEarn, tooltip="Format is LABEL|VALUE. Usually the beat or miss.")
earnRow4 = input.string("", "  Row 4", group=gEarn, tooltip="Format is LABEL|VALUE. Usually forward guidance.")


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// SESSION AND BUCKET
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

inPre  = session.ispremarket
inPost = session.ispostmarket

sessId = inPre ? 1 : inPost ? 2 : 0

// State for the escalation gate. Declared here so it can be cleared the moment a
// new session block starts - last night's print must never gate tonight's.
var int   lastSigDir = 0
var float lastSigVol = na
var float lastSigExc = na

// ── REVERSAL state (v3.12) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Kept separate from the escalation state above on purpose. A reversal is judged
// against the previous REVERSAL, never against the earnings print - the print is
// almost always bigger, so measuring a fade against it would block every one.
var int   revCount   = 0    // reversals fired since the current print
var float lastRevVol = na
var float lastRevExc = na

// Declared up here, not down with the rest of the trade state, because the escalation gate below
// needs to know whether a trade is already open. Pine has no forward references - a var must
// exist before the line that reads it. v8.1 - THE WHOLE MOVE, which is what every magnitude test
// should be measured against.
//
// CRWV 2026-08-11 is the case. Its print candle body is small, so a reversal test
// scaled to that body is cleared by an ordinary later candle and prints a false
// reversal. Scaled to the whole move it is not.
//
// A re-entry does NOT reset it: the move began where it began. `movRef` is fixed at the
// confirmation that opened the trade and then frozen.
var float movOpen = na
var float movRef  = na
// v8.1d - the furthest favourable CLOSE since the direction was armed. The move is
// measured OPEN TO CLOSE throughout, so this is the close-based counterpart of
// `impE`, which is an extreme and therefore carries the wick.
var float movBest = na
var bool  vetoMarked = false
// v9.0 - the open of the bar that started this EARNINGS EPISODE. `movOpen` resets on a reversal
// as well as a print, which is right for the magnitude tests but wrong for the exhaustion veto:
// a reversal-driven trade was being asked "how far has price come since the REVERSAL candle",
// which is never 9% and so could never be vetoed. This is set by a PRINT only and survives
// reversals and re-entries.
var float epiOpen = na

var int   trDir = 0

// ── EARNINGS PRINT STATE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Declared HERE, not down with the trade block where it used to live, because the
// v3.12 reversal test (`revCandidate`) reads impBar and impDir and Pine has no
// forward references. Moving these up is the whole fix for four
// "Undeclared identifier" errors. Assignments still happen in the trigger block
// far below; only the declarations moved.
var int   impBar = na
var float impA   = na
var float impE   = na
var int   impDir = 0
var bool  impDone = false
// v4.6 - DID THIS PRINT ACTUALLY EARN A CONFIRM? This is NOT impDone.
// impDone means "this print is finished being a confirm CANDIDATE" and is set
// true both by a successful confirm AND by the confirm window simply expiring.
// Using it as the stale-reset guard is what silently disabled that whole
// feature in v4.4 - see the reset block below.
var bool  impConf = false
var bool  invFired = false
var float impOpp = na       // print candle's OPPOSITE extreme - the real stop anchor
var float impRng = na       // print candle's range - the yardstick for "quiet"
var float impBody = na      // print candle's BODY - the yardstick for a REVERSAL

// ── RE-ENTRY STATE (v5.4) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Declared HERE rather than down with the trade block, because the post-trade reset
// below has to clear them and Pine has no forward references.
var int   reBar   = na      // bar of the stop-out we may re-enter from
var float reRef   = na      // extreme the thesis must beat to prove it re-asserted
var float reStop  = na      // the STOP LEVEL that was hit - what price must reclaim
// v8.14 - and the DIRECTION that re-entry is allowed to re-arm. It is the direction of the trade
// that was stopped out, which is not always `impDir`: a reversal flips impDir on the way out,
// and keying the re-entry to impDir then let it re-arm the reversal's direction instead of the
// original trade's.
var int   reArmDir = 0
var float reArmOpen = na    // the move's origin for the trade being re-entered
var float reArmMove = na    // and that trade's whole move, frozen at its exit
var int   reCount = 0       // re-entries already used for this print
var bool  holdOn    = false // the override is armed for the CURRENT trade
var bool  holdBreach = false// currently sitting past the stop while held open,
                            // so the marker prints once per breach and re-arms
var int   trBarTime = na    // wall-clock time of the entry bar, so the override
                            // can tell which trade the chosen bar belongs to
var int   exitBar = na      // bar the position closed on, whatever closed it

var int sessStartMs = na
if sessId != 0 and sessId != nz(sessId[1], 0)
    sessStartMs := time
    lastSigDir := 0
    lastSigVol := na
    lastSigExc := na
    revCount   := 0
    lastRevVol := na
    lastRevExc := na

intoMin = na(sessStartMs) ? 0 : int(math.floor((time - sessStartMs) / 60000))
phase   = intoMin < phaseMin ? 0 : 1
bucket  = sessId == 0 ? -1 : (inPre ? 0 : 2) + phase

// Bars elapsed since this BUCKET began, not since the session began. The guard
// therefore also applies just after the phase boundary, which is how the
// thresholds were calibrated.
var int bucketBars = 0
bucketBars := bucket != nz(bucket[1], -1) ? 0 : bucketBars + 1


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ROLLING PER-BUCKET BASELINES
//
// Four volume arrays and four true-range arrays, each holding the most recent N
// bars of its own bucket and spanning as many days as that takes.
//
// Pushes are gated on barstate.isconfirmed. This is load-bearing: Pine rolls back
// scalar var state on every realtime tick, but it does NOT roll back mutations to
// arrays. Without the guard, a live bar would push its partial volume into the
// baseline on every single tick and poison it within a minute.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Fifth bucket: REGULAR-HOURS volumes, used for a chart-native absolute-size
// gate. This is the same idea as the old request.security() ADV gate - "is this
// actually a lot of shares, not just loud for a dead tape" - but built entirely
// from bars the chart already has, so it cannot disagree with TradingView's feed
// and cannot silently zero out the signal.
var array<float> vRth   = array.new_float()

// v5.1 - the same idea for RANGE. This is the per-stock yardstick the absolute
// reversal path needs. Built from REGULAR-HOURS minutes only, so an after-hours
// release cannot inflate its own reference the way the bucket ATR does.
var array<float> rRth   = array.new_float()

// v4.6 - POOLED extended-hours samples, every bucket together. Used ONLY when
// the phase-matched bucket is too cold to produce a median. See usePoolFallback.
var array<float> vAll   = array.new_float()
var array<float> tAll   = array.new_float()

var array<float> vPre0  = array.new_float()
var array<float> vPre1  = array.new_float()
var array<float> vPost0 = array.new_float()
var array<float> vPost1 = array.new_float()
var array<float> tPre0  = array.new_float()
var array<float> tPre1  = array.new_float()
var array<float> tPost0 = array.new_float()
var array<float> tPost1 = array.new_float()

f_push(array<float> a, float v, int cap) =>
    array.push(a, v)
    if array.size(a) > cap
        array.shift(a)

// ta.tr must be evaluated on every bar, never inside a branch, or its history
// goes inconsistent.
trNow = ta.tr(true)

// Point at this bar's bucket. Arrays are reference types in Pine, so the two
// selections below stand in for the real arrays for both reading and writing.
array<float> vSel = na
array<float> tSel = na
if bucket == 0
    vSel := vPre0
    tSel := tPre0
else if bucket == 1
    vSel := vPre1
    tSel := tPre1
else if bucket == 2
    vSel := vPost0
    tSel := tPost0
else if bucket == 3
    vSel := vPost1
    tSel := tPost1

// Sample count as a plain scalar. array.size() on an na array is a RUNTIME error
// in Pine, not an na, so it can never be reached from a ternary or a plot call.
int baseN = 0
if not na(vSel)
    baseN := array.size(vSel)

// NESTED ifs, deliberately, not `not na(vSel) and array.size(vSel) >= n`.
// Pine's `and` is not reliably short-circuiting, so the flat version can still
// reach array.size() while vSel is na - which is a RUNTIME ERROR that halts the
// whole script. A halted script draws no arrows AND no status table, which is
// exactly the symptom seen on CBRS. Nested ifs cannot be evaluated eagerly.
float medVol = na
float atrSes = na
bool  basePooled = false
if not na(vSel)
    if not na(tSel)
        if array.size(vSel) >= minSamples and array.size(tSel) >= minSamples
            medVol := array.median(vSel)
            // v7.0 - STAYS A MEAN, AND THIS IS A MEASURED DECISION, NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
            //
            // The owner is right about the statistic: the bucket straddles the release,
            // so a mean lets the print inflate its own denominator and the NEXT
            // genuine print is judged against a yardstick the first one bent. The
            // volume baseline one line up has always been a median for exactly that
            // reason.
            //
            // BUT SWITCHING THIS ONE TO A MEDIAN BREAKS THE ACCEPTANCE SET, and the
            // measurement says the size gate cannot fix it. Median atrSes is about a
            // third of the mean here, so every range/ATR reading roughly triples. On
            // CRWV 2026-08-11 that let two bars INSIDE the release itself print:
            //
            //     20:09  real print   range/ATR 16.16   RVOL  48.1
            //     20:12  spurious     range/ATR 18.39   RVOL  74.5   <- prints DOWN
            //     20:13  spurious     range/ATR 14.94   RVOL  98.3   <- prints as a reversal
            //
            // The spurious bar scores HIGHER than the real print, so no threshold
            // separates them. The honest reading is that the inflated mean has been
            // quietly doing the job the print cooldown should do - cooldownBars is 3,
            // and the release plainly lasts longer than three minutes.
            //
            // So the fix is a longer cooldown plus a median, recalibrated together
            // and measured across every symbol - not a one-line statistic swap
            // dropped into a build that is about to be tagged golden. Tracked as its
            // own item rather than smuggled in here.
            atrSes := array.avg(tSel)

// v4.6 - COLD-BUCKET FALLBACK. The phase-matched bucket is always preferred; this
// only ever runs where the alternative is no signal at all. PLTR 2026-08-03 is the
// case: an 8.5% print at 16:05 on the first loaded session, with 5 samples in a
// bucket that needs 12 and no deeper 1-minute history available to fetch.
if na(medVol) or na(atrSes)
    if usePoolFallback and bucket >= 0
        if array.size(vAll) >= minSamples and array.size(tAll) >= minSamples
            medVol := array.median(vAll)
            atrSes := array.avg(tAll)
            basePooled := true

// ── v6.7 - THE TRAIL'S OWN VOLATILITY MEASURE ──────────────────────────────────
// atrSes above is bucketed by SESSION PHASE and its window straddles the release,
// so it deliberately blends quiet and explosive minutes - correct for asking "is
// this bar unusual for this time of day", useless for asking "how volatile is it
// right now". The trail needs the second question, so it gets its own number:
// a plain rolling mean of true range over the last atrTrailLen bars, no buckets.
// Declared at top level because ta.* series functions must be evaluated on every
// bar, not conditionally inside the trade block.
float atrLive = ta.sma(ta.tr(true), atrTrailLen)

// Append AFTER measuring, so a bar is never part of its own baseline.
// Nested for the same reason as above.
if barstate.isconfirmed
    if not na(vSel)
        if not na(tSel)
            f_push(vSel, volume, baseLen)
            f_push(tSel, trNow, atrLen)
    if bucket >= 0
        f_push(vAll, volume, baseLen * 4)
        f_push(tAll, trNow, atrLen * 4)

// Regular-hours volumes for the absolute-size gate. vRth is created by
// array.new_float() and is therefore never na, so array.size() is safe here.
if barstate.isconfirmed and session.ismarket
    f_push(vRth, volume, rthSamples)
    f_push(rRth, high - low, rthSamples)

float rthMed = na
if array.size(vRth) >= 30
    rthMed := array.median(vRth)

// Median REGULAR-HOURS minute range for this symbol. "What does one ordinary
// minute of this stock look like." A $2 speculative name and Home Depot get
// completely different numbers, which is the entire point.
float rthRngMed = na
if array.size(rRth) >= 30
    rthRngMed := array.median(rRth)
rthRatio = not na(rthMed) and rthMed > 0 ? volume / rthMed : 0.0


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// MEASUREMENTS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

advDaily  = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "D", ta.sma(volume, advDays)[1], lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off)
advMinute = nz(advDaily) / math.max(rthMinutes, 1)

rvol     = nz(medVol, 0.0) > 0 ? nz(volume, 0.0) / medVol : 0.0
advRatio = advMinute > 0 ? nz(volume, 0.0) / advMinute : 0.0
rangeAtr = nz(atrSes, 0.0) > 0 ? (high - low) / atrSes : 0.0

// Measured to the bar's extremes, not its close, so the value can only grow as
// the minute develops. That is what keeps a live marker from disappearing.
refPx  = nz(close[impulseLen], close)
upExc  = high - refPx
dnExc  = refPx - low
isUp   = upExc >= dnExc
excPct = refPx > 0 ? 100.0 * math.max(upExc, dnExc) / refPx : 0.0

// Conviction 0-100. Ratio-to-threshold on each active gate, capped at 3x, so
// meeting every threshold exactly lands near 50 and doubling them lands at 100.
// It ranks signals; it never creates them.
// The inputs, used directly. No preset, no override.
eRvol  = minRvol
eAdv   = minAdvRatio
eRng   = minRangeAtr
eMove  = minMovePct

// v8.1f - PRICE ACTION SO LARGE IT CANNOT BE ANYTHING BUT THE RELEASE.
// Used by two gates below, both of which are volume-shaped and both of which can
// veto the very candle they exist to catch. Price cannot be faked by a thin tape,
// which is why it is the right thing to trust when volume disagrees.
// v8.3 - MEASURED ON THE CANDLE'S OWN VIOLENCE, NOT ON A PERCENTAGE MOVE.
// v8.1f keyed this on excursion AND range/ATR together, and excursion is the wrong
// co-requirement: it is a percentage, so a high-priced name that falls 2% scores low
// on it however violent the candle is.
//
//   NVDA 2026-08-26 20:20, the earnings candle, 210.80 -> 206.27 on 226,469 shares:
//        excursion   1.99%  vs 1.5 needed  =  1.3x   <- fails a 3x waiver
//        range/ATR   83.4x  vs 5.8 needed  = 14.4x
//        RVOL        1356x  vs 12  needed  =  113x
//   The candle is 83 times a normal bar's range on 113 times normal volume, and the
//   waiver still refused it because the stock "only" moved 2%. The print landed a
//   minute late on 20:21 and the whole trade shifted with it.
//
// Range/ATR and RVOL are both measured against this bar's OWN bucket baseline, so they
// say "this candle is nothing like the tape around it" - which is the actual claim the
// waiver needs to make. Both must clear the multiple, so an ordinary bar cannot reach
// it: at the shipped 3x that is 17.4x ATR AND 36x RVOL.
bool absPriceOverwhelming = absWaiveMult > 0 and eRng > 0 and eRvol > 0 and
     rangeAtr >= absWaiveMult * eRng and rvol >= absWaiveMult * eRvol
eAbsOn = useAbsGate

// Second route in, for reactions that arrive as a staircase rather than one
// violent candle. Both terms are monotonic inside a live bar, like the rest.
vol3   = nz(volume, 0) + nz(volume[1], 0) + nz(volume[2], 0)
ref3   = nz(close[3], close)
hi3    = math.max(high, nz(high[1], high), nz(high[2], high))
lo3    = math.min(low, nz(low[1], low), nz(low[2], low))
move3  = ref3 > 0 ? 100.0 * math.max(hi3 - ref3, ref3 - lo3) / ref3 : 0.0
burst3 = advMinute > 0 ? vol3 / (3.0 * advMinute) : 0.0

c1 = eRvol > 0 ? math.min(rvol     / eRvol, 3.0) : 1.0
c2 = eRng  > 0 ? math.min(rangeAtr / eRng,  3.0) : 1.0
c3 = eMove > 0 ? math.min(excPct   / eMove, 3.0) : 1.0
float c4 = eAbsOn and eAdv > 0 ? math.min(advRatio / eAdv, 3.0) : na
cN = na(c4) ? (c1 + c2 + c3) / 3.0 : (c1 + c2 + c3 + c4) / 4.0
score = math.round(math.min(100.0, 100.0 * cN / 2.0))


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// FILTERS AND SIGNAL
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

inSess = sessMode == "Post-market only" ? inPost :
     sessMode == "Pre-market only" ? inPre : (inPre or inPost)

minOfDay   = hour * 60 + minute
preOpenOk  = preOpenGuard <= 0 or not inPre or minOfDay < (570 - preOpenGuard)
// v8.1f - THE SESSION GUARD MAY NOT SKIP THE RELEASE CANDLE ITSELF.
// guardBars = 1 discards the session's first bar, which is correct almost always: on
// AMAT 2026-08-13 that bar traded 1,647 shares against 12,103 on the next one, and
// raising this from 2 to 1 is what fixed the old "print is one candle late" bug.
//
// But for a report released AT the bell the first post-market bar IS the earnings
// candle, and the guard then throws it away. INTU 2026-08-25:
//
//     20:00   open 364.00  close 316.00   body 48.00   [n]%   126.6x ATR
//     20:01   open 316.43  close 307.48   body  8.95    [n]%     9.1x ATR
//
// The 20:00 bar cleared RVOL 1273x, range/ATR 126.6x and excursion 11.71% - every
// price gate by more than an order of magnitude - and was discarded for being first.
// The print landed on 20:01, a bar less than a fifth its size, and every downstream
// calculation anchored to the wrong candle.
//
// The guard stays, because thin 16:00 prints are real. It is overridden only when the
// bar's own price action is overwhelming, which a thin bar can never be.
guardOk    = bucketBars >= guardBars or absPriceOverwhelming
armed      = bucket >= 0 and not na(medVol) and not na(atrSes) and medVol > 0 and atrSes > 0

// The "once only" allowance is keyed on the session block (sessStartMs uniquely
// identifies this pre-market or this post-market), never on the calendar day -
// see the input tooltip for the COHR case that forced this.
var int lastFireBar = na
var int lastFireBlock = na
coolOk = na(lastFireBar) or (bar_index - lastFireBar) >= cooldownBars
dayOk  = not oneShot or na(lastFireBlock) or lastFireBlock != sessStartMs

volOk = rvol     >= eRvol
rngOk = rangeAtr >= eRng
movOk = excPct   >= eMove
// v8.1e - a candle whose PRICE action is many times its own requirements is an
// earnings print whatever its volume did, because a gap through an empty book
// trades LESS, not more. Defined above; INTU 2026-08-25 is the worked case.
absOk = not eAbsOn or eAdv <= 0 or advMinute <= 0 or advRatio >= eAdv or absPriceOverwhelming

// v8.17 - `volOk` REMOVED. It required the very gate it exists to bypass.
//
// This is the second way in, for a reaction that arrives as a staircase rather than one
// violent candle. Its own tooltip says so: "for reports whose reaction arrives as a fast
// staircase rather than one violent candle. Those never clear the single-bar range gate
// no matter how it is set." But `volOk` IS the single-bar gate - RVOL >= 12 against the
// bucket median - so the alternative route demanded the thing it was built to replace,
// and could only ever rescue a bar that had already cleared it. A move spread evenly
// over three ordinary-looking minutes cannot reach 12x on any one of them, which is
// precisely the case this path was written for.
//
// The three-bar terms are the gate now, and they are not loose: 1.5x normal volume
// across the window AND a 4% move. `gatesOk` still requires `rthOk` on top.
//
// NO BEHAVIOUR CHANGE AS SHIPPED - `useBurst` is false by default, so burstOk is false
// either way. This makes the feature real if it is ever switched on, instead of dead
// code that looks like a feature. The owner agreed it could simply be deleted; deleting three
// inputs would shift every stored input position and reset his saved settings, which is
// not worth paying for a switched-off path right before v9.0 is cut.
burstOk = useBurst and burst3 >= minBurstVol and move3 >= minBurstMove

// Chart-native absolute-size gate. Passes automatically until enough regular
// hours bars exist to form a median, so a fresh chart is never dead.
// v4.6 - see the thinFloor tooltip. Where the extended-hours tape is thinner than
// this fraction of a regular-hours minute, a MULTIPLE-of-an-RTH-minute test cannot
// be satisfied by any real move, so it is not a filter and is skipped. LLY reads
// 0.008 here; INFQ 0.055; ASTS 0.065. minRthMult itself is unchanged at 3.0.
thinRatio = not na(rthMed) and rthMed > 0 and not na(medVol) ? medVol / rthMed : na
rthDead   = thinFloor > 0 and not na(thinRatio) and thinRatio < thinFloor
rthOk = not useRthGate or na(rthMed) or rthMed <= 0 or rthDead or rthRatio >= minRthMult

gatesOk = ((volOk and rngOk and movOk and absOk) or burstOk) and rthOk

// Escalation gate: after a signal, the next one must either flip direction or
// beat the last on BOTH volume and move. Never caps the session - a genuinely
// bigger or opposite event always gets through.
// v3.11 - THE SIZE TEST NOW APPLIES REGARDLESS OF DIRECTION.
//
// The old rule was `flip OR (bigger volume AND bigger move)`. That `thisDir !=
// lastSigDir` term let an opposite-direction print skip the size test COMPLETELY,
// and that is the AMD 2026-08-04 defect:
//
//   16:15  real print, DOWN   vol 95,183   excursion 7.79%
//   16:22  flip,       UP     vol 42,697   excursion 3.30%   <- fired anyway
//
// 45% of the volume and 42% of the excursion of the real print, and it produced a
// long print AND a long confirm in the middle of a live short. Nothing about that
// bar was more significant than the event it was contradicting.
//
// Now: a re-fire in EITHER direction must beat the last signal on volume OR on
// excursion. AND vs OR was checked against real bars and it matters:
//
//   AMD  16:22 flip     blocked under both - the fix works either way
//   ASTS 16:30 reversal vol 16,970 > 16,625 but excursion 3.01% < 4.31%
//                       -> AND would BLOCK it, OR lets it through
//
// ASTS is the reversal-warning case worth keeping, so OR it is, which is
// also the intent: one or more of greater relative volume or a greater
// move.
//
// KNOWN COST, measured, not hidden: this also blocks the CSCO 2026-08-12 short.
// Its print was 16:05 (vol 104,787, exc 7.74%) and the flip that produced the
// trade was 16:08 (vol 30,384, exc 5.13%) - smaller on both, so it no longer
// fires. There is no clean discriminator between that flip and AMD's on these
// numbers. To get CSCO back, restore `thisDir != lastSigDir` as a third OR term.
thisDir = isUp ? 1 : -1
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// FORGET AN UNCONFIRMED PRINT THAT WENT NOWHERE - v4.4
//
// ASTS 2026-08-10 is the case. A big pre-earnings candle at 16:05 cleared every gate
// on merit - real volume, real range - and became the session print. It never earned a
// confirm. But it stayed the anchor, so when the actual release landed at 16:29 the
// script measured it against that stale blip, and the real earnings move came out
// classified as a REVERSAL against a false print. Everything downstream inherited the
// wrong reference.
//
// The owner's rule, and it is the right one: a print that never confirmed and then went
// nowhere was not an earnings release. Forget it, so the next real move is judged as a
// FIRST signal rather than having to out-muscle a ghost.
//
// AGE, NOT A QUIET RUN. A consecutive-quiet-bar counter was tried first and measured
// against the real bars it does not work - the longest unbroken quiet run between the
// ASTS blip and the release is 7 bars, because three middling bars keep restarting it.
// Age has no such hole.
//
// WHY 12 IS SAFE: confirmWindow is 6, so an unconfirmed print is already dead for
// confirmation purposes at 6 bars. Forgetting it at 12 discards nothing that could
// still have become a trade.
//
// Conservative on the one thing that must not break:
//   * requires NO OPEN POSITION      (trDir == 0)
//   * requires the print UNCONFIRMED (not impDone)
// A confirmed trade is never forgotten, whatever price does afterwards.
//
// Clearing lastSigDir is the part that actually fixes ASTS - it is what lets the real
// release be judged on its own merits instead of having to beat the blip.
// ── v4.6 - WHY THIS DID NOTHING IN v4.4 ────────────────────────────────────────
// The guard was `not impDone`, and impDone does NOT mean "a trade is running".
// It means "this print has finished being a confirm candidate", and it is set true
// by BOTH outcomes: a successful confirm, and the confirm window simply expiring.
//
//     confirmWindow = 6, so an unconfirmed print sets impDone at impBar + 7
//     staleBars     = 8, so the reset was due at impBar + 8
//
// impDone was therefore ALWAYS true one bar before the reset could run. Every
// threshold greater than confirmWindow + 1 was unreachable by construction, which
// is exactly why 12 and then 8 both changed nothing and why moving the block
// around the file changed nothing either. It was never a threshold or an ordering
// problem.
//
// The guard is now `not impConf` - a print that never earned a confirm may be
// forgotten; one that did is protected, and trDir == 0 still protects an open
// position on top of that.
// Both medians are evaluated UNCONDITIONALLY on every bar and only then read in
// the test. ta.median depends on its own history, so calling it inside a
// conditional lets it skip bars and silently drift - and this file already
// learned the hard way that Pine's `or` is not reliably short-circuiting.
int   sqLen      = math.max(staleQuietBars, 1)
float sqRvolMed  = ta.median(rvol,     sqLen)
float sqRangeMed = ta.median(rangeAtr, sqLen)
bool  staleQuietOk = staleQuietBars <= 0 or
     (sqRvolMed < minRvol and sqRangeMed < minRangeAtr)
bool staleFire = false
if staleBars > 0 and not na(impBar) and impDir != 0 and trDir == 0 and not impConf
    if (bar_index - impBar) >= staleBars and staleQuietOk
        staleFire  := true
        impBar     := na
        movOpen    := na
        movBest    := na
        epiOpen    := na
        movRef     := na
        impDir     := 0
        impA       := na
        impE       := na
        impOpp     := na
        impRng     := na
        impDone    := false
        // v8.14 - THE SIZE BASELINE SURVIVES A STALE RESET. This block exists to retire
        // a print that never produced a trade so a LATER one can be found; erasing
        // lastSigVol/lastSigExc as well threw away the record of how big this session's
        // real move was, and the next bar then had nothing to beat.
        //
        //   SMCI 2026-08-11, read off the chart. Real print 20:05, o31.83 c34.02, body
        //   2.19 on the session's volume spike. It never confirmed, the tape quietened,
        //   and the stale reset fired at 20:17 (reset FIRED 1, bars-since-print -> -1).
        //   At 20:20 a bar with o34.43 c34.74 - body 0.31, about a seventh of the real
        //   move - printed as a fresh EARNINGS PRINT because the baseline was gone.
        //   Everything the owner objected to downstream followed from that one false print.
        //
        // `escResetBars` is the deliberate, time-based way to forget the size gate: 30
        // quiet bars with no position. That mechanism is untouched. A stale print at 12
        // bars is a different event and has no business clearing it early.
        //
        // lastSigDir is kept too - it is what `escBase` tests to know a baseline exists.
        revCount   := 0
        lastRevVol := na
        lastRevExc := na

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// A FINISHED TRADE MUST NOT GATE THE NEXT EVENT  (v5.6)
//
// THE NBIS BUG. The stale-print reset above is gated on `not impConf`, and impConf
// latches TRUE the moment a print confirms and never clears. So the reset can only
// ever forget a print that NEVER traded. A print that confirmed, opened a position
// and then stopped out stayed the session anchor forever - and every gate
// downstream kept measuring against it:
//
//    * `escBase` / `supersedes` demanded the next event beat the DEAD print's
//      volume AND excursion
//    * `lastSigDir` made the real release look like a same-direction re-fire
//    * `impBar` / `impDir` kept the reversal machinery pointed at it
//
// NBIS 2026-08-11: a false breakout printed at 199, confirmed, stopped out, re-entered and
// stopped again - and the REAL earnings move the next morning could never print, because it was
// still being judged against a trade that had been dead for hours.
//
// So: once the position is closed AND the re-entry window has elapsed, forget
// everything. Waiting for the re-entry window matters - clearing immediately would
// destroy the very state the re-entry needs to re-arm the print.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v8.6 - AND THE REVERSAL WINDOW MUST HAVE ELAPSED TOO. The comment above already
// makes the argument for the re-entry window: clearing immediately would destroy the
// state the re-entry needs. Exactly the same is true of the reversal window, and it
// was not honoured, so the reversal window could never outlive the re-entry window
// however high it was set.
//
//   NVDA 2026-08-26 is the case. Print 20:20, trade closed 20:49, re-entry window is
//   twelve bars, so at 21:02 every reference was wiped. The earnings CALL ran at 21:03
//   on a candle 24.8x a normal minute with 6.6x bucket volume - and by then there was
//   no print left to reverse AGAINST. `bars since print` reads 35 at 21:01 and -1 at
//   21:02. Raising revWindowBars from 20 to 60 in v8.4 did nothing for this, because
//   the thing the window measures FROM had already been deleted.
//
// The state now survives until BOTH windows have closed. revWindowBars is a hard cap
// of its own, so this cannot drift back to the "judged against a trade dead for hours"
// failure the block was written to fix - it simply extends the memory to the horizon
// the reversal rules already claim to use.
bool revWindowLive = revWindowBars > 0 and not na(impBar) and
     (bar_index - impBar) <= revWindowBars
if not na(exitBar) and trDir == 0 and (bar_index - exitBar) > reWindowBars and
     not revWindowLive
    impBar     := na
    movOpen    := na
    movBest    := na
    epiOpen    := na
    movRef     := na
    impDir     := 0
    impA       := na
    impE       := na
    impOpp     := na
    impRng     := na
    impBody    := na
    impDone    := false
    impConf    := false
    lastSigDir := 0
    lastSigVol := na
    lastSigExc := na
    revCount   := 0
    lastRevVol := na
    lastRevExc := na
    reBar      := na
    reStop     := na
    reCount    := 0
    exitBar    := na


// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE GATES GO BACK TO NORMAL WHEN THE TAPE DOES  (v7.0)
//
//   1. Normally, after a print, NOTHING is blocked. A second print is allowed - it
//      just has to be MORE SIGNIFICANT than the first. That is `escBase` below and
//      it already worked.
//   2. After the tape has been back to normal for a while, the gates should RESET
//      to their traditional values, so the next event no longer has to out-muscle
//      a print that is long dead. THIS BLOCK.
//   3. While a trade is open the rules are different again - `busyWhileOpen`.
//
// WHY 1 ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, AND WHY THIS IS NOT THE SAME AS THE STALE RESET.
// `supersedes` demands the new bar beat the old one on volume AND excursion. After
// a genuinely enormous release - CRWV printed at 176x RVOL - essentially nothing
// later that day can beat it, so "only something bigger may fire" collapses in
// practice into "nothing may fire". That is the deaf stretch.
//
// The existing stale reset cannot cover this: it is gated on `not impConf`, so it
// only ever forgets a print that NEVER traded. The finished-trade reset covers the
// case where a position opened and closed. Neither covers a print that fired,
// never confirmed, and sits there holding the bar high while the tape has plainly
// gone quiet - nor a confirmed print whose trade ended without the exit path that
// sets `exitBar`.
//
// So this is deliberately narrow. It clears ONLY the escalation baseline. It does
// NOT clear `impBar`/`impDir`, because those are the reversal machinery's anchor
// and a fade must still be judged against the print it is fading. Situation 3 is
// untouched: nothing here runs while a position is open.
//
// "Back to normal" reuses the SAME test the stale reset uses - median RVOL and
// median range/ATR over the last few bars both below the print gates - so there is
// one definition of a quiet tape in this script, not two.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
if useEscalate and escResetBars > 0 and trDir == 0 and lastSigDir != 0
    if not na(lastFireBar) and (bar_index - lastFireBar) >= escResetBars
        if staleQuietOk
            lastSigDir := 0
            lastSigVol := na
            lastSigExc := na

// Does this bar BEAT the last signal on both measures? This one test does double
// duty: it is the escalation gate for prints, and it is also what separates a
// SUPERSEDING print from a FADE when the direction flips.
supersedes = nz(volume, 0) > nz(lastSigVol, 0) and excPct > nz(lastSigExc, 0)

// ── HOW FAR HAS THE FADE ALREADY TRAVELLED? (v3.14, default OFF) ────────────────
// Measures this bar's adverse extreme against the PRINT'S extreme, in units of the
// print candle's own range. It is the cleanest separator found between a real
// reversal and heavy consolidation, because it asks whether the earnings move has
// actually been undone rather than merely paused.
//
// Measured on TradingView bars:
//
//   ASTS 2026-08-10  16:30   print hi 72.18, bar lo 67.34   -> 2.99 x print range
//   FN   2026-08-17  16:17   print hi 650.89, bar lo 600.00 -> 1.06 x
//   SMCI 2026-08-11  16:28   print hi 34.56,  bar lo 33.45  -> 0.41 x   <- the chop
//
// DEFAULT IS 0 (OFF) ON PURPOSE. CSCO 2026-08-12 is a working trade and
// its one-minute history would not load on any tab, so this could not be checked
// against it - and a rough read put CSCO near 0.65, which 0.75 would block. Not
// shipping a gate that might silently delete a working trade. Set it to 0.75 once
// CSCO is confirmed, or leave it off and accept the SMCI entry.
revRetraceOk = revMinRetrace <= 0 or na(impE) or na(impRng) or impRng <= 0 or
     (impDir > 0 ? (impE - low) : (high - impE)) >= revMinRetrace * impRng

escBase = not useEscalate or lastSigDir == 0 or supersedes

// Now ANY direction is blocked while a position is open. Once a trade exists it
// has a stop and targets; the trailing stop decides the exit, not a second marker.
// The stop-out is what re-opens the door.
busyWhileOpen = suppressWhileOpen and trDir != 0

raw = armed and inSess and guardOk and preOpenOk and gatesOk

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// TWO CLASSES OF SIGNAL - v3.12
//
// The owner's framing, and it resolves several fights at once:
//
// EARNINGS PRINT  ▲ / ▼   the release itself. Must be RARE. A later candidate
//                         only supersedes an earlier one by being genuinely
//                         bigger on volume AND excursion, in either direction.
//
// REVERSAL        R       fires only AFTER a print is established, only in the
//                         OPPOSITE direction, and is EXEMPT from the escalation
//                         size test. Everything downstream is identical - confirm,
//                         stop, targets, adds - because it is a tradeable setup.
//
// WHY THE REVERSAL MUST BE EXEMPT FROM ESCALATION. Measured on real TradingView
// bars, CSCO 2026-08-12: the print was 16:05 at 104,787 vol / 7.74% excursion,
// and the reversal that set up the short was 16:08 at 30,384 /
// 5.13% - SMALLER ON BOTH. A reversal is not a bigger version of the earnings
// move, it is a different event reacting to it. Any "must supersede" rule kills
// it by construction, which is exactly what happened in v3.11 when that trade
// disappeared. Escalation is the right rule for identifying the release and the
// wrong rule for identifying a fade.
//
// It still has to clear every detection gate - RVOL, range vs bucket ATR,
// excursion, RTH-median - so a pullback or a consolidation bar cannot produce
// one. Requiring range >= 5x the bucket ATR is itself the conviction test, and
// it is what separates "price drifted back" from "something changed".
//
// HOW THIS FIXES EACH REPORTED CASE, using only the open-trade rule plus the
// class split:
//   CRWV  ▼ 16:21   a long was OPEN  -> blocked. The spurious mark is gone.
//   AMD   ▲ 16:22   a short was OPEN -> blocked.
//   CSCO  ▼ 16:08   long never confirmed, so NO trade was open -> fires as R.
//   SMCI  ▼         long never confirmed -> fires as R, which is the setup asked
//                   for on exactly this shape.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// A flip is only a FADE if it does NOT supersede the print. If it beats the print
// on both volume and excursion it is the real release and the earlier mark was the
// false one, so it stays a ▲/▼.
//
// ── v3.14: THE REVERSAL JUDGES DIRECTION BY THE CLOSE ──────────────────────────
// This is the fix for "why does the R fire a few candles late".
//
// `isUp` (line ~542) compares which EXTREME is further from the close 3 bars back.
// That is deliberate for prints: measured to the extremes, the value can only GROW
// as a live minute develops, which is what stops a live marker appearing and then
// vanishing. Do not change it.
//
// But it misclassifies the very candle a reversal lives on. ASTS 2026-08-10 16:30
// fell 71.70 -> 67.38, a 4.36 range, and closed 2.05 BELOW the reference - yet
// because it OPENED at the spike top its high sat 2.27 above the reference while
// its low was only 2.09 below. upExc >= dnExc, so the biggest reversal candle on
// the chart was labelled an UP bar, was never considered a reversal, fell through
// to the print path, failed the size test and was blocked. The R could not appear
// until the 3-bar reference rolled forward past the spike.
//
// A reversal only ever matters at a bar CLOSE - there is no live-flicker exposure
// to protect against - so it judges direction the way the eye does: where did this
// candle close relative to the reference.
// ── v4.0: THE REVERSAL IS JUDGED ON ITS OWN TERMS ──────────────────────────────
// The deeper problem:
//
//   A move far up and then all the way back down is a large reversal, yet price
//   ends very close to where it started. Excursion measures distance from the
//   start, so it does not register that reversal even though one happened on
//   the tape.
//
// `excPct` measures from the close 3 bars back. When a bar undoes the
// whole earnings move, price ENDS near where it started, so the down-excursion is
// tiny - and worse, `excPct` takes the LARGER of up and down, so on a bar that
// opened at the spike top the gate passes on the UP magnitude while the candle is
// violently red. The excursion gate is right for finding a release and actively
// misleading for finding a fade.
//
// Two replacements, reversal path only:
//
//   DIRECTION   the candle itself is against the print - close vs OPEN. A red
//               candle after an up-print. This is what the eye reads, and unlike
//               the extreme test or the close-vs-reference test it cannot be
//               fooled by a bar that opens at the top of the spike.
//
// Verified on TradingView bars:
//
//   FN   16:16   red, gives back 0.72x impulse   FIRES  <- the arrowed candle
//   FN   16:17   red, 1.04x                      (capped by revMaxCount)
//   ASTS 16:30   red, 3.18x                      FIRES  <- the marked candle
//   SMCI deepest bar reaches only 0.43x          never fires  <- the chop, excluded
//
// One rule, and it fixes the late-FN problem and the false-SMCI problem together.
// ── v4.2: JUDGE THE CANDLE, NOT THE CUMULATIVE RETRACEMENT ─────────────────────
// The old measure was (impE - low) / impulse: how far price had come back from the
// print's extreme. That is CUMULATIVE, and it fails in both directions at once:
//
//   * once price has drifted past the threshold, EVERY later bar against the trade
//     satisfies it - including tiny ones. SPCX fired on the smallest red candle in
//     the middle of a bounce, because by then the cumulative test was long since
//     satisfied and nothing else was asking how big THAT bar was.
//
//   * the FIRST big red candles can FAIL it, because price has not retraced far
//     enough from impE yet. On SPCX the move ran up well past the print extreme, so
//     the major red candles that actually began the reversal were rejected.
//
// So it skipped every worthy candle and then picked the worst one.
//
// v4.2 measures THIS BAR'S OWN BODY against the print candle's range. A reversal is
// a big candle going the other way - that is the whole claim, and it is now the
// whole test.
//
//   ASTS 16:30   body 4.32 / print range 1.62  = 2.67x   FIRES
//   FN   16:16   body 23.77 / 50.89            = 0.47x   FIRES  <- arrowed candle
//   SMCI 16:28   body 0.40 / 2.73              = 0.15x   blocked <- the chop
//
// A small candle can no longer qualify however far price has already fallen, and a
// large one no longer has to wait for a retracement threshold to be reached.
revAgainst = impDir > 0 ? close < open : close > open
revBarBody = impDir > 0 ? (open - close) : (close - open)
// v5.1 - MEASURED AGAINST THE PRINT CANDLE'S BODY, NOT ITS RANGE. The range counts the print
// candle's own WICK as part of the move a reversal has to undo, which is the same mistake the
// reversal candles themselves are protected from. COHR 2026-08-12: the print ran 360.05 ->
// 373.97 but wicked down to 354.72, so its range is 24.28 against a body of 13.92. The two red
// candles that followed gave back 11.12 - visibly "well over half" the move - and scored 0.458
// of the range, failing every gate, while scoring 0.799 of the body. The body is what the market
// actually committed to; the wick is noise on both sides of the comparison. v8.1 - THE
// YARDSTICK. Before a confirmation exists the print candle's body is the only thing available,
// so nothing changes there. ONCE CONFIRMED the yardstick becomes the whole move - print candle
// open to confirmation candle close - which is the move the trade was actually taken from. This
// is the single change that stops CRWV printing a reversal on an ordinary candle. BEFORE a
// confirmation exists there is still a move to measure - the travel from the print candle's open
// to the furthest favourable point reached so far. TWLO 2026-08-06 is why this matters: the
// print candle closed at 193.30 while price was already above 215 two minutes later, so the
// print candle's BODY is a hopeless yardstick and an ordinary bar clears 0.42 of it. Using the
// move so far, the same bar is nowhere near the threshold. v8.1d - BODY TO BODY. This used
// `impE`, which is the print candle's HIGH (or LOW), so the reference carried the wick while the
// reversal candle it was compared against was measured by its BODY.
//
// COHR 2026-08-12 is the case. Print candle open 360.05, close 373.97, so the body is
// 13.92 - but the running high was about 379, making the old reference 19.0. The two
// red candles that followed measured 5.09 and 6.03: against 19.0 they are 0.268 and
// 0.317, combined 0.585, failing both the 0.30-each and 0.60-combined tests by a
// whisker. Against the 13.92 body they are 0.366 and 0.433, combined 0.799, and PATH 2
// fires on the second one - which is where the owner's eye said the reversal belonged.
if not na(movBest) and impDir != 0
    movBest := impDir > 0 ? math.max(movBest, close) : math.min(movBest, close)
revMoveSoFar = na(movOpen) or na(movBest) ? na : math.abs(movBest - movOpen)
revRefPre    = na(revMoveSoFar) ? impBody : math.max(nz(impBody, 0.0), revMoveSoFar)
revRef       = impConf and not na(movRef) and movRef > 0 ? movRef : revRefPre
revGiveX   = na(revRef) or revRef <= 0 ? na : revBarBody / revRef

// Where this bar closed inside its OWN range, on the reversal side. 0.0 = closed
// hard at the adverse extreme, 1.0 = closed at the far end. A reversal candle should
// close near its low (fade of an up-print) or near its high (fade of a down-print);
// a consolidation candle closes in the middle. This is the single cheapest test that
// separates the two, and it is applied to both of the new paths below.
revBarRng   = high - low
revBarClosePos = revBarRng > 0 ? (impDir > 0 ? (close - low) / revBarRng : (high - close) / revBarRng) : 1.0

// Note revBarBody is open-to-close and has ALWAYS excluded wicks, so no wick has ever
// counted toward any magnitude threshold here. What was missing was this: a bar could
// have a qualifying BODY and still close far from its adverse extreme.
revGiveOk1 = not na(revGiveX) and revGiveX >= revImpGive and revBarClosePos <= revPairClose

// That is a real defect and it is an artefact of the one-minute grid, not of the
// market. A reversal does not know where the minute boundary is. Both candles must be
// adverse, both must individually be worth something, together they must clear the
// bar, and THIS one must close at its adverse end.
//
// This is NOT the v4.0/v4.1 cumulative-retracement rule that had to be removed. That
// one measured from the print extreme forever, so once price had fallen far enough
// every later bar qualified including tiny ones - it fired on the smallest red candle
// in the middle of a bounce on SPCX. This looks at exactly two adjacent bars and
// imposes a floor on each, so it cannot drift into that failure.
revPrevAgainst = impDir > 0 ? close[1] < open[1] : close[1] > open[1]
revPrevBody    = impDir > 0 ? (open[1] - close[1]) : (close[1] - open[1])
revPrevGiveX   = na(revRef) or revRef <= 0 ? 0.0 : revPrevBody / revRef
revGiveOk2 = revPairSum > 0 and revPrevAgainst and
     nz(revGiveX, 0) >= revPairMin and revPrevGiveX >= revPairMin and
     (nz(revGiveX, 0) + revPrevGiveX) >= revPairSum and
     revBarClosePos <= revPairClose

// Every other measure here is RELATIVE: to the print candle, to a rolling bucket
// median, to a bucket ATR the release itself has already inflated. All three can
// shrink a genuinely enormous candle into a small number. This path is ABSOLUTE -
// body as a percentage of price - and it answers only "is this candle, on its own,
// unmistakably large". It can only ADD reversals, never remove one.
//
// v5.1 - SCALED PER STOCK, not a fixed percentage. Exactly right - a flat 4% is meaningless for
// a $2 name and unreachable for Home Depot, so it would have been a per-symbol tuning knob
// forever.
//
// The yardstick is the median REGULAR-HOURS minute range for this symbol. Regular
// hours on purpose: the bucket ATR is contaminated by the release itself, which is
// the documented reason a genuine reversal candle scores 1.4x minutes after an
// identical candle scored 8.5x. RTH minutes are not.
//
// Set revSelfMult to 0 to switch this path off.
revSelfX   = not na(rthRngMed) and rthRngMed > 0 ? revBarBody / rthRngMed : 0.0
revGiveOk3 = revSelfMult > 0 and revSelfX >= revSelfMult and revBarClosePos <= revPairClose

// Any one of the three is enough. They are deliberately NOT mutually exclusive. v8.7 - AND IT
// MUST ACTUALLY HAVE GIVEN SOMETHING BACK. The three paths above all ask how big THIS CANDLE is
// - against the move, against its neighbour, or against a normal minute. Not one of them asks
// whether price has come back. That is a pause, not a reversal, and it was cancelling live
// trades.
//
// The move is measured the way everything else in v8.1+ measures it: the open of the bar
// that armed the direction, through to the furthest CLOSE reached since. Giving back
// 0.30 of that means closing beyond the 70% mark on the way home.
//
// Applied to revGiveOk itself rather than to one path, so the absolute single-candle
// path - which has no relationship to the move at all - cannot route around it.
float revSpan = na(movOpen) or na(movBest) ? na : (movBest - movOpen) * impDir
float revBack = na(movBest) ? na : (movBest - close) * impDir
bool  revGiveBackOk = revGiveBackMin <= 0 or na(revSpan) or na(revBack) or revSpan <= 0 or
     (revBack / revSpan) >= revGiveBackMin
revGiveOk  = (revGiveOk1 or revGiveOk2 or revGiveOk3) and revGiveBackOk
revPath    = not revGiveBackOk ? 0 : revGiveOk1 ? 1 : revGiveOk2 ? 2 : revGiveOk3 ? 3 : 0

// v4.6 - a reversal belongs to the print it is reversing. Past revWindowBars the
// release is no longer what is moving the tape, and the bar falls through to the
// normal first-signal path where it may print as its own event.
revWinOk = revWindowBars <= 0 or (not na(impBar) and (bar_index - impBar) <= revWindowBars)

// v6.0 - `not supersedes` REMOVED, and this was a real hole.
//
// SPCX 2026-08-04, the bar ONE minute after the 16:02 print:
//     body 3.37 vs a print body of 2.73  ->  1.23x, against a gate of 0.42
//     closed at 0.026 of its own range   ->  slammed shut on its low
//     volume 159,033 vs the print's 109,410
// A textbook reversal, and it printed nothing at all.
//
// The reason: it had MORE volume and MORE excursion than the print, so
// `supersedes` was true, which vetoed it as a reversal candidate. And a fresh
// PRINT was blocked at the same moment by cooldownBars. Too big to be a reversal,
// too soon to be a print - the bar fell through the crack between the two.
//
// The veto was wrong in principle anyway. A reversal is a move in the OPPOSITE
// direction to the print; it being LARGER than the print is not a disqualification,
// it is the strongest possible evidence that the move has turned. The escalation
// test that `supersedes` implements belongs to same-direction re-fires, which is
// exactly where `escBase` still applies it.
revCandidate = showReversal and not na(impBar) and impDir != 0 and
     revAgainst and revGiveOk and revRetraceOk and revWinOk

// Reversals get their own escalation among themselves, so a fade cannot spam:
// a second R must beat the first on volume AND excursion, same test the print
// uses, but measured against the last REVERSAL rather than against the print.
// Scored on volume and on GIVEBACK, not excursion - same reasoning as above.
revEscOk = revCount == 0 or
     (nz(volume, 0) > nz(lastRevVol, 0) and nz(revGiveX, 0) > nz(lastRevExc, 0))

// THE REVERSAL GETS ITS OWN COOLDOWN, and this was the other half of the "R fires
// a few candles late" problem. `cooldownBars` is 3, so after a print NOTHING could
// fire for five bars - but a reversal happens IMMEDIATELY after the print by
// definition. That is the shape. On ASTS the print is 16:29 and the reversal candle
// is 16:30, one bar later, so the general cooldown alone guaranteed the R could
// never land on it. Sensible for prints, exactly backwards for fades.
revCoolOk = na(lastFireBar) or (bar_index - lastFireBar) >= revCooldown


// A reversal clears its OWN gate set, not the print's. It keeps the range-vs-ATR
// test, which is the real "significant move, not consolidation" filter, and the
// RVOL test. It drops two that are wrong for a fade:
//
//   EXCURSION      replaced by the impulse-giveback test above.
//   vs RTH MEDIAN   a fade is a REACTION and trades less than the release by
//                   nature. Requiring 3x a normal regular-session minute is a
//                   "this is a major event" test - correct for the release, too
//                   strict for the response to it. On FN 16:16 volume is 899
//                   against an RTH median near 2,570, so that gate alone would
//                   veto the correct candle.
// v4.6 - THE REVERSAL NOW HAS ITS OWN THREE GATES rather than borrowing the
// print's. The print gates were raised to RVOL 12 / range-ATR 6 and that is correct
// for identifying a release; applied to a fade they measure it against a bucket ATR
// the release itself has already inflated, so a genuine reversal candle scores 1.4x
// minutes after an identical candle scored 8.5x. The excursion floor is kept at the
// print's own 1.5% so the looser pair cannot admit chop.
revRngOk   = rangeAtr >= revMinRangeAtr
revVolOk   = rvol     >= revMinRvol
// v8.5 - A SINGLE BAR MAY QUALIFY ON ITS OWN. `excPct` measures how far price has
// TRAVELLED from the close three bars back - a property of a 3-bar window, not of this
// candle. That is the right question for a release and the wrong one for a fade, which
// the revImpGive tooltip already says at length: a move up and straight back down ends
// near where it started, so the round trip scores near zero however violent the candle
// is. The code then gated on it anyway.
//
//   NBIS 2026-08-26 is the proof. The reversal belongs on 20:23 - a GREEN candle, body
//   2.19, clearing the magnitude test decisively at 0.78 against the 0.42 needed:
//        20:23  close[3] = 213.83 (20:20). Price had come back to where it began, so
//               travel is tiny and excPct lands near 1%      -> BLOCKED
//        20:24  close[3] = 210.89 (20:21). The window now straddles the low, so travel
//               reads 2.2%                                   -> FIRED
//   A SMALLER candle fired because the 3-bar window happened to sit differently. That is
//   an artefact of where the window falls, not a judgement about the candle.
//
// Two ways to satisfy the floor now, either alone:
//   1. the 3-bar travel, exactly as before - nothing that already worked changes, OR
//   2. THIS BAR'S OWN RANGE as a percentage of price, so one violent candle needs no
//      help from its neighbours.
// And it is waived outright when the magnitude test is comfortably clear, because by then
// the candle has proved it is a fade and the floor is only a noise filter in the way.
float revOwnExc  = close > 0 ? 100.0 * (high - low) / close : 0.0
float revExcUse  = math.max(nz(excPct, 0.0), revOwnExc)
bool  revGiveBig = revExcWaive > 0 and revImpGive > 0 and
     nz(revGiveX, 0.0) >= revExcWaive * revImpGive
revExcOk   = revExcUse >= revMinExc or revGiveBig
revGatesOk = revRngOk and revVolOk and revExcOk and (revExemptRth or rthOk)
rawRev     = armed and inSess and guardOk and preOpenOk and revGatesOk

// v5.1 - THERE IS NO LONGER ANY TIME-BASED RESTRICTION ON A REVERSAL beyond
// revCooldown = 1, which only means "not the print bar itself". The v4.6 near-print
// magnitude escalation is GONE. The owner's reasoning, and it is correct: preliminary
// headline numbers move price one way, then the fuller release lands a minute or two
// later and reverses it. The first few candles after a print are precisely WHERE
// reversals live, so gating them harder than any other bar is backwards.
//
// What separates a reversal from consolidation is now carried entirely by the three
// magnitude paths above plus the reversal's own RVOL / range / excursion gates.
// v8.12 - `not busyWhileOpen` REMOVED. THIS IS THE "ONE REVERSAL DETECTOR" CHANGE,
// which was written into v8.1's notes and never actually landed on this line.
//
// Compare the two tests as they stood:
//     revCoreNow = rawRev and revCandidate and revCoolOk and (...)
//     trigRev    = revCoreNow AND not busyWhileOpen AND revCount<max AND revEscOk
//
// busyWhileOpen is true for every bar of an open trade, so while in a trade ONLY THE
// EXIT HALF COULD FIRE. revCoreNow closed the position and drew the badge; trigRev was
// blocked, so the arm block never ran and the opposite direction was never armed. A
// SECOND reversal candle was then required, once flat, just to arm what the first one
// had already proved.
//
// revCount < revMaxCount and revEscOk are KEPT - those are what stop a fade spamming the
// chart, and they are about the reversal's own escalation, not about whether a position
// happens to be open.
trigRev   = rawRev and revCandidate and revCoolOk and
     revCount < revMaxCount and revEscOk and (liveMode or barstate.isconfirmed)

// v8.1 - THE SAME TEST, FOR THE IN-TRADE EXIT.
//
// `trigRev` governs ARMING - drawing the badge and pointing the machinery the other way.
//
//   * the re-entry freeze captured the reversal's own open and a cleared move, which
//     disabled the 30% continuation guard entirely  (fixed, see movOpenBar0/movRefBar0)
//   * the arm block wiped reBar, killing a re-entry that was still eligible
//     (fixed, see the `if not isRev` guard on reCount/reBar)
// Both consequences now land on the same bar, by design. Anything that reads print
// state after a reversal must take it from a bar-start snapshot, not live.
//
// `revCoreNow` is the identical magnitude/direction/cooldown test with only the
// arming-specific terms dropped: the badge budget (revCount/revMaxCount) and the
// reversal-vs-reversal escalation gate, both of which are about how many badges may
// be DRAWN, not about whether this bar is a reversal. So there is one detector, and
// the two consequences differ exactly as the owner specified - flat, it arms; in a trade,
// it exits AND arms on that same candle.
revCoreNow = rawRev and revCandidate and revCoolOk and (liveMode or barstate.isconfirmed)

// An earnings print is anything that is NOT a reversal candidate, still subject
// to the escalation size test in both directions.
// A non-superseding opposite bar is a REVERSAL candidate and is handled by the
// reversal path, so the two stay mutually exclusive without needing a term here:
// a superseding bar is never a revCandidate, and escBase is exactly "supersedes".
trigPrint = raw and coolOk and dayOk and escBase and
     not busyWhileOpen and (liveMode or barstate.isconfirmed)

trigger = trigPrint or trigRev
// The old rule let the PRINT win every tie. Since v6.0 removed the `not supersedes`
// veto a bar CAN satisfy both, and when it did the print won and took its direction
// from `isUp`, which compares (high - close[3]) against (close[3] - low). On the exact
// shape the reversal path exists to catch - opens at the spike top, closes on its low
// - that reports UP. A green up-print painted on a violently red candle, with the
// confirm path then hunting a new HIGH in a collapsing tape.
revBeatsPrint = not na(impBar) and impDir != 0 and revAgainst
isRev   = trigRev and (not trigPrint or revBeatsPrint)

// Direction of the SIGNAL BEING DRAWN, as opposed to the raw bar classification.
// A print keeps the extreme-based reading. A reversal uses the close-based one, so
// the badge lands on the correct side of the candle and impDir/impE/impOpp/impRng
// are anchored to the direction the reversal is actually trading.
// A reversal is by definition opposite to the print, so its direction is simply
// the print's inverted. No reference-price comparison is involved any more.
sigUp = isRev ? (impDir < 0) : isUp


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// RENDER
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

f_num(x, p) => str.tostring(x, p)

if trigger
    lastFireBar := bar_index
    lastFireBlock := sessStartMs
    // v4.1: ONLY A PRINT MOVES THE ESCALATION BASELINE.
    //
    // This used to update on every trigger, reversals included, and that is the
    // second half of the ASTS 2026-08-10 mess. A false pre-earnings print fired at
    // 16:05, a fade fired off it, and the fade - being small - OVERWROTE the bar
    // that the next signal had to clear. The real 16:29 release was then measured
    // against a wiggle instead of against the print, so the escalation gate lost
    // its meaning exactly when it mattered.
    //
    // The baseline is a statement about the biggest EVENT seen this session. A fade
    // is not an event of that kind, so it does not get to move it.
    if not isRev
        lastSigDir := thisDir
        lastSigVol := volume
        lastSigExc := excPct

    // v3.12 bookkeeping for the two signal classes. A reversal counts and scores
    // against the previous REVERSAL. A genuine new print resets the reversal
    // counter, because the fades that followed the old print no longer apply to a
    // new release.
    if isRev
        revCount   := revCount + 1
        lastRevVol := volume
        lastRevExc := nz(revGiveX, 0)   // giveback, not excursion - see revImpGive
    else
        revCount   := 0
        lastRevVol := na
        lastRevExc := na

    col = isRev ? colRev : (sigUp ? colUp : colDn)

    tip = (isRev ? "REVERSAL  " : "EARNINGS PRINT  ") + (sigUp ? "UP" : "DOWN") + "\n" +
     (isRev ? "PATH " + (revGiveOk1 and revGiveOk2 ? "1+2" : revGiveOk1 and revGiveOk3 ? "1+3" : revGiveOk2 and revGiveOk3 ? "2+3" : revGiveOk1 ? "1 single candle vs print body" : revGiveOk2 ? "2 two candles combined" : revGiveOk3 ? "3 absolute size vs a normal minute" : "?") + "   |   p1 body/printBody " + str.tostring(nz(revGiveX, 0), "#.##") + " need " + str.tostring(revImpGive, "#.##") + "   |   p2 pair sum " + str.tostring(nz(revGiveX, 0) + nz(revPrevGiveX, 0), "#.##") + " need " + str.tostring(revPairSum, "#.##") + "   |   p3 body/normalMin " + str.tostring(nz(revSelfX, 0), "#.#") + " need " + str.tostring(revSelfMult, "#.#") + "   |   closePos " + str.tostring(nz(revBarClosePos, 1), "#.##") + " need <= " + str.tostring(revPairClose, "#.##") + "   ---   against the " + (impDir > 0 ? "UP" : "DOWN") + " earnings print — the release is already out,\nso this is something else driving price the other way.\n" : "") +
     syminfo.ticker + "  " + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) + "\n" +
     "+" + str.tostring(intoMin) + " min into " + (inPre ? "pre-market" : "post-market") + "\n" +
     "-----------------------\n" +
     (burstOk and not rngOk ? "triggered by the 3-BAR BURST route\n" : "") +
     "RVOL          " + f_num(rvol, "#.#")     + "x   (need " + f_num(eRvol, "#.#") + ")\n" +
     "Range / ATR   " + f_num(rangeAtr, "#.#")  + "x   (need " + f_num(eRng, "#.#")  + ")\n" +
     "Excursion     " + f_num(excPct, "#.##")   + "%   (need " + f_num(eMove, "#.##") + ")\n" +
     "vs RTH minute " + f_num(advRatio, "#.##") + "x" + (eAbsOn ? "   (need " + f_num(eAdv, "#.##") + ")" : "   (gate off)") + "\n" +
     "3-bar burst   " + f_num(burst3, "#.##") + "x vol, " + f_num(move3, "#.##") + "% move\n" +
     "-----------------------\n" +
     "Conviction    " + str.tostring(score) + " / 100"

    // The reversal badge is BLUE and its arrow is COLOURED; a print keeps its
    // red/green triangle on a red/green badge. So the badge colour carries the
    // CLASS of event and the glyph colour carries the DIRECTION, and the two can
    // never collide.
    //
    // `sigUp` is the direction of THIS bar, so on a reversal it is the direction of
    // the fade rather than of the print being faded. A reversal that turns the move
    // down therefore draws the down glyph in the down colour.
    string revMark = sigUp ? revGlyphUp : revGlyphDn

    label.new(
     bar_index, sigUp ? low : high,
     isRev ? revMark : (sigUp ? "▲" : "▼"),
     yloc      = sigUp ? yloc.belowbar : yloc.abovebar,
     style     = sigUp ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down,
     color     = col,
     // v4.3: the reversal ARROW is red when the fade turns the move bearish and
     // green when it turns it bullish. On a print the glyph stays white, because
     // that badge is already red or green itself.
     textcolor = isRev ? (sigUp ? colUp : colDn) : color.white,
     // v4.5: the reversal badge gets its own size, because it is the only marker
     // whose colour lives in the glyph rather than in the badge behind it.
     size      = isRev ? revSize : size.normal,
     tooltip   = tip)

    if showLevels
        float ext  = sigUp ? high : low
        float f50  = ext + (refPx - ext) * 0.5
        float f618 = ext + (refPx - ext) * 0.618
        line.new(bar_index, f50,  bar_index + levelLen, f50,  color=color.new(col, 40), style=line.style_dashed, width=1)
        line.new(bar_index, f618, bar_index + levelLen, f618, color=color.new(col, 65), style=line.style_dotted, width=1)

    alert(
     (isRev ? "REVERSAL " : "EARNINGS PRINT ") + (sigUp ? "UP" : "DOWN") + " - " + syminfo.ticker +
     " @ " + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) +
     " | RVOL " + f_num(rvol, "#.#") + "x" +
     " | range " + f_num(rangeAtr, "#.#") + "x ATR" +
     " | move " + f_num(excPct, "#.##") + "%" +
     " | +" + str.tostring(intoMin) + "m into session" +
     " | conviction " + str.tostring(score) + "/100",
     alert.freq_once_per_bar)

alertcondition(trigger, "Earnings print detected", "Earnings print detected on {{ticker}} at {{close}}")

// v1.9: the duplicate plotshape arrows are gone. Every signal was drawing twice -
// once as a plotshape triangle and once as the label above it.
//
// The label is kept as the single marker rather than the plotshape, because the
// label is what carries the hover tooltip and what can be coloured per element.
// The garbage-collection worry that motivated plotshape in v1.7 turned out to be
// unfounded: measured across the full loaded history, this fires 4-6 times per
// symbol, against a 500-label cap. Two orders of magnitude of headroom.


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// PER-BAR DIAGNOSTICS
//
// The status panel only ever describes the LAST bar, which is useless when the
// question is "why was there no arrow on THAT candle 20 minutes ago". These plot
// into the Data Window (Alt+D on the chart) - hover any historical bar and read
// the exact numbers the gates saw on it, against the thresholds in force.
//
// display.data_window keeps them out of the chart pane and off the price scale.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

plot(rvol,       "· RVOL",            display=display.data_window)
plot(eRvol,      "· RVOL needed",     display=display.data_window)
plot(rangeAtr,   "· Range/ATR",       display=display.data_window)
plot(eRng,       "· Range/ATR needed", display=display.data_window)
plot(excPct,     "· Excursion %",     display=display.data_window)
plot(eMove,      "· Excursion needed", display=display.data_window)
plot(advRatio,   "· vs RTH minute",   display=display.data_window)
plot(eAdv,       "· vs RTH needed",   display=display.data_window)
plot(burst3,     "· 3bar vol x",      display=display.data_window)
plot(move3,      "· 3bar move %",     display=display.data_window)
plot(armed   ? 1 : 0, "· armed (1=yes)",     display=display.data_window)
plot(gatesOk ? 1 : 0, "· gates pass (1=yes)", display=display.data_window)
plot(trigger ? 1 : 0, "· FIRED (1=yes)",      display=display.data_window)
plot(bucket,     "· bucket 0-3",      display=display.data_window)
plot(baseN,      "· baseline samples", display=display.data_window)


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// CONTINUATION CONFIRM
//
// The print marker says "the release just hit". It does NOT say "this move
// continues" - measured across [n] prints, the print candle's extreme was taken
// out [n]% of the time, but the session still tended to close BELOW that extreme.
// The move overshoots, extends a bit further, then hands much of it back.
//
// So this second marker fires only when the move has proven two things at a bar
// CLOSE: it made a new extreme beyond the print candle, and it is still holding
// most of the impulse. That pair measured [n]% win / [n]R where entering
// immediately measured [n]% / [n]R.
//
// Evaluated on confirmed bars only - deliberately unlike the print marker. The
// giveback test uses the close, which can move both ways inside a bar, so a live
// version would flicker and would not match what was backtested.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// impBar / impDir and the rest of the print state are declared far above, next to
// trDir, because the v3.12 reversal test reads them and Pine has no forward
// references. See the note there.

// Consolidation tracking, for the ADD marker. v3.3 keeps NO running state for
// the pause itself - it is a rolling window recomputed every bar (see the ADD
// block). The only thing remembered is the bar of the last +, which enforces
// spacing between two of them.
// v3.5 pullback tracker for the + marker. Deliberately NOT cleared when a trade
// exits, so a re-entry breakout after a stop-out still has a peak to measure
// from. Cleared only by a fresh print.
var float addPeakPx  = na   // best price since the confirm, in the trade's direction
var int   addPeakBar = na
var float addPbExt   = na   // extreme against the trade since that peak
var int   addCount   = 0    // badges fired for this trade. v4.6: NOT a cap any more
// ── v4.2: AVERAGE ENTRY ONCE THE + HAS FIRED ───────────────────────────────────
// The + says "here is where you add". Nothing on the chart then told you what your
// position actually costs after you did, so every add left you doing the arithmetic
// in your head mid-trade - exactly when you have the least attention to spare.
//
// These record the fill price of each + so the panel can quote a blended entry.
// EQUAL SIZE per fill is assumed, because the script has no idea what size you
// traded; the panel says so on the row itself rather than letting the number imply
// a precision it does not have. Cleared with addCount, and again at every confirm,
// so an average can never outlive the entry it is blended with.
var float addSumPx  = 0.0   // sum of the fill prices of every + so far
var int   addFills  = 0     // how many of them there are
var float addFirstPx = na   // the FIRST +, kept separately for the row's tooltip
var float trPeak    = 0.0   // best favourable excursion so far, for the BE stop
var bool  beMoved   = false

// Plain (non-var) flags so the confirm marker can also be a plotshape rather than
// a garbage-collectable label. Reset to false every bar, set true on the bar that
// confirms.
bool confirmNow = false

// ─── LIVE TRADE STATE, opened by a confirm ─────────────────────────────────────
// An earlier comment here carried a table of R-expectancies that compared
// several exit rules. Those numbers came from the
// consolidated-tape feed, not from TradingView bars, so they described different
// trades than this script fires. Removed rather than repeated. See the provenance
// note at the top of the file.
var float trEntry  = na
var float trStop   = na
// ── v10.0 TAKE-PROFIT LADDER ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Measured on the v9.0 run. The old ladder was 1R / 2R / 3R.
//   T1 [n]R - [n] trades reached no target at all. BABA, TGT
//              and TJX each peaked between [n]R and [n]R and then went to a
//              FULL stop. A first target here banks a slice before they turn.
//   T2 [n]R, T3 [n]R - pulled in with T1 so the spacing stays even.
//   T4 [n]R - NEW. [n] of the [n] reached +4R or better, so the top of the ladder
//              had real population running past it with nothing left to sell into.
// Constants rather than inputs on purpose: four new inputs would shift every
// stored input index below them and reshuffle the settings on a live chart.
float TP1R = 0.60
float TP2R = 1.40
float TP3R = 2.40
float TP4R = 4.00

var float trTarget = na          // TP1
var float trTarget2 = na         // TP2
var float trTarget3 = na         // TP3
var float trTarget4 = na         // TP4 - v10.0
var int   trBar    = na
var bool  trTp1Done = false
var bool  trTp4Done = false
var bool  trTp2Done = false
var bool  trTp3Done = false
var line  lnStop = na
var line  lnTp1  = na
var line  lnTp2  = na
var line  lnTp3  = na
var line  lnTp4  = na
var int   lnSess = na       // session block the current level set belongs to
// Latches true once regular hours are reached AFTER the signal. The levels stop the
// moment regular hours then end - see the v4.1 note at the extension block.

// ─── TRAIL STATE (v3.4) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// trRisk0 is the ORIGINAL entry-to-stop distance, frozen at the confirm. Every R
// measurement in the trail must divide by THIS and never by the live stop
// distance: once the stop starts moving, the live distance shrinks toward zero,
// so a live-risk denominator makes trPeak/risk explode and the ladder fire every
// step at once. v3.3 read the live distance and was only saved from that by the
// beMoved flag letting the block run exactly once.
var float trRisk0    = na
var int   lockedStep = 0    // highest whole-R milestone the ladder has locked
var bool  trArmed    = false
var float trailHold  = na   // structural level held over a consolidation freeze
// Whether the trail was frozen on the last confirmed bar. Kept as state rather
// than recomputed in the status table, because the consolidation window is
// defined below the table and Pine has no forward references.
var bool  trFrozen   = false
// The last CONFIRMED trade's direction, deliberately NOT cleared when the trade
// exits. The + reads this so a re-entry breakout after a stop-out still knows
// which way the earnings move went. Cleared only by a fresh print.
var int   trDirLast  = 0

// v5.8 - has this trade traded through a REGULAR session yet? Latches while the
// position is open, resets when a new one opens. See the session-end close below.
var bool  trSawRth   = false


// v9.1 - CRITICAL FIX. The re-entry freeze in the exit block far below must capture the
// move belonging to the trade being EXITED. A reversal fires earlier in this same bar and
// overwrites movOpen and clears movRef, and Pine runs top to bottom, so the freeze was
// reading the reversal candle's open and a na move. na(reArmMove) is an OR term in the
// continuation gate, so the 30% "is the original move still standing" guard - the AAOI
// protection - short-circuited to true and never ran on any reversal exit. Snapshot both
// values as they stand at the START of the bar and let the freeze read these instead.
float movOpenBar0 = movOpen
float movRefBar0  = movRef

if trigger
    impBar  := bar_index
    // v8.1 - a print or a reversal starts a fresh move; a re-entry does not.
    movOpen := open
    // v9.0 - only a genuine PRINT starts a new episode. A reversal trades the same
    // release from the other side, so it inherits the origin rather than resetting it.
    if not isRev or na(epiOpen)
        epiOpen := open
    movBest := close
    movRef  := na
    vetoMarked := false
    impA    := nz(close[1], close)
    impE    := sigUp ? high : low
    impDir  := sigUp ? 1 : -1
    impDone := false
    impConf := false
    impOpp  := sigUp ? low : high
    impRng  := high - low
    impBody := math.abs(close - open)
    // v9.1 - MAJOR FIX. These ran for prints AND reversals, because trigger is either. A
    // reversal firing while flat, inside an earlier stop-out's 12-bar re-entry window, set reBar
    // to na and killed a re-entry that was still eligible - and reCount := 0 refilled a budget
    // the reversal had nothing to do with. v8.15 removed exactly this dependency from the EXIT
    // block and left an identical one sitting here in the ARM block. Only a genuine PRINT is a
    // new episode.
    if not isRev
        reCount := 0
        reBar   := na
    // ── WHAT A NEW PRINT MAY AND MAY NOT RESET ─────────────────────────────────
    // Prints can fire mid-trade: the escalation gate deliberately lets a FLIP
    // through as a reversal warning while a position is open. CRWV 2026-08-11 has
    // exactly that - a bearish print around 16:20 inside a live long.
    //
    // v3.5/v3.6 reset the whole ADD anchor here unconditionally, so that counter
    // print wiped addPeakPx / addPbExt / addCount for a trade that was still open.
    // The pullback the + needs was erased, the peak re-tracked from a lower point,
    // and the badge that should have appeared at 16:22 never did. The trailing
    // stop kept working, which is why it looked like the + alone was broken.
    //
    // The add anchor belongs to the TRADE, not to the print. Only clear it when no
    // trade is open; a live position keeps the anchor it has been building.
    // The same argument applies to the TRAIL state. Zeroing trPeak and lockedStep
    // mid-trade makes the ladder forget how far the move has already come, so it
    // has to re-earn steps it already earned. The ratchet stops that from ever
    // loosening the stop, which is why it was survivable, but it silently delays
    // every later step and there is no reason to accept it.
    //
    // Only the print's OWN fields above are unconditional. Everything that
    // describes a position is preserved while that position is open.
    if trDir == 0
        addPeakPx  := na
        addPeakBar := na
        addPbExt   := na
        addCount   := 0
        addSumPx   := 0.0
        addFills   := 0
        addFirstPx := na
        trDirLast  := 0
        // v6.2 - a fresh print cancels the pending post-trade reset timer. Without
        // this, a trade that ended 31 bars ago could fire the "forget everything"
        // block and wipe a print that had just been established, silently truncating
        // its reversal window.
        exitBar    := na
        trPeak     := 0.0
        beMoved    := false
        lockedStep := 0
        trArmed    := false
        trailHold  := na
        trFrozen   := false
    // DISARM invalidation on a new print. It re-arms only when that print earns
    // a confirm. Resetting it to false here was the bug that let an "!" fire off
    // the PREVIOUS trade's state before the new one had confirmed anything.
    invFired := true

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// RE-ENTRY AFTER A STOP-OUT (v5.4)
//
// Runs BEFORE the confirm block on purpose. A stop-out is registered far below
// this point in the script, so by the time we get here `reBar` always refers to a
// PREVIOUS bar - which is what we want: never re-enter on the same bar that
// stopped us out.
//
// It re-arms the print rather than opening a position itself. Clearing impDone /
// impConf and re-anchoring impBar to this bar puts the ordinary confirm path back
// in play, so the re-entry gets a normal structural stop, normal T1/T2/T3 and the
// normal trail. Nothing about the stop or the trail is modified anywhere - that
// machinery works and is left alone.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bool  reNow   = false
// v6.5 - hoisted so the marker's tooltip below can quote what it measured.
float reBodyX   = na
float reRngX    = na
float reBodyRng = na
if useReentry and barstate.isconfirmed and trDir == 0 and not na(reBar) and impDir != 0
    if reCount < reMaxCount and bar_index > reBar and (bar_index - reBar) <= reWindowBars
        // v6.1 - THE RE-ENTRY IS ABOUT RECLAIMING THE STOP LEVEL, WITH CONVICTION.
        //
        // It must not fire when the stop is hit and a small consolidation candle then
        // crosses the stop level again. That is fluctuation and shows no conviction
        // back to the original direction. Firing there was
        // the SPCE failure.
        //
        // So the test is now (a) the CLOSE - not a wick - back through the STOP LEVEL
        // itself, and (b) magnitude measured from that level. Beating the stop-out
        // bar's extreme by a tick, which is what v5.4-v6.0 asked for, is nearly free.
        //
        // And the shape it is meant to catch: the original earnings move, then a
        // pullback that is deep but is NOT reversal-grade, which clips a stop
        // that was simply a bit too tight - and then buyers (or sellers) absorb
        // it and drive straight back through that level. "Buyers are aggressive
        // and they're defending support." That re-validates the original
        // hypothesis, so the print is re-armed.
        //
        // THE MARKER IS NOT AN ENTRY. It clears impDone/impConf and re-anchors the print; an
        // ordinary confirm must still fire afterwards before any position exists.
        //
        // 1. RECLAIM - the CLOSE, not a wick, back through the stop level.
        // v8.14 - measured against the STOPPED TRADE's direction, not the live impDir.
        int   reD   = reArmDir != 0 ? reArmDir : impDir
        bool  reExt = na(reStop) or reD == 0 ? false : (reD > 0 ? close > reStop : close < reStop)
        float reRng = math.max(high - low, syminfo.mintick)
        // v8.15 - reD, not impDir. A reversal flips impDir on its way out, so this
        // asked whether the candle closed strongly in the REVERSAL's direction.
        float reLoc = reD > 0 ? (close - low) / reRng : (high - close) / reRng
        // 2. DIRECTION - the candle itself must push the original way. A green
        //    bar for a long, a red bar for a short. A doji that drifts across the
        //    level is not buyers defending anything.
        bool  reDirOk = reD > 0 ? close > open : close < open
        // 3. MAGNITUDE OF THE CANDLE ITSELF - not how far past the stop it
        //    closed, which is what v6.1 measured and why NBIS 2026-08-12 07:48
        //    was rejected. Two readings, EITHER of which qualifies, because the
        //    print body is not a fair yardstick on every re-entry.
        reBodyX := na(impBody) or impBody <= 0 ? 0.0 : math.abs(close - open) / impBody
        reRngX  := na(atrSes) or atrSes <= 0   ? 0.0 : reRng / atrSes
        bool  reBig = reBodyX >= reMinBody or reRngX >= reMinRangeAtr
        reBodyRng := math.abs(close - open) / reRng
        bool  reDecisive = reBodyRng >= reMinBodyRng
        // v8.1c - HOW MUCH OF THE ORIGINAL MOVE IS STILL STANDING.
        //
        // A re-entry is a CONTINUATION of the move that produced the print. If the move is
        // entirely gone, there is nothing to continue.
        //
        // AAOI 2026-08-06 is the case: it tried to re-enter LONG at a price BELOW where
        // the whole bullish move began, having handed back more than all of it. NBIS
        // 2026-08-12 is the counter-case: it gave back more than a typical pullback but
        // price was still well above where the move started, so the move was still
        // there to continue.
        //
        // Measured from the print candle's OPEN to the confirmation candle's CLOSE -
        // the same whole-move yardstick everything else in v8.1 uses. Before a
        // confirmation exists there is no yardstick, so the test stands aside.
        // v8.15 - THE CONTINUATION TEST WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE WRONG EPISODE, AND IN
        // THE WRONG DIRECTION. It used movOpen/movRef/impDir, all three of which a
        // reversal rewrites on its way out. So after a reversal it asked "has this candle
        // retained 30% of the REVERSAL's move, in the REVERSAL's direction" - which is
        // not a question about the trade being re-entered at all, and a candle that had
        // given back the entire original move could score positive and pass.
        //
        // reArmOpen and reArmMove are frozen at the exit of the trade being re-entered,
        // so this now asks the only question that matters: does this candle still hold
        // reKeepFrac of THAT trade's move, in THAT trade's direction.
        float reHold = na(reArmOpen) ? na : (close - reArmOpen) * reD
        bool  reContOk = reKeepFrac <= 0 or na(reArmMove) or reArmMove <= 0 or na(reHold) or
         reHold >= reKeepFrac * reArmMove
        if reContOk and reExt and reDirOk and reBig and reDecisive and reLoc >= (1.0 - reCloseFrac) and
         rvol >= reMinRvol
            impBar  := bar_index
            // v8.15 - AND THE DIRECTION. This block armed impBar/impE/impOpp and never touched
            // impDir, so after a reversal had flipped impDir the re-entry armed everything
            // EXCEPT the direction, and the confirmation then hunted a confirm the OTHER way.
            //
            // Each of the three now arms impDir explicitly and independently:
            //   print     -> its own direction
            //   reversal  -> the opposite of the print it fades
            //   re-entry  -> the direction of the trade that was stopped out
            impDir  := reD
            impA    := nz(close[1], close)
            // v6.6 - THE CONFIRM REFERENCE IS THIS BAR'S CLOSE, NOT ITS WICK.
            //
            // A re-entry candle is BY CONSTRUCTION a large bar - that is what the
            // gates above just tested for. Anchoring the confirm's new-extreme
            // test to its HIGH therefore demands that the next few bars beat an
            // extreme set by an unusually big candle, which is the same "too big
            // to follow" trap that blocked this signal in the first place.
            //
            // MEASURED ON NBIS 2026-08-12. Re-entry 11:48Z, high 217.60, close
            // 216.69. Against the HIGH: 11:51 closes beautifully (0.95 of its
            // range) but never makes a new extreme - 217.45, short by 0.15 - while
            // 11:52 and 11:53 DO make new extremes and then close at 0.567 and
            // 0.548 against a 0.60 gate. Three candidates, all blocked by margins
            // of 0.03 to 0.15, and no trade is ever re-entered.
            //
            // The close is the price the market actually accepted; the high is a
            // wick that was already rejected inside that same minute. Only the
            // re-entry path is affected - the ordinary print path still anchors to
            // the print candle's extreme, which is correct there.
            impE    := close
            impOpp  := reD > 0 ? low : high
            impRng  := high - low
            impBody := math.abs(close - open)
            impDone := false
            impConf := false
            reCount := reCount + 1
            reBar   := na
            reNow   := true

if reNow
    label.new(
     bar_index, impDir > 0 ? low : high,
     "⟲",
     yloc      = impDir > 0 ? yloc.belowbar : yloc.abovebar,
     style     = impDir > 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down,
     color     = color.new(#9c27b0, 0),
     textcolor = color.white,
     size      = size.small,
     tooltip   = "RE-ENTRY ARMED  " + (impDir > 0 ? "LONG" : "SHORT") + "\n" +
     syminfo.ticker + "\n" +
     "-----------------------\n" +
     "The stop took us out and the original direction has re-asserted:\n" +
     "this candle CLOSED back through the stop level, pushing the original\n" +
     "way, big for its own tape, on volume, and finishing at the end that\n" +
     "shows the level being defended.\n\n" +
     "This is NOT an entry by itself. The print has been re-armed, so a\n" +
     "normal confirm must still fire before any position exists - with a\n" +
     "normal structural stop, normal targets and the normal trail.\n" +
     "-----------------------\n" +
     "RVOL " + str.tostring(rvol, "#.#") + "  (need " + str.tostring(reMinRvol, "#.#") + ")\n" +
     "candle body   " + str.tostring(reBodyX, "#.##") + " x print body   (need " + str.tostring(reMinBody, "#.##") + ")\n" +
     "candle range  " + str.tostring(reRngX, "#.##") + " x session ATR   (need " + str.tostring(reMinRangeAtr, "#.##") + ")\n" +
     "either size reading alone is enough\n" +
     "body / range  " + str.tostring(reBodyRng, "#.##") + "   (need " + str.tostring(reMinBodyRng, "#.##") + ", REQUIRED)\n" +
     "re-entries used " + str.tostring(reCount) + " of " + str.tostring(reMaxCount))
    alert("RE-ENTRY ARMED " + (impDir > 0 ? "LONG" : "SHORT") + " - " + syminfo.ticker, alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

if showConfirm and barstate.isconfirmed and not impDone and not na(impBar) and impDir != 0
    if bar_index > impBar and (bar_index - impBar) <= confirmWindow
        float impR = math.abs(impE - impA)
        bool  newExt = impDir > 0 ? high > impE : low < impE
        float favE = (impE - impA) * impDir
        float favC = (close - impA) * impDir
        float give = impR > 0 ? (favE - favC) / impR : 1.0
        // Where this bar closes inside its own range, in the trade's direction.
        // A poke to a new extreme that closes back down is a rejection.
        float cbRng = math.max(high - low, syminfo.mintick)
        float cbLoc = impDir > 0 ? (close - low) / cbRng : (high - close) / cbRng
        bool  closeOk = minConfClose <= 0.0 or cbLoc >= minConfClose
        // v8.1 - THE EXHAUSTION VETO. The move from the print candle's OPEN to this
        // close, as a percentage of price. At or above maxMovePct the release is spent
        // and the trade is refused. The owner's argument for a veto rather than merely a
        // tighter stop: a stop does not guarantee the fill - you are filled at the
        // candle close. LYFT realised [n]% against a stop implying [n]%.
        // v9.0 - MEASURED FROM THE EPISODE'S ORIGIN, NOT THE CURRENT ARM. Using movOpen
        // meant a reversal-driven trade measured its move from the REVERSAL candle's open,
        // which is a fraction of a percent, so the veto could never fire on one however
        // exhausted the underlying release already was. epiOpen is the open of the bar
        // that started the earnings episode and is untouched by reversals and re-entries.
        float vetoRef     = na(epiOpen) ? movOpen : epiOpen
        float vetoMovePct = na(vetoRef) or vetoRef <= 0 ? na : 100.0 * math.abs(close - vetoRef) / vetoRef
        bool  exhausted   = maxMovePct > 0 and not na(vetoMovePct) and vetoMovePct >= maxMovePct
        // v8.1c - the owner asked that a refused trade say so on the chart rather than
        // simply not appearing. Marked once per print episode, on the first bar that
        // would otherwise have confirmed.
        if impR > 0 and newExt and closeOk and give < maxGiveback / 100.0 and exhausted and not vetoMarked
            vetoMarked := true
            label.new(bar_index, impDir > 0 ? high : low, "%",
             yloc  = impDir > 0 ? yloc.abovebar : yloc.belowbar,
             style = impDir > 0 ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up,
             color = color.new(#78909c, 20), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny,
             tooltip = "TRADE REFUSED - THE MOVE IS ALREADY SPENT.\n\nThis bar met every condition to confirm, and was declined because the move from the print candle's open to this close is " + str.tostring(vetoMovePct, "#.#") + "% of price, against a limit of " + str.tostring(maxMovePct, "#.#") + "%.\n\nA stop does not protect you here: you are filled at the candle close, not at the stop level, so on a move this size the realised loss can be far larger than the stop implies. Refusing the trade is the only thing that removes that risk.\n\nRaise or zero 'Skip the trade once the move is this % of price' in the top settings group to take these.")

        if impR > 0 and newExt and closeOk and give < maxGiveback / 100.0 and not exhausted
            // Freeze the whole move at the confirmation that opened this trade.
            //
            // v8.9 - AND ACTUALLY FREEZE IT. This said "freeze" and then recomputed on
            // EVERY confirmation, including a re-entry's. movOpen still points at the
            // original print bar, so a re-entry that fires after price has retraced
            // overwrote the real move with the small distance left between the print open
            // and the re-entry close.
            //
            //   MRVL 2026-08-27. Print 20:05 open 241.05, first confirm 20:11 close
            //   232.87, so the move is 8.18 and the first trade got R = 5.73. The
            //   re-entry confirmed at 20:52 at 239.70, and movRef was rewritten to
            //   |239.70 - 241.05| = 1.35. The v8.8 earnings-move floor then computed
            //   0.60 x 1.35 = 0.81, lost the max() to a 0.95 structural term, and the
            //   re-entry went in on R = 0.95 - a sixth of the first trade's risk. T1 and
            //   T2 both filled on the next bar and the stop went one bar later.
            //
            // THIS CORRUPTED THREE THINGS AT ONCE, because movRef is the shared "whole
            // move" yardstick: the earnings-move stop floor, the 30% re-entry
            // continuation gate, and the reversal magnitude reference all measure against
            // it. All three were quietly reading a shrunken move on every re-entry.
            //
            // A genuinely new episode - a fresh print, or a reversal - clears movRef back
            // to na in the arm block, so freezing here does not stop a real new move from
            // taking its own measurement.
            if not na(movOpen) and na(movRef)
                movRef := math.abs(close - movOpen)
            impDone := true
            impConf := true
            confirmNow := true
            // STOP ANCHOR - the PRINT candle's opposite extreme, not this bar's.
            // The confirm bar's own extreme is a [n]%-of-price stop on a name
            // that just moved 6%; it gets taken out by noise within a minute or
            // two, which closes the trade before any target can be reached. That
            // is exactly why no take-profit marker ever appeared on a clean
            // trend. Anchoring to the print candle gives a [n]% stop, which is
            // also the placement that measured best ([n]R at a 3R target vs
            // [n]R for the tight one).
            // Stop sits a FRACTION of the way from entry back toward the print
            // candle's far side. 1.0 = the far side itself (v2.7, too wide -
            // a full round trip on a 6% earnings move). 0.5 measured identically
            // on win rate and R-expectancy while halving the cash at risk:
            //     0.35 -> [n]% win, [n]R, stop [n]% of price   (too tight)
            //     0.50 -> [n]% win, [n]R, stop [n]% of price   <- shipped
            //     1.00 -> [n]% win, [n]R, stop [n]% of price
            float farPx  = na(impOpp) ? (impDir > 0 ? low : high) : impOpp
            float stopPx = close + stopRetrace * (farPx - close)
            // v6.1 - R IS THE GREATER OF STRUCTURE AND LIVE VOLATILITY. See the
            // riskAtrMult tooltip. The ATR floor only binds where the print candle is
            // unrepresentative of the trade being taken - i.e. on re-entries.
            float riskStr = math.abs(close - stopPx)
            // v8.1 - THE FLOOR IS MEASURED ON THE BARS AROUND THE TRADE, NOT ON A CALM-DAY
            // AVERAGE. The right measure already existed - atrLive, a plain rolling true range
            // over the last atrTrailLen bars - and was wired to the trail but never to the stop.
            // HIMS 2026-08-10's re-entry got a 1.1%-of-price stop because of it. The ceiling
            // below is what keeps this honest, because atrLive is large immediately after a
            // print.
            float atrFloorSrc = na(atrLive) or atrLive <= 0 ? atrSes : atrLive
            float riskAtr = na(atrFloorSrc) or atrFloorSrc <= 0 ? 0.0 : riskAtrMult * atrFloorSrc
            // v8.8 - AND THE EARNINGS MOVE ITSELF, WHICH WAS MISSING ENTIRELY. Only two of those
            // three were ever in the max.
            //
            // It matters on RE-ENTRIES, and only there. `riskStr` is measured against
            // `impOpp`, and a re-entry deliberately re-anchors impOpp to the RE-ENTRY
            // CANDLE'S own extreme - a single ordinary bar. So the structural term collapses
            // to a fraction of one candle, and if recent volatility is also quiet the ATR
            // floor cannot rescue it. MRVL and INFQ 2026-08-27 both show the result: a
            // re-entry whose stop and first target sit so close together that one moderate
            // red candle takes out both.
            //
            // `movRef` is the whole move - the open of the bar that armed the direction
            // through to that episode's confirmation close - and it is NOT reset by a
            // re-entry, so it still describes the earnings move the re-entry is rejoining.
            // Scaled by the same stopRetrace the original stop uses, so a re-entry risks
            // what the first entry risked rather than a fraction of one bar.
            //
            // On a FIRST entry this term cannot bind: riskStr is measured from the print
            // candle's far extreme and movRef from its open, and the extreme is always at
            // least as far away, so riskStr >= riskMove by construction. Nothing that works
            // today changes.
            float riskMove = na(movRef) or movRef <= 0 ? 0.0 : stopRetrace * movRef
            float riskPx  = math.max(math.max(riskStr, riskAtr), riskMove)
            // v8.1 - CEILING ON THE FINAL RISK, as a fraction of the whole move. AAOI's stop sat
            // where price had been BEFORE the earnings move, which is why its re-entry could
            // fire at all - fix the stop and that re-entry never arises. It bounds the FINAL
            // risk, after the floor, or the floor change above would hand back an unbounded stop
            // on the next thin re-entry. v8.10 - THE CAP IS MEASURED AGAINST THE FROZEN MOVE,
            // NOT THE CURRENT DISTANCE FROM THE PRINT OPEN. This used `movNow` - how far THIS
            // bar's close happens to sit from the print candle's open - which on a first entry
            // is the move, and on a RE-ENTRY is whatever is left of it after the retracement.
            //
            //   MRVL 2026-08-27, the full chain, and every number here was read off the
            //   live chart:
            //        riskStr, from the re-entry candle ............ 0.95
            //        riskMove, 0.60 x the real move 8.18 .......... 4.91
            //        max(...) ..................................... 4.91   correct
            //        cap, 0.70 x movNow 1.35 ...................... 0.945  CRUSHES IT
            //        R actually taken ............................. 0.95
            //   The v8.8 floor was computing the right number and the cap was throwing it
            //   away on the very next line. T1 and T2 then both filled on the following
            //   bar and the stop went one bar after that.
            //
            // `movRef` is the whole move, frozen at the confirmation that opened the
            // episode and untouched by a re-entry. Capping against it gives 0.70 x 8.18 =
            // 5.73 - which is exactly the R the FIRST entry took, so the cap and the floor
            // now agree with each other instead of fighting.
            //
            // Falls back to movNow when movRef does not exist yet, which is the pre-first-
            // confirmation case, so first entries behave exactly as before.
            float movNow = na(movOpen) ? na : math.abs(close - movOpen)
            float capRef = na(movRef) or movRef <= 0 ? movNow : movRef
            if stopCapFrac > 0 and not na(capRef) and capRef > 0
                riskPx := math.min(riskPx, stopCapFrac * capRef)
            // v8.11 - AND THE VOLATILITY FLOOR WINS, ALWAYS. THIS IS THE CLASS FIX.
            //
            // v8.10 fixed the cap's yardstick and that fixed the RE-ENTRY case on MRVL.
            // It did not fix the class, because the same crush reappears through the
            // REVERSAL path: a reversal resets movOpen/movBest/movRef in the arm block, so
            // its own confirmation measures a move only as long as the reversal candle
            // itself, and the cap computed off that is tiny again.
            //
            //   INFQ 2026-08-12. Reversal 20:10 open 12.71, confirm 20:11 close 12.53, so
            //   the "move" is 0.18 and the cap is 0.70 x 0.18 = 0.126. R taken was 0.13.
            //   All three targets sat inside a single candle's range and T1, T2 and T3 all
            //   printed on one bar.
            //
            // The mistake was structural, not per-symbol: a CAP was allowed to override a
            // FLOOR. riskAtr is not a preference, it is the size of the noise in the tape
            // right now. A stop placed inside that band is not a risk decision, it is a
            // guarantee of being hit by ordinary jitter - which is exactly what both
            // symbols show. Re-asserting it after the cap makes the ordering explicit:
            //
            //     structure, earnings move, volatility  ->  take the largest
            //     cap against the whole move            ->  trim an absurd stop
            //     volatility                            ->  but never below the noise
            //
            // A cap that can undercut the ATR term breaks that rule no matter which path opened
            // the trade, so the fix belongs here rather than in any one path.
            riskPx := math.max(riskPx, riskAtr)
            // Re-derive the stop from the FINAL risk so stop and targets always agree.
            stopPx := close - riskPx * impDir
            float tgtPx  = close + riskPx * impDir
            if riskPx > 0
                trDir     := impDir
                trDirLast := impDir
                trBarTime := time
                trEntry   := close
                trStop    := stopPx
                trTarget  := close + riskPx * TP1R * impDir
                trTarget2 := close + riskPx * TP2R * impDir
                trTarget3 := close + riskPx * TP3R * impDir
                trTarget4 := close + riskPx * TP4R * impDir
                trBar     := bar_index
                trTp1Done := false
                trTp4Done := false
                trTp2Done := false
                trTp3Done := false
                // Freeze the R denominator here, once, for the life of the trade.
                trRisk0    := riskPx
                // v6.2 - trPeak BELONGS TO THIS TRADE. It was previously reset ONLY
                // inside `if trigger` -> `if trDir == 0`, and the RE-ENTRY path
                // deliberately bypasses `trigger` (it re-arms the print directly and
                // lets this confirm open the position). So a re-entry inherited the
                // PREVIOUS trade's peak favourable excursion, in price units, and
                // divided it by a NEW and much smaller trRisk0.
                //
                // The result was a fabricated instant win: trArmed fired on bar one,
                // the ladder jumped several steps, the stop ratcheted ABOVE price, and
                // the next bar "stopped out" booking a green multi-R that never
                // happened. It also inflated peakR, so useExhaustExit could never fire
                // on a re-entry either. Every other trade-scoped variable is reset
                // here; this one was simply missed.
                trPeak     := 0.0
                // v4.2: a fresh entry means any blended average from the previous
                // trade is meaningless. Cleared here as well as on a new print,
                // because the average is quoted against THIS trEntry.
                addSumPx   := 0.0
                addFills   := 0
                addFirstPx := na
                lockedStep := 0
                trArmed    := false
                trailHold  := na
                trFrozen   := false
                beMoved    := false
                invFired  := false     // re-arm: this trade may now be invalidated
                // Previous trade's lines are left exactly as they are: still
                // extending right, forever. Two earlier attempts at being clever
                // here were both wrong - deleting them lost the older trade's
                // stop entirely, and freezing them at the exit bar produced the
                // short staggered stubs. Every level stays full width.
                if showTradeLines
                    // extend.none + an x2 that is pushed forward every bar of the
                    // SAME session. The levels therefore span the whole extended
                    // session they belong to and stop at its end, instead of
                    // running across the following days.
                    lnSess := sessStartMs
                    // Fresh level set, so the regular-hours latch resets with it.
                    lnStop := line.new(bar_index, stopPx, bar_index + 1, stopPx,
                     color=colStop2, style=line.style_dashed, width=2)
                    lnTp1 := line.new(bar_index, trTarget, bar_index + 1, trTarget,
                     color=colTp, style=line.style_dotted, width=1)
                    lnTp2 := line.new(bar_index, trTarget2, bar_index + 1, trTarget2,
                     color=colTp, style=line.style_dotted, width=1)
                    // T3 was drawn at 45% transparency while T1 and T2 were solid,
                    // which is why the third line looked like a different object.
                    // All three target lines are now identical.
                    lnTp3 := line.new(bar_index, trTarget3, bar_index + 1, trTarget3,
                     color=colTp, style=line.style_dotted, width=1)
                    lnTp4 := line.new(bar_index, trTarget4, bar_index + 1, trTarget4,
                     color=colTp, style=line.style_dotted, width=1)
            label.new(
             bar_index, impDir > 0 ? high : low,
             "✓",
             yloc      = impDir > 0 ? yloc.abovebar : yloc.belowbar,
             style     = impDir > 0 ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up,
             color     = colConf,
             textcolor = impDir > 0 ? colUp : colDn,
             size      = size.small,
             tooltip   = "CONTINUATION CONFIRMED  " + (impDir > 0 ? "LONG" : "SHORT") + "\n" +
             syminfo.ticker + "\n" +
             "-----------------------\n" +
             "new extreme beyond the print bar: yes\n" +
             "impulse retained: " + str.tostring(100.0 * (1.0 - give), "#.#") + "%\n" +
             "-----------------------\n" +
             "entry (this close)  " + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) + "\n" +
             "structural stop     " + str.tostring(stopPx, format.mintick) + "\n" +
             "1R target           " + str.tostring(tgtPx, format.mintick) + "\n" +
             "risk                " + str.tostring(100.0 * riskPx / close, "#.##") + "% of price\n" +
             "-----------------------\n" +
             "Base rate for this setup: [n]% win, [n]R (n=[n]).\n" +
             "Small sample.")
            alert("CONTINUATION CONFIRMED " + (impDir > 0 ? "LONG" : "SHORT") + " - " + syminfo.ticker +
             " @ " + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) +
             " | stop " + str.tostring(stopPx, format.mintick) +
             " | 1R target " + str.tostring(tgtPx, format.mintick) +
             " | risk " + str.tostring(100.0 * riskPx / close, "#.##") + "%",
             alert.freq_once_per_bar)
    else if (bar_index - impBar) > confirmWindow
        impDone := true


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// LIVE DIAGNOSTICS PANEL
//
// ─── v4.3: TWO BLOCKS SIDE BY SIDE ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// v4.2 stacked everything in one 3-wide, 15-tall column, which made a stripe down
// the side of the chart deep enough to cover price. The content splits cleanly in
// two - DETECTION (the gates, and whether anything has cleared them) and POSITION
// (what an open trade is doing) - and those two halves are read at different
// times, so they sit next to each other instead of one under the other.
//
// 7 columns x 9 rows: three for detection, a thin spacer, three for the position.
// Half the height, and the two banner rows span the full width across the top so
// the stage is still the first thing the eye lands on.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

f_pos() =>
    tblPos == "Top left"      ? position.top_left :
     tblPos == "Bottom right" ? position.bottom_right :
     tblPos == "Bottom left"  ? position.bottom_left :
     tblPos == "Middle right" ? position.middle_right : position.top_right

// Text sizes. Every step is one notch smaller than v4.2 shipped with - that panel
// was legible but heavy, and shrinking it is most of what "make it smaller" means.
txBody = tblSize == "Small" ? size.tiny  : tblSize == "Medium" ? size.tiny   :
 tblSize == "Large"         ? size.small : size.normal
txHead = tblSize == "Small" ? size.small : tblSize == "Medium" ? size.normal :
 tblSize == "Large"         ? size.normal : size.large
txNote = tblSize == "Small" ? size.tiny  : tblSize == "Medium" ? size.tiny   :
 tblSize == "Large"         ? size.tiny  : size.small

// Column widths as a percent of chart width. 0 = size to content, which is what
// Small keeps. Fixed widths on the larger sizes stop the panel jittering sideways
// every time a price gains a digit.
wKey = tblSize == "Small" ? 0 : tblSize == "Medium" ? 7.0 : tblSize == "Large" ? 8.0 : 10.0
wVal = tblSize == "Small" ? 0 : tblSize == "Medium" ? 4.2 : tblSize == "Large" ? 4.8 : 6.0
wGap = tblSize == "Small" ? 0 : tblSize == "Medium" ? 0.7 : tblSize == "Large" ? 0.8 : 1.0
frameW = tblSize == "Huge" ? 2 : 1

// v10.1 - five extra rows (9..13) for the EARNINGS RESULT block. Rows 0..8 are
// untouched, and rows that are never written render nothing at all, so with no
// earnings text loaded the panel looks exactly as it did before.
var table diag = table.new(f_pos(), 7, 14, border_width=1, frame_width=frameW,
     frame_color=color.new(color.gray, 55), border_color=color.new(color.gray, 80))

// The banner rows span whole blocks. Merging happens ONCE, against empty
// placeholder cells, before anything is written - a merged region can only be
// addressed through its top-left cell afterwards, so writing to the cells it
// swallowed on the next tick would be an error rather than a no-op.
var bool diagMerged = false

// Panel palette. Named once so a colour change is one edit rather than fourteen.
cPos  = color.new(#00e676, 0)     // a number that is working for you
cNeg  = color.new(#ff5252, 0)     // a number that is against you
cVal  = color.new(color.white, 5)
cKey  = color.new(color.white, 28)
cDim  = color.new(color.gray, 25)
cBg   = color.new(color.black, 80)
cGap  = color.new(color.black, 90)

f_pct(float v) => na(v) ? "—" : (v > 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(v, "#.##") + "%"
f_rr(float v)  => na(v) ? "—" : (v > 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(v, "#.##") + "R"
f_px(float v)  => na(v) ? "—" : str.tostring(v, format.mintick)

// DETECTION row - column 0..2.
f_row(int r, string k, float v, string vf, float need, bool ok, bool active) =>
    table.cell(diag, 0, r, k, text_color=color.new(color.white, active ? 25 : 65), text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=cBg, width=wKey)
    table.cell(diag, 1, r, f_num(v, vf), text_color=not active ? color.new(color.gray, 50) : ok ? cPos : cVal, text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_right, bgcolor=cBg, width=wVal)
    table.cell(diag, 2, r, active ? f_num(need, vf) : "off", text_color=cDim, text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_right, bgcolor=cBg, width=wVal)

// POSITION row - column 4..6. Always "label, level, what that level is worth in
// percent", so the columns mean the same thing all the way down the block.
// v10.1 - one row of the earnings block. "LABEL|VALUE" splits into the two halves;
// text with no pipe runs across the whole width. Declared here at top level because
// Pine will not accept a function defined inside an `if`.
f_erow(int r, string s) =>
    if str.length(s) > 0
        int p = str.pos(s, "|")
        string k = na(p) ? s : str.substring(s, 0, p)
        string v = na(p) ? "" : str.substring(s, p + 1)
        table.cell(diag, 0, r, "  " + k, text_color=cKey, text_size=txBody,
         text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=cBg)
        table.merge_cells(diag, 0, r, 3, r)
        table.cell(diag, 4, r, v + "  ", text_color=cVal, text_size=txBody,
         text_halign=text.align_right, bgcolor=cBg)
        table.merge_cells(diag, 4, r, 6, r)

f_trow(int r, string k, string v1, color c1, string v2, color c2, string tip) =>
    table.cell(diag, 4, r, k,  text_color=cKey, text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_left,  bgcolor=cBg, width=wKey, tooltip=tip)
    table.cell(diag, 5, r, v1, text_color=c1,   text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_right, bgcolor=cBg, width=wVal, tooltip=tip)
    table.cell(diag, 6, r, v2, text_color=c2,   text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_right, bgcolor=cBg, width=wVal, tooltip=tip)

if showTable and barstate.islast and not diagMerged
    for c = 0 to 6
        table.cell(diag, c, 0, "")
        table.cell(diag, c, 1, "")
    for r = 2 to 8
        table.cell(diag, 3, r, "", bgcolor=cGap, width=wGap)
    for c = 4 to 6
        table.cell(diag, c, 2, "")
    for c = 0 to 2
        table.cell(diag, c, 8, "")
    table.merge_cells(diag, 0, 0, 6, 0)     // stage banner, full width
    table.merge_cells(diag, 0, 1, 6, 1)     // stage note, full width
    table.merge_cells(diag, 4, 2, 6, 2)     // position banner, right block
    table.merge_cells(diag, 0, 8, 2, 8)     // detection block has one row spare
    diagMerged := true

if showTable and barstate.islast
    // ── THE STAGE MACHINE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Ordered by what you most need to know, not by how the script evaluates. An
    // open position outranks the session read: a trade opened post-market and
    // still running at 09:45 the next morning must say IN TRADE, not OUT OF
    // SESSION. The gate rows still show the session picture underneath.
    bool awaitConf = not na(impBar) and impDir != 0 and not impDone and trDir == 0

    string stage = not armed ? "BUILDING BASELINE" :
     trDir > 0                     ? (holdOn ? "IN TRADE — LONG  (HOLD)"  : "IN TRADE — LONG")  :
     trDir < 0                     ? (holdOn ? "IN TRADE — SHORT  (HOLD)" : "IN TRADE — SHORT") :
     awaitConf                     ? "AWAITING CONFIRM" :
     not inSess                    ? "OUT OF SESSION" :
     not guardOk                   ? "SESSION GUARD" :
     not preOpenOk                 ? "PRE-OPEN EXCLUDED" :
     gatesOk and coolOk and dayOk  ? "★ PRINT DETECTED" :
     gatesOk and not dayOk         ? "ALREADY FIRED THIS SESSION" :
     gatesOk and not coolOk        ? "COOLDOWN" : "ARMED"

    // The one-line translation, deliberately kept to one short clause. A Pine
    // table sizes itself to its widest cell, so a long note here would widen the
    // whole panel and, worse, change its width as the stage changed. The full
    // glossary lives in the hover tooltip, which costs no width at all.
    string stageWhy =
     stage == "BUILDING BASELINE"  ? "warming up — not enough extended-hours bars yet" :
     trDir != 0                    ? "stop, targets and trail are live on the right" :
     stage == "AWAITING CONFIRM"   ? "needs a new extreme past the print, within " + str.tostring(confirmWindow) + " bars" :
     stage == "OUT OF SESSION"     ? "regular hours — this hunts pre/post-market only" :
     stage == "SESSION GUARD"      ? "too few bars into this block to trust the baseline" :
     stage == "PRE-OPEN EXCLUDED"  ? "inside the excluded minutes before the 09:30 open" :
     stage == "★ PRINT DETECTED"   ? "every gate cleared — the confirm window is now open" :
     stage == "ALREADY FIRED THIS SESSION" ? "one print per session block, and this one is spent" :
     stage == "COOLDOWN"           ? "gates pass, but too soon after the last signal" :
     "watching every bar — all gates live, none cleared yet"

    string stageTip = "WHAT THE STAGES MEAN, in the order the panel prefers them:\n\n" +
     "BUILDING BASELINE — not yet enough extended-hours bars to know what normal volume and range look like for this symbol and session bucket. Every gate is measured against that baseline, so nothing can fire until it fills. If it never leaves this stage, the chart has not loaded enough pre/post-market history.\n\n" +
     "IN TRADE — a confirm opened a position. The block on the right is live.\n\n" +
     "AWAITING CONFIRM — a print has fired and the script is inside the confirm window, waiting for price to make a new extreme past the print bar while retaining most of the impulse. Most prints that fail, fail here. Nothing has been risked yet.\n\n" +
     "OUT OF SESSION — regular trading hours, or the wrong side of the session filter. Detection is deliberately asleep.\n\n" +
     "SESSION GUARD — the session block just started and there are too few bars in it for the baseline to mean anything.\n\n" +
     "PRE-OPEN EXCLUDED — the minutes immediately before 09:30, where pre-market volume ramps into the open and would fake a print.\n\n" +
     "★ PRINT DETECTED — this bar cleared every gate. The arrow is on the chart.\n\n" +
     "ALREADY FIRED THIS SESSION — one-shot mode has used its shot for this session block.\n\n" +
     "COOLDOWN — the gates pass again but too soon after the last signal, so it is treated as the same event rather than a new one.\n\n" +
     "ARMED — the normal resting state. Baseline is full, session is right, every gate is being evaluated on every bar."

    color stageCol = stage == "★ PRINT DETECTED" ? (isUp ? colUp : colDn) :
     trDir > 0                   ? colUp :
     trDir < 0                   ? colDn :
     stage == "AWAITING CONFIRM" ? colConf :
     stage == "ARMED"            ? color.new(#2962ff, 0) : color.new(color.gray, 25)

    // ── BANNERS, full width ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    table.cell(diag, 0, 0, stage, text_color=color.white, text_size=txHead, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.new(stageCol, 45), tooltip=stageTip)
    table.cell(diag, 0, 1, stageWhy, text_color=color.new(color.white, 18), text_size=txNote, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.new(stageCol, 82), tooltip=stageTip)

    // ── LEFT BLOCK: DETECTION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    string hdr = "EPD " + VERSION + "   " + (bucket < 0 ? "RTH" : (inPre ? "pre" : "post") + " +" + str.tostring(intoMin) + "m")
    table.cell(diag, 0, 2, hdr,   text_color=color.new(color.white, 0), text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_left,  bgcolor=color.new(color.black, 62), width=wKey)
    table.cell(diag, 1, 2, "now",  text_color=cDim, text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_right, bgcolor=color.new(color.black, 62), width=wVal)
    table.cell(diag, 2, 2, "need", text_color=cDim, text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_right, bgcolor=color.new(color.black, 62), width=wVal)

    f_row(3, "RVOL",        rvol,     "#.#",  eRvol, volOk, true)
    f_row(4, "Range / ATR", rangeAtr, "#.#",  eRng,  rngOk, true)
    f_row(5, "Excursion %", excPct,   "#.##", eMove, movOk, true)
    f_row(6, "vs RTH med",  rthRatio, "#.##", minRthMult, rthOk, useRthGate)

    table.cell(diag, 0, 7, "Conviction", text_color=cKey, text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=cBg, width=wKey)
    table.cell(diag, 1, 7, str.tostring(score), text_color=color.new(color.white, 0), text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_right, bgcolor=cBg, width=wVal)
    table.cell(diag, 2, 7, "/100", text_color=cDim, text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_right, bgcolor=cBg, width=wVal)

    // The detection block is one row shorter than the position block. The spare
    // row is merged and left blank rather than padded with a number nobody asked
    // for - it reads as deliberate whitespace, and it keeps both blocks the same
    // height so the panel is a rectangle.
    table.cell(diag, 0, 8, "", bgcolor=cBg)

    // ── RIGHT BLOCK: THE OPEN POSITION ─────────────────────────────────────────
    // Divide R by the ORIGINAL risk, not the live stop distance. Once the stop
    // trails, the live distance shrinks toward zero and this reading would
    // inflate without the trade having gained anything.
    float trR = trDir != 0 and not na(trEntry) and not na(trRisk0) and trRisk0 > 0 ?
     ((close - trEntry) * trDir) / trRisk0 : na

    // PERCENTS ARE QUOTED AGAINST THE ENTRY PRICE, always, including the stop and target rows.
    float pctOpen = trDir != 0 and not na(trEntry) and trEntry > 0 ?
     100.0 * (close - trEntry) * trDir / trEntry : na

    string trTxt = trDir == 0 ? "no open position" :
     (trDir > 0 ? "▲  LONG from " : "▼  SHORT from ") + str.tostring(trEntry, format.mintick)
    color  trCol = trDir == 0 ? color.new(color.gray, 30) : (trDir > 0 ? colUp : colDn)

    table.cell(diag, 4, 2, trTxt, text_color=color.white, text_size=txBody, text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.new(trCol, 62),
     tooltip = trDir == 0 ? "Nothing is open. A ✓ confirm is what opens a position; until then the rows below have nothing to describe." :
     "Opened at the close of the ✓ confirm bar. The stop is placed a fraction of the way back toward the print candle's far side, and 1R is that entry-to-stop distance — every target and every percent below is measured from this entry.")

    f_trow(3, "open P/L",
     f_pct(pctOpen), na(pctOpen) ? cDim : pctOpen > 0 ? cPos : pctOpen < 0 ? cNeg : cVal,
     f_rr(trR),      na(trR)     ? cDim : trR     > 0 ? cPos : trR     < 0 ? cNeg : cVal,
     "Unrealised, on the last price, from the confirm entry.\n\nThe percent is the move in the underlying — NOT your account P/L, which depends on size and on options leverage if you are trading contracts.\n\nR is the same move in units of the original entry-to-stop risk. [n]R means the trade has made exactly what it was risking.")

    float nextTgt = na
    string nextNm = "next target"
    if trDir != 0
        if not trTp1Done
            nextTgt := trTarget
            nextNm  := "next target  T1"
        else if not trTp2Done
            nextTgt := trTarget2
            nextNm  := "next target  T2"
        else if not trTp3Done
            nextTgt := trTarget3
            nextNm  := "next target  T3"
        else if not trTp4Done
            nextTgt := trTarget4
            nextNm  := "next target  T4"
        else
            nextNm  := "past T4 — trail only"
    float pctTgt = not na(nextTgt) and not na(trEntry) and trEntry > 0 ?
     100.0 * (nextTgt - trEntry) * trDir / trEntry : na

    f_trow(4, nextNm, f_px(nextTgt), na(nextTgt) ? cDim : cVal,
     f_pct(pctTgt), na(pctTgt) ? cDim : cPos,
     "The next take-profit level not yet reached, and what it is worth as a percent of the entry if price gets there.\n\nT1 = +1R, T2 = +2R, T3 = +3R, where R is the original entry-to-stop distance. Reaching T1 also starts moving the stop, so the two rows below change as this one ticks over.\n\nOnce all three are done the row reads 'past T3 — trail only': no further target, and the trailing stop alone decides the exit.")

    float stop0 = trDir != 0 and not na(trEntry) and not na(trRisk0) ? trEntry - trDir * trRisk0 : na
    float pctStop0 = not na(stop0) and not na(trEntry) and trEntry > 0 ?
     100.0 * (stop0 - trEntry) * trDir / trEntry : na

    f_trow(5, "stop  as placed", f_px(stop0), na(stop0) ? cDim : cVal,
     f_pct(pctStop0), na(pctStop0) ? cDim : cNeg,
     "Where the stop was FIRST placed, at the confirm, and what being taken out there would have cost as a percent of the entry.\n\nThis is the number the position was sized on — 1R by definition — and it does not move for the life of the trade, even after the live stop below has trailed away from it.")

    float pctStopNow = trDir != 0 and not na(trStop) and not na(trEntry) and trEntry > 0 ?
     100.0 * (trStop - trEntry) * trDir / trEntry : na

    f_trow(6, "stop  trailed to", trDir == 0 ? "—" : f_px(trStop), trDir == 0 ? cDim : cVal,
     f_pct(pctStopNow), na(pctStopNow) ? cDim : pctStopNow > 0 ? cPos : pctStopNow < 0 ? cNeg : cVal,
     "Where the stop is RIGHT NOW, after trailing, and what getting hit there would book as a percent of the entry.\n\nThis is the row that matters mid-trade — it is the price that will actually execute. While it matches the row above, the trail has not moved yet.\n\nWhen this percent turns GREEN the stop has ratcheted past breakeven and the trade can no longer lose. A ✕ from there is a win being collected, not a loss.")

    // What the ladder has locked, in R: step 1 = half the risk removed (-0.5R),
    // step 2 = breakeven, step 3 = +1R (the T1 level), step n = (n-2)R.
    float lockedR = lockedStep <= 0 ? na : (ladderT1R + ladderStepR * (lockedStep - 1))
    string trailTxt = trailMode == "Fixed" ? "fixed — off" :
     not trArmed ? "not armed yet" :
     trFrozen ? "FROZEN" : "trailing"
    color trailCol = trailMode == "Fixed" ? cDim :
     not trArmed ? cDim : trFrozen ? color.new(#ffc400, 0) : cPos

    f_trow(7, "trail", trDir == 0 ? "—" : trailTxt, trDir == 0 ? cDim : trailCol,
     trDir == 0 or na(lockedR) ? "—" : f_rr(lockedR),
     trDir == 0 or na(lockedR) ? cDim : lockedR > 0 ? cPos : cVal,
     "What the trailing stop is doing, and the highest rung the target ladder has locked.\n\n'not armed yet' — the move has not gone far enough in your favour for the trail to start. Nothing tightens before that; an earnings move needs room in its first minutes.\n\n'trailing' — the stop is following structure and can only ever move in your favour.\n\n'FROZEN' — price is consolidating and the trail is deliberately holding still rather than tightening into a pause.\n\n'fixed — off' — trailing is disabled in settings; the stop stays where it was placed.\n\nThe locked figure is the ladder's rung: T1 reached moves to breakeven (0.00R), T2 reached locks +0.50R, T3 reached locks +1.00R - half a unit of risk per rung. The live stop above may be tighter than this if structure has pulled it further.")

    // Average entry once the + has fired. Gated on trDir like every other row in
    // this block: the add tracker is deliberately NOT cleared when a trade exits -
    // a re-entry after a stop-out still needs its anchor - so without this gate the
    // row went on quoting the last trade's blended entry under "no open position".
    float avgEntry = trDir != 0 and addFills > 0 and not na(trEntry) ?
     (trEntry + addSumPx) / (addFills + 1) : na
    float pctAvg = not na(avgEntry) and avgEntry > 0 ?
     100.0 * (close - avgEntry) * trDir / avgEntry : na
    string avgNm = trDir == 0 or addFills <= 0 ? "avg entry  (no adds)" :
     "avg entry  (+" + str.tostring(addFills) + ")"

    f_trow(8, avgNm, f_px(avgEntry), na(avgEntry) ? cDim : cVal,
     f_pct(pctAvg), na(pctAvg) ? cDim : pctAvg > 0 ? cPos : pctAvg < 0 ? cNeg : cVal,
     na(avgEntry) ? "Blended entry across the confirm and every + add marker. Nothing to blend yet — no + has fired on this trade, so your entry is the confirm price on the banner above." :
     "Your blended cost after taking the + adds, and what the position is worth from THAT price rather than from the original confirm.\n\nFirst add filled at " + f_px(addFirstPx) + ", confirm entry " + f_px(trEntry) + ".\n\nEQUAL SIZE PER FILL is assumed — the script has no idea what size you actually traded, so it weights the confirm and each + the same. If you added a different size, this is the shape of the answer and not the answer.\n\nEach + is booked at the close of the bar it printed on, which is the same price the reclaim test used. The R figures, the targets and the stop rows all stay measured from the ORIGINAL entry, because that is what the levels on the chart were drawn from.")

// ── v10.1 - THE EARNINGS RESULT BLOCK, rows 9..12 ────────────────────────────
// Only drawn when earnTitle is non-empty, so with nothing loaded the panel is
// byte-for-byte what it always was. Each row is "LABEL|VALUE"; anything without
// a pipe is printed across the label column as free text.
if showTable and barstate.islast and str.length(str.tostring(earnTitle)) > 0
    table.cell(diag, 0, 9, "  " + earnTitle, text_color=color.white, text_size=txBody,
     text_halign=text.align_left, bgcolor=color.new(color.teal, 55),
     tooltip="Filed with the SEC and read straight off the 8-K, not from a news article.\n\nWritten here by tools/earnings-now.py. Clear the Headline input to hide this block.")
    table.merge_cells(diag, 0, 9, 6, 9)
    f_erow(10, earnRow1)
    f_erow(11, earnRow2)
    f_erow(12, earnRow3)
    f_erow(13, earnRow4)


// v1.9: confirm plotshapes removed too - same duplicate-marker problem. The
// confirm is now a single label whose badge stays yellow but whose TICK is
// coloured by the direction of the print it belongs to: green tick = bullish
// continuation, red tick = bearish. See the label.new call above.


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// EXIT MARKERS
//
// A confirm opens a tracked trade. Two things can end it, and each prints once:
//
//   T1/T2/T3 - price reached [n].
//   ✕  STOP - the stop was touched. Since v3.4 the stop trails, so this is no
//               longer synonymous with a losing trade: the marker reports which
//               stop was hit, what it banked, and is tinted green when the exit
//               was profitable.
//
// On the INVALIDATION marker: it is probably redundant, because on the original
// calibration the structural stop had already fired at or before the point where
// the whole move was given back, in every case that got there. That count came
// from the consolidated-tape feed and is void here, so the marker is left ON
// rather than asserted useless - judge it on your own charts.
//
// Evaluated live rather than on bar close: these are price levels, and a touch
// is a touch. High only rises and low only falls inside a bar, so neither marker
// can appear and then vanish.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

bool tp1Now  = false
bool tp2Now  = false
bool stopNow = false
bool invNow  = false
int  exitDir = 0        // captured BEFORE trDir is cleared, or the marker lands wrong

bool tp3Now = false
bool tp4Now = false

// ─── BREAKEVEN STOP + CONSOLIDATION ADD MARKER ─────────────────────────────────
// WITHDRAWN 2026-08-16: this block used to carry a table of R-expectancies for
// five stop policies, and chose the breakeven-once stop from it.
// Every one of those
// numbers came from a non-TradingView feed whose extended-hours volume is a
// fraction of this chart's, so the trades they were measured on are not the
// trades this script fires. They are void and have been removed rather than
// repeated - a wrong number in a tooltip is worse than no number.
//
// What that leaves: the stop still moves to breakeven once and then stops, but
// that is now an UNMEASURED default, not a supported conclusion. The owner has asked
// for a stop that keeps stepping with price through the move; re-measuring on
// TradingView bars is the open work that decides it.
// The consolidation window, computed on EVERY bar at global scope. ta.highest /
// ta.lowest keep their own history, so they must never be called conditionally.
// The window ends at the PREVIOUS bar: the breakout bar is not allowed to be
// part of the range it has to break.
float consWinHi = ta.highest(high, consMinBars)[1]
float consWinLo = ta.lowest(low,  consMinBars)[1]
float consSpan  = consWinHi - consWinLo
float consBudget = na(impRng) ? na : consMaxFrac * impRng

// HOISTED to global scope in v3.4, because two features read it now: the +
// marker (a breakout OUT of the pause) and the trail freeze (do not tighten
// INSIDE the pause). One definition, so the two can never disagree about
// whether the move is consolidating.
bool consTight = not na(consSpan) and not na(consBudget) and consSpan <= consBudget

// Structural swing window for the trail. Same rules as above: ta.* keeps its own
// history so it must be unconditional and at global scope, and it ends at the
// PREVIOUS bar so the bar being judged is not part of its own reference range.
float swingLo  = ta.lowest(low,   trailLookback)[1]
float swingHi  = ta.highest(high, trailLookback)[1]
float trailBuf = nz(atrSes, 0.0) * trailATRmult

bool addNow = false
int  addDir = 0
bool revNow = false      // a reversal-character bar ended the trade
if trDir != 0 and not na(trBar) and bar_index > trBar
    float favNow = ((trDir > 0 ? high : low) - trEntry) * trDir
    trPeak := math.max(trPeak, favNow)

    // Arm on PEAK excursion against the ORIGINAL risk. Nothing tightens the stop
    // before this; an earnings move needs room in its first minutes.
    if not na(trRisk0)
        if trRisk0 > 0
            if trPeak >= beAfterR * trRisk0
                trArmed := true

    // ── THE TRAIL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Confirmed bars only. A trail that steps intrabar would ratchet off a close
    // that has not happened yet, and the drawn step would belong to no bar.
    // The stop-HIT test further down stays live: a touch is a touch.
    //
    // Structure: every mechanism below only PROPOSES a level into `cand`. One
    // ratchet at the end decides, and one place draws. That is deliberate - the
    // reason v3.2/v3.3 drew a mismatched connector is that moving and drawing
    // were tangled together in the one branch that happened to run.
    if barstate.isconfirmed
        float cand = trStop

        // It is gated on TIME instead, and phased in rather than switched on.
        //
        // The earnings move runs hot for roughly 30-60 minutes. Overnight it calms down, and
        // through that decay the stop should tighten to limit the loss. At the open volatility
        // returns: if the move REVERSES the tightened stop is hit sooner and less is lost; if it
        // CONTINUES the stop is not hit, but because volatility rose the trail should give the
        // run more room by advancing less per favourable move. If volatility later falls again
        // it resumes advancing more.
        //
        //    All of that falls out of ONE mechanism: aim at a target a multiple
        //    of CURRENT volatility behind price, and close only a fraction of the
        //    remaining gap each bar. When atrLive grows the target sits further
        //    back, so the same favourable move advances the stop less. When
        //    atrLive shrinks the target closes in and the stop catches up faster.
        int atrAge = bar_index - trBar
        if atrTighten and not na(atrLive) and atrLive > 0 and atrAge >= atrTightAfter
            float wRaw  = atrTrailBlend <= 0 ? 1.0 :
                 1.0 * (atrAge - atrTightAfter) / math.max(1, atrTrailBlend)
            float wAtr  = math.min(1.0, math.max(0.0, wRaw))
            // v6.8 - THE FLOOR. Never closer to price than atrFloorR of the
            //        trade's own risk. Without it a dead overnight tape drives
            //        atrLive to nearly zero and the stop lands on top of price.
            // ── v7.5 - IT TIGHTENS AS THE TRADE AGES ──────────────────────────
            //
            // MEASURED CAUSE, and it was not the multiple - it was the FLOOR.
            // CBRS entry 238.55, R 13.97, so atrFloorR 0.75 pins the stop 10.48
            // points behind price no matter what volatility does. Overnight
            // atrLive collapses, atrTightMult * atrLive goes tiny, the floor wins,
            // and the stop parks 10.48 points away. That IS the 222.09-versus-212
            // gap he pointed at. Price reached [n]R and the trade closed [n]R.
            //
            // So BOTH quantities decay on one ramp: the ATR multiple from
            // atrTightMult down to atrMultLate, and the floor from atrFloorR down
            // to atrFloorRLate, over atrTightDecay bars starting at atrTightAfter.
            // Before that point NOTHING changes - the first hour is untouched,
            // exactly as asked.
            // 0 means NO decay - hold the early distance for the whole trade.
            // (The first cut had this as 1.0, which made 0 mean INSTANTLY fully
            // tight - the exact opposite of what the tooltip promised. Caught by
            // A/B-ing it on CBRS rather than trusting the code.)
            float dRaw  = atrTightDecay <= 0 ? 0.0 :
                 1.0 * (atrAge - atrTightAfter) / math.max(1, atrTightDecay)
            float wDec  = math.min(1.0, math.max(0.0, dRaw))
            float multNow  = atrTightMult + (atrMultLate   - atrTightMult) * wDec
            float floorNow = atrFloorR    + (atrFloorRLate - atrFloorR)    * wDec
            float atrDist = multNow * atrLive
            float atrMin  = na(trRisk0) or trRisk0 <= 0 ? 0.0 : floorNow * trRisk0
            float atrTgt = close - math.max(atrDist, atrMin) * trDir
            float atrProp = trStop + (atrTgt - trStop) * atrTrailStep * wAtr
            cand := trDir > 0 ? math.max(cand, atrProp) : math.min(cand, atrProp)

        // 1. TARGET LADDER - the stop moves to LEVELS ALREADY DRAWN ON THE CHART.
        //
        //    v3.5 computed (step - 1.75) x R, which produced 94.405 and 96.223 on
        //    CRWV. Those are arbitrary numbers that line up with nothing you can
        //    see - the "they move down random amounts" complaint, and it was fair.
        //    The ladder now lands on the take-profit levels themselves:
        //
        //      T1 reached  ->  entry (breakeven)     v8.1, was -0.5R
        //      T2 reached  ->  +0.5R
        //      T3 reached  ->  +1.0R
        //      [n]R, [n]R    ->  [n]R, [n]R ...
        //
        //    Every level is one you can already point at, and the step at T1 keeps
        //    CBRS honest - at v3.4's setting that first step landed exactly on the
        //    initial stop, so nothing moved and the line sat flat.
        //
        //    Reading the PEAK rather than the close means it can only ever act on
        //    new profit, so it is structurally incapable of tightening into a pause.
        if useLadder and trArmed and not na(trRisk0) and trRisk0 > 0
            int stepNow = int(math.floor(trPeak / trRisk0))
            if stepNow > lockedStep
                lockedStep := stepNow
                // v9.1 - CORRECTED. This block claimed "breakeven at T1", which was the
                // third place in the file asserting that. The rungs come from ladderT1R
                // and ladderStepR, and at the shipped defaults (-0.25R, 0.50R) they are:
                //      T1 reached  ->  -0.25R   (NOT breakeven - risk is still on)
                //      T2 reached  ->  +0.25R
                //      T3 reached  ->  +0.75R
                //      [n]R         ->  [n]R
                // The old first rung left half the original risk exposed while the T1
                // tooltip claimed the trade could not become a loser. With half the
                // risk still open, it could. Breakeven at T1 makes the
                // claim true. Above that it moves half a unit of risk per rung, which is
                // what the owner asked for after CRWV: a full R per rung parks the stop on
                // the T1 line at +2R and collects an ordinary pullback.
                // v8.1c - both rungs are inputs now. Default: T1 -> -0.25R, then
                // +0.5R per rung. See the ladderT1R tooltip for why breakeven at T1
                // was too tight and a full R per rung was too tight above that.
                float lad = trEntry + trDir * (ladderT1R + ladderStepR * (stepNow - 1)) * trRisk0
                cand := trDir > 0 ? math.max(cand, lad) : math.min(cand, lad)

        // 2. BREAKEVEN ONCE - the v3.1..v3.3 behaviour, kept selectable so the
        //    old shape can be compared against the new one on the same chart.
        if useBeOnce and trArmed and not beMoved
            beMoved := true
            cand := trDir > 0 ? math.max(cand, trEntry) : math.min(cand, trEntry)

        // 2b. TRUE ATR TRAIL, ONCE THE TARGETS ARE BEHIND US.
        //     The ladder can only act when the trade gains another whole R, so
        //     past +3R its steps become rare and the line looks parked - which is
        //     exactly the "it just levels off" complaint. From T3 onward the stop
        //     also tracks a fixed ATR distance behind the best price reached, and
        //     that re-computes every closed bar, so it keeps moving with price for
        //     as long as the trend runs and only gives out when the move is
        //     genuinely exhausted or reverses.
        //     MEASURED IN R, NOT IN ATR. v3.5 used 3 x the session-phase ATR, and
        //     that ATR is built from QUIET extended-hours minutes: on CRWV it is
        //     0.33 against a trade risk of 1.82, so "3 x ATR" put the stop only
        //     [n]R behind the peak - 98.861 with T3 at 99.404 and the very next
        //     bar dipping to 98.60. That is the "cuts in ridiculously close"
        //     failure, and it was a unit error, not a tuning miss. R is the right
        //     unit here because it is the same unit the targets are spaced in, so
        //     the trail stays visually consistent with the rest of the staircase.
        if trTp3Done and not na(trRisk0) and trRisk0 > 0
            float peakPx3 = trEntry + trDir * trPeak
            float rLvl    = peakPx3 - trDir * trailGiveR * trRisk0
            cand := trDir > 0 ? math.max(cand, rLvl) : math.min(cand, rLvl)

        // 3. STRUCTURAL SWING TRAIL, FROZEN THROUGH A CONSOLIDATION.
        //    The level sits beyond the recent swing extreme by an ATR buffer, so
        //    it follows real pullback structure rather than a fixed distance.
        //    trailHold is the ratcheted memory of that level: while the move is
        //    consolidating it is simply not updated, so the stop holds where the
        //    last genuine swing put it and does NOT creep into the compression.
        //    That is the mechanism that lets retracement run without being cut.
        trFrozen := useStruct and trArmed and freezeInCons and consTight
        if useStruct and trArmed
            bool frozen = freezeInCons and consTight
            float structLvl = trDir > 0 ? swingLo - trailBuf : swingHi + trailBuf
            // Floor it a minimum distance behind the best price seen. The swing
            // window collapses after a vertical move, which would otherwise park
            // the stop right under price for the next bounce to collect. This
            // only ever loosens, so it cannot cause an early exit.
            if trailMinGiveR > 0 and not na(trRisk0) and trRisk0 > 0 and not na(structLvl)
                float peakPx = trEntry + trDir * trPeak
                float loosest = peakPx - trDir * trailMinGiveR * trRisk0
                structLvl := trDir > 0 ? math.min(structLvl, loosest) : math.max(structLvl, loosest)
            if not frozen and not na(structLvl)
                trailHold := na(trailHold) ? structLvl :
                 (trDir > 0 ? math.max(trailHold, structLvl) : math.min(trailHold, structLvl))
            if not na(trailHold)
                cand := trDir > 0 ? math.max(cand, trailHold) : math.min(cand, trailHold)

        // 4. REVERSAL CHARACTER - the early cut.
        //    Depth of retracement cannot separate consolidation from reversal;
        //    both retrace. The KIND of bar can. All four must be true at once:
        //    large for this session's ATR, closing at the end that hurts, on
        //    elevated bucket-relative volume, and giving back a real fraction of
        //    the print candle in this single bar. Consolidation fails the first
        //    three by construction - small bars, mid-range closes, volume drying
        //    up - so the two shapes separate on character instead of on distance.
        //    All terms are plain float comparisons, which yield false on na
        //    rather than raising, so chaining them is safe here.
        //
        //    THE MISTAKE THIS BLOCK ALREADY MADE ONCE, kept as a warning: the
        //    first version gated only on bucket ATR and bucket volume. SPCE's
        //    post-market ATR is about two cents, so "2x ATR and 1.5x median
        //    volume" was cleared by a four-cent chop bar at 16:14 and it killed a
        //    trade that went on to make all three targets. Baselines measured on
        //    QUIET minutes cannot judge bars that follow an earnings print.
        //    Everything decisive here is now scaled to the PRINT CANDLE, and the
        //    exit cannot fire until there is profit to protect.
        // v8.1 - THE IN-TRADE REVERSAL EXIT, on the shared detector.
        //
        // This block used to run its own private six-threshold reversal test that existed
        // nowhere else in the file, and refused to fire until the trade was up revMinR. So it
        // now fires on revCoreNow - the identical test that draws the badge when flat.
        //
        // Nothing about the open trade's accounting is touched: trDir, trEntry, trRisk0
        // and the targets are all untouched, and the direction the exit books against is
        // exitDir := trDir, captured in the exit block itself.
        //
        // The stop jumps to this bar's close so the stop-hit test below fires on THIS
        // bar and the X lands on the reversal candle rather than a minute later.
        if revExit and trDir != 0 and revCoreNow
            revNow := true
            if trDir > 0 ? close > cand : close < cand
                cand := close

        // ── RATCHET, THEN DRAW ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // The stop may only move in the trade's favour. Every move is rendered as
        // a STEP: close the old horizontal run at this bar, drop a vertical riser,
        // open a new horizontal run at the new level. line.set_y1/set_y2 is never
        // used on an existing segment - sliding a line rewrites history and makes
        // the chart read as though the stop had always been at the new level, or
        // worse, as though it had been hit.
        //
        // Riser and run are drawn with IDENTICAL colour, style and width. In
        // v3.2/v3.3 the riser was dotted width 1 against a dashed width 2 run,
        // which is the mismatch that made it read as two unrelated marks instead
        // of one staggered line.
        if trailMode != "Fixed" and not na(cand) and not na(trStop)
            bool better = trDir > 0 ? cand > trStop : cand < trStop
            if better
                float oldStop = trStop
                trStop := cand
                if showTradeLines and not na(lnStop)
                    line.set_x2(lnStop, bar_index)
                    line.new(bar_index, oldStop, bar_index, trStop,
                     color=colStop2, style=line.style_dashed, width=2)
                    lnStop := line.new(bar_index, trStop, bar_index + 1, trStop,
                     color=colStop2, style=line.style_dashed, width=2)

    // ── CONSOLIDATION BREAKOUT - the + marker ──────────────────────────────────
    // v3.3 REWRITE. v3.2 could not fire, and it was not a tuning problem: it used
    // ONE fraction for two tests that pull against each other. A bar counted as
    // "quiet" at up to consMaxFrac x impRng, but the whole run of such bars then
    // had to fit inside consMaxFrac x impRng as well. Three bars each allowed to
    // be half the print range cannot collectively occupy half the print range
    // unless they overlap almost exactly. And the run only reset on a NON-quiet
    // bar - after a large print candle almost every bar is quiet - so once the
    // span exceeded the budget it stayed exceeded for the rest of the trade.
    //
    // Measured on the real SPCE 2026-08-12 post-market bars pulled from this
    // chart: count>=3 passed on 8 bars, the span test on 6 bars, both on ZERO.
    // The gates cross in opposite directions; they can never be true together.
    //
    // The accumulator is gone. The pause is a fixed-length rolling window, so it
    // re-tightens by itself and there is no absorbing state. One budget, one test:
    //     span     = highest(high, N)[1] - lowest(low, N)[1]
    //     tight    = span <= consMaxFrac * impRng
    //     breakout = close beyond the window edge, in the trade's direction
    //
    // It may fire more than once per trade. A move that pauses three times is
    // offering three adds, and a spare + costs nothing while a missed one costs
    // the fill. Spacing is one window length so a single breakout is not marked
    // twice on consecutive bars.
    // On short-circuiting: the hazard this script was once halted by was
    // `not na(arr) and array.size(arr)`, where the second term RAISES on an na
    // array. Comparing a float against na does not raise - it yields false - so
    // chaining the guards here is safe. Every block indent below is a multiple of
    // four, because Pine reads any other indent as a continuation of the line
    // above and will report the next `if` as a variable name.
    // (the + is decided in its own block below - see WHY THE ADD MOVED OUT)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// WHY THE ADD MOVED OUT OF THE TRADE BLOCK - v3.4
//
// The + used to require an OPEN position, and on NBIS 2026-08-12 that made it
// unreachable exactly where it was wanted. Walked on real TradingView bars:
//
//   07:39  print   212.59 -> 218.03
//   07:41  confirm long at 220.73, stop 215.85
//   07:45  STOP HIT
//   07:52  consolidation breakout, close 217.85   <- the circle on the chart
//   07:58  again, 219.16
//   08:01  again, 220.99
//
// The consolidation worth adding into begins AFTER the stop. Requiring an open
// trade meant the one case that most needed the marker could never produce it.
//
// And the stop was not too tight. At 0.60 retrace it is hit at 07:45; at 0.80 at
// 07:45; at 1.00 - the print candle's own low - still hit, at 07:46. Price gave
// back 109% of the impulse, trading BELOW the pre-print price. No stop width
// survives that, so the exit was right and the answer is not a wider stop.
//
// The answer is that the breakout after it is a RE-ENTRY, and at 217.85 it is a
// better price than the original 220.73 confirm. So the + now keys off the last
// confirmed trade's direction rather than an open position, bounded by a window
// after the print so it cannot wander into an unrelated later move.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE + RULE - REWRITTEN IN v3.5
//
// v3.4 asked "has the last N bars' whole range compressed below the print
// candle's range?" That is a COMPRESSION test, and compression takes time to
// develop, so the badge always arrived several bars after the dip you actually
// wanted to buy. On CRWV the + landed well right of the candle you arrowed; on
// NBIS it was later still. It also had no natural limit, so a trending move
// produced a badge at every pause - five or more on CBRS, CSCO and PLUG.
//
// v3.5 asks your question instead: "has price pulled back a modest amount and
// then started going again?" Pullback-and-reclaim, not compression:
//
//   peak      = best price since the confirm, in the trade's direction
//   pbExt     = the extreme AGAINST us since that peak
//   pull      = peak - pbExt, measured in R
//   qualifies = pull is between addMinPull and addMaxPull R, and at least
//               addMinBars bars have passed since the peak
//   fires     = close has reclaimed addReclaim of the pullback
//
// The band on `pull` is what separates the two cases: too small and it is noise
// inside a straight run, too large and it is a reversal rather than a pause.
//
// Verified on real TradingView bars against the marks on your screenshots:
//   CRWV  fires 16:22 at 96.75   (target was ~16:20-16:22)
//   NBIS  fires 07:46 at 214.90  (target was ~07:47-07:52; this is one bar
//                                 earlier and 3 points better than 217.85)
//   SPCE  fires 16:12 and 16:32, then stops - the cap is reached
// Stable across addReclaim 0.20-0.25; at 0.33+ the fires drift later, which is
// the failure mode v3.4 had.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
int addRefDir = trDir != 0 ? trDir : (addAfterStop ? trDirLast : 0)
if addRefDir != 0 and not na(trEntry)
    // Track the peak and the pullback on EVERY bar once a trade has been
    // confirmed, whether or not it is still open, so a re-entry after a stop-out
    // still has an anchor. Cleared only by a fresh print.
    float favPx = addRefDir > 0 ? high : low
    bool  newPeak = na(addPeakPx) ? true : (addRefDir > 0 ? favPx > addPeakPx : favPx < addPeakPx)
    if newPeak
        addPeakPx  := favPx
        addPeakBar := bar_index
        addPbExt   := na
    else
        float advPx = addRefDir > 0 ? low : high
        addPbExt := na(addPbExt) ? advPx :
         (addRefDir > 0 ? math.min(addPbExt, advPx) : math.max(addPbExt, advPx))

if showAdd and addRefDir != 0 and not na(trBar) and not na(impBar) and not na(trRisk0)
    if (bar_index - trBar) <= addWindowBars and trRisk0 > 0
        if not na(addPbExt) and not na(addPeakPx) and not na(addPeakBar)
            if (bar_index - addPeakBar) >= addMinBars
                float pullPx = math.abs(addPeakPx - addPbExt)
                if pullPx >= addMinPull * trRisk0 and pullPx <= addMaxPull * trRisk0
                    float reclaimPx = addPbExt + addRefDir * addReclaim * pullPx
                    bool  resumed = addRefDir > 0 ? close >= reclaimPx : close <= reclaimPx
                    if resumed
                        addNow   := true
                        addDir   := addRefDir
                        addCount := addCount + 1
                        // v4.2: book the fill at this bar's CLOSE. That is the
                        // price the reclaim test itself used, so the average the
                        // panel quotes is the average of the prices the script
                        // actually pointed at - not an optimistic one.
                        addSumPx := addSumPx + close
                        addFills := addFills + 1
                        if na(addFirstPx)
                            addFirstPx := close
                        // Re-arm from here so the NEXT pullback can produce the
                        // next badge, instead of re-firing on this same one.
                        addPeakPx  := addRefDir > 0 ? high : low
                        addPeakBar := bar_index
                        addPbExt   := na

if trDir != 0 and not na(trBar) and bar_index > trBar
    // Stop is checked FIRST. Within a single bar we cannot know which level was
    // touched first, so assume the unfavourable one - never book a target the
    // bar might not have reached before stopping out.
    //
    // v5.3 - TESTED AGAINST THE STOP AS IT STOOD AT THE END OF THE PREVIOUS BAR,
    // never against the level this bar just ratcheted it to. The trail update runs
    // EARLIER in the script than this test, so without `[1]` the sequence inside a
    // single bar was:
    //
    //    1. trPeak takes this bar's FAVOURABLE extreme  (a new profit high)
    //    2. the ladder ratchets trStop to that new milestone
    //    3. this test compares the SAME bar's ADVERSE extreme to the level
    //       that only exists because of step 1
    //
    // Those two extremes are not simultaneous. INFQ 2026-08-12, one bar after a
    // short entry at 12.53: the bar OPENED at its high 12.50 and fell to 12.13.
    // The low banked [n]R, the ladder moved the stop from 12.662 down to T1 at
    //
    // A stop cannot be hit by a price that happened before the stop moved there.
    // On the first bar after entry trStop[1] is the initial structural stop, so
    // the original behaviour is unchanged; only same-bar self-stopping is removed.
    float stopRef = nz(trStop[1], trStop)
    // v6.4 - THE REVERSAL EXIT GETS ITS OWN SAME-BAR PATH.
    //
    // v5.3 made this test read trStop[1] so a stop could never be hit by a price
    // that happened BEFORE the stop moved there - the INFQ same-bar self-stop.
    // That fix is correct and it stays. But it also made the reversal exit's
    // `cand := close` jump invisible on its OWN bar: the exit could only fire a
    // bar later, and by then `revNow` - a plain bool, not a var - had already
    // reset to false, so the marker fell through and mislabelled itself
    // "TRAILED STOP hit".
    //
    // MEASURED LIVE on CRWV 2026-08-11: the reversal fired at 16:17 and the X
    // printed at 16:18 reading "TRAILED STOP hit, entry 93.95 stop 97.32,
    // banked [n]R".
    //
    // A reversal exit is a DECISION taken on THIS bar's own close, not a level
    // being touched by a wick, so it neither needs nor may use the [1] guard.
    // ── v7.2 - WHILE THE OVERRIDE IS ON, A STOP BREACH MARKS BUT DOES NOT CLOSE ──
    //
    // v7.1 only suppressed the CONVICTION exits, so a stop hit still closed the
    // position and took the panel, the stop line and the targets with it. That is
    // the whole thing he was trying to avoid, so the stop is now included.
    //
    // `stHit` is what CLOSES the trade; `stRaw` is what actually happened to
    // price. Holding the trade open leaves stHit false, which means execution
    // falls through to the target tests below - so the take-profits keep working,
    // exactly as asked - and trDir stays non-zero, so the trail, the four lines
    // and the panel all carry on.
    //
    // ONE MARKER PER TRADE, and this was measured rather than assumed. The first build released
    // the latch whenever price came back to the favourable side of the stop, on the theory that
    // a fresh break was a fresh event. On BABA 2026-08-20 the stop sat right inside the chop and
    // that produced TEN X markers on one trade.
    bool stRaw = (trDir > 0 ? low <= stopRef : high >= stopRef) or revNow
    bool stHit = stRaw and not holdOn
    if holdOn and stRaw and not holdBreach
        stopNow    := true
        exitDir    := trDir
        holdBreach := true
    bool t1Hit = trDir > 0 ? high >= trTarget : low <= trTarget
    bool t2Hit = trDir > 0 ? high >= trTarget2 : low <= trTarget2
    bool t3Hit = trDir > 0 ? high >= trTarget3 : low <= trTarget3
    bool t4Hit = trDir > 0 ? high >= trTarget4 : low <= trTarget4
    if stHit
        stopNow := true
        exitDir := trDir
        // v5.4 - remember where we were stopped out so a re-entry can be judged
        // against it. The thesis has to beat THIS bar's extreme in the original
        // direction, not merely drift back through the stop level.
        reBar := bar_index
        reRef := trDir > 0 ? high : low
        // v6.1 - the level that actually stopped us out. Reclaiming THIS is the
        // event the re-entry is about.
        // v6.4 - on a REVERSAL exit the level that actually ended the trade is
        // this bar's jumped stop (the reversal close), not last bar's stopRef.
        // v8.14 - AND A REVERSAL EXIT ARMS NO RE-ENTRY AT ALL.
        // A re-entry means "the stop took us out and the ORIGINAL direction has
        // re-asserted". A reversal exit says the opposite: the move has turned, and the
        // reversal has ALREADY armed the other way for its own confirmation. Offering a
        // re-entry on top is both redundant and contradictory.
        //
        // SMCI 2026-08-11: print LONG, confirm 20:21, reversal 20:23 cancels it and arms SHORT -
        // and then a RE-ENTRY fired SHORT as well.
        //
        // Leaving reStop as na means the re-entry test can never fire for this exit, which is
        // the correct answer: the reversal owns what happens next. v8.15 - REVERTED FROM v8.14.
        // That build made a reversal exit suppress the re-entry, which invented a dependency
        // that does not exist. A re-entry has its own gates; if a later candle satisfies them it
        // is a valid re-entry whether or not a reversal happened, and the reversal's own arming
        // is untouched either way.
        //
        // v6.4 - on a REVERSAL exit the level that ended the trade is this bar's jumped
        // stop (the reversal close), not last bar's stopRef.
        reStop   := revNow ? trStop : stopRef
        // v8.15 - EVERYTHING THE RE-ENTRY IS JUDGED AGAINST IS FROZEN HERE, at the exit
        // of the trade being re-entered. impDir/movOpen/movRef all belong to whatever is
        // armed NOW, and a reversal rewrites all three on its way out, so reading them
        // later measured the re-entry against the wrong episode entirely.
        // v9.1 - read the BAR-START snapshot, not the live values. See the note at the
        // arm block: a reversal on this same bar has already overwritten movOpen and
        // cleared movRef by the time execution reaches here.
        reArmDir := trDirLast
        reArmOpen := movOpenBar0
        reArmMove := movRefBar0
        trDir := 0
    else
        if t1Hit and not trTp1Done
            tp1Now := true
            trTp1Done := true
        if t2Hit and not trTp2Done
            tp2Now := true
            trTp2Done := true
        if t4Hit and not trTp4Done
            tp4Now := true
            trTp4Done := true
            // v10.0 - T4 is a LEVEL AND A BADGE ONLY. It never closes the position.
            // The only close-on-target path in this file is the T3 one below, which
            // is gated on runPastT3 and ships off. Adding a second way for a trade
            // to end would change far more than the ladder.
        if t3Hit and not trTp3Done
            tp3Now := true
            trTp3Done := true
            // Closing at T3 is the v3.0 behaviour and stays the default. With
            // runPastT3 on, the badge still prints at +3R but the position is
            // handed to the trail instead of being closed, so a move that keeps
            // going keeps paying. exitDir is only set when the trade is actually
            // being closed here - otherwise the T3 badge would be placed using an
            // exit direction for a trade that is still open.
            if not runPastT3
                exitDir := trDir
                trDir := 0

// Push the levels forward for every bar of the session they were drawn in. They
// stop growing when the session block changes, so each set spans exactly its own
// extended-hours session and never bleeds into the next day.
//
// THE STOP LINE IS THE EXCEPTION - v3.6. It stops growing the moment the trade
// closes. Extending the final level across the rest of the session drew a red
// dashed line straight through later price action, and since the last trailed
// level sits close to the peak by design, that line lands right on top of the
// candles. It reads as a live stop sitting on the price when the trade actually
// ended minutes earlier. On CRWV that is the "putting the stop right where the
// price is" complaint: the level was 99.8865, the trade had already exited there,
// and the line just kept running.
//
// exitDir is included so the CLOSING bar still gets the segment - the exit block
// above clears trDir before this runs, which would otherwise cut the line one bar
// short of its own X marker.
//
// The TARGET lines do keep running for the whole session. Those are reference
// levels, not position state.
// v3.10, at the owner's request: levels now run for a FULL 24 HOURS from the session
// block they were drawn in, instead of stopping at the end of that extended
// session. That deliberately carries them through the following regular-hours
// session and into the next extended session, which is why `bucket >= 0` is gone
// - that test excluded regular hours and was what cut the lines at 20:00.
//
// Hardcoded rather than exposed as an input on purpose: adding an input shifts
// every stored input value by position on charts that already have the study, and
// would force a remove-and-re-add on every open tab for a number that is not
// going to be tuned.
//
// The stop line keeps its own extra condition - it still ends when the trade ends.
// The two rules are independent: 24h is how long a level may run, `trDir != 0` is
// whether the stop is still a live level at all. Extending a dead stop across a
// whole day is what made it read as a stop sitting on top of the price.
// ── v4.1: LEVELS RUN TO THE END OF THE NEXT REGULAR SESSION ─────────────────────
// The owner's spec, replacing the flat 24 hours: from the signal, carry through the
// extended session, through any overnight, through the following REGULAR trading
// day, and stop at the end of that regular day - before the next extended session.
//
// That is the same rule whether the signal was a morning pre-market print or an
// evening post-market one, which is why it is expressed in SESSIONS rather than in
// clock time. A flat 24 hours cut a 16:05 signal off at 16:05 the next day, in the
// middle of the following post-market.
//
// v6.4 - the `lnSawRth` latch this paragraph used to describe was written but
// never read, so it is REMOVED. The reasoning is kept, because it explains why
// a latch was the wrong shape: it would have stopped the levels the moment
// regular hours ended, which is a PAUSE, not an ending.
//
// v5.2 - THE SET NEEDS A TERMINAL STATE, NOT A PAUSE. The old single test
// `not (lnSawRth and not session.ismarket)` went TRUE AGAIN at 09:30 the next
// day, and the day after that, because session.ismarket is true again. It never
// ended a level set; it only skipped the hours between one regular session and
// the next, and line.set_x2 pushed the same lines forward again every morning.
// Measured on CSCO: confirm 2026-08-12 16:17, target lines reaching 2026-08-19
// 15:59 - having been pushed to 15:59 on 08-13, 08-14, 08-17 and 08-18 on the
// way. INFQ shows the same from a 16:11 confirm. That is the "lines run for ten
// days" report, and it is also why they always LOOKED like they ended at a
// regular close: they were PAUSED there, not finished.
//
// Clearing lnSess is what makes it terminal. Every block here is already gated
// on `not na(lnSess)`, so one assignment retires the whole set for good - no new
// variable, and above all no new INPUT, which would shift every stored input by
// position and force a remove-and-re-add on every open tab.
//
// The stop line only escaped this by accident: its extra `trDir != 0 or
// exitDir != 0` test froze it at the exit, which is why the runaway levels were
// always the TARGET lines.
// v5.5 - THE LEVELS BELONG TO THE TRADE, NOT TO THE SESSION.
//
// Two separate complaints, one root cause, one fix.
//
// (a) "the take profit lines go on forever." The session rule was still wrong even
//     after v5.2 gave it a terminal state: a level set outlives the trade it
//     describes, so on any symbol where the trade ended early the targets carried on
//     across days on their own. Tying extension to the SESSION was the mistake -
//     nothing about a regular-hours boundary has anything to do with whether a stop
//     and three targets are still meaningful.
//
// (b) "wherever the stop loss stops is the same spot where the take profit should
//     stop." Correct, and the old code could not do that: the stop line carried an
//     extra `trDir != 0 or exitDir != 0` test that the three target lines did not,
//     so they terminated in different places by construction.
//
// Now all four lines share ONE condition and stop on the same bar.
//
// WHY `stopNow` AND `tp3Now` ARE IN THE TEST. `trDir` is already zeroed further up
// this bar when a stop or a T3 close fires, so `trDir != 0` alone would freeze the
// levels one bar SHORT of the exit they belong to. The conviction exit needs no such
// term - it runs later in the script than this block, so trDir is still live here.
bool lnLive = trDir != 0 or stopNow or tp3Now or tp4Now

if showTradeLines and not na(lnSess)
    if lnLive
        if not na(lnStop)
            line.set_x2(lnStop, bar_index)
        if not na(lnTp1)
            line.set_x2(lnTp1, bar_index)
        if not na(lnTp2)
            line.set_x2(lnTp2, bar_index)
        if not na(lnTp3)
            line.set_x2(lnTp3, bar_index)
        if not na(lnTp4)
            line.set_x2(lnTp4, bar_index)
    else
        // Terminal, and unconditional. Every block here is gated on `not na(lnSess)`,
        // so this retires the whole set for good the moment the trade is over.
        lnSess := na

// Full invalidation - ONLY after a confirm, never after a bare print.
// The old version was gated on impBar (the print candle), so it fired on prints
// that never produced a confirm at all. That is why an "!" appeared before any
// yellow tick. Every exit marker in this script belongs to a CONFIRMED trade, so
// this one is gated on trBar like the rest.
if showInvalidation and not na(trBar) and bar_index > trBar and not na(impA) and not invFired
    int invDir = trDir != 0 ? trDir : exitDir
    if invDir != 0
        bool back = invDir > 0 ? low <= impA : high >= impA
        if back
            invNow := true
            invFired := true

// The + is drawn with plotshape, NOT label.new. This marker has failed to render
// three times, and label.new is garbage-collected once max_labels_count is passed
// while plotshape never is (the same lesson that fixed the print arrows in v1.7).
// Using plotshape removes that entire class of failure from the one marker that
// keeps going missing. It costs the dynamic tooltip; the Data Window readouts
// below carry the same numbers.
//
// addDir is captured at fire time rather than read from trDir here, because the
// exit block above may already have cleared trDir on this same bar - which would
// have drawn the badge on the wrong side of the candle.
// SIDE OF THE BAR - v3.4. The + used to sit on the SAME side as T1/T2/T3
// (below the bar on a long, above it on a short) at the same offset, so when a
// target and an add landed on one candle the two badges drew on top of each
// other and one of them was simply not there to click.
//
// The + is now on the OPPOSITE side from the target badges - above the bar on a
// long, below it on a short. That makes an overlap between the two families
// impossible rather than unlikely, and it reads better besides: the add sits in
// the direction the trade is travelling, the targets bank behind it.
plotshape(addNow and addDir > 0, "ADD — consolidation breakout (bullish)",
     style=shape.labeldown, location=location.abovebar,
     color=colConf, textcolor=colUp, text="+", size=size.small)
plotshape(addNow and addDir < 0, "ADD — consolidation breakout (bearish)",
     style=shape.labelup, location=location.belowbar,
     color=colConf, textcolor=colDn, text="+", size=size.small)

// ONE LABEL FOR ALL TARGETS REACHED ON THIS BAR - v3.4.
//
// T1 and T2 were separate labels drawn at the same yloc on the same side, so a
// bar that cleared both stacked them exactly on top of each other and one was
// simply not there to read. That is not hypothetical: CRWV 2026-08-11 clears [n]R
// AND +2R on the 16:16 bar, which is why that chart appears to show T2 and T3 but
// no T1 - T1 is underneath T2. A fast earnings move covers 1R and 2R inside one
// minute often enough that this needs to be structurally impossible, not unlikely.
//
// So the badges are merged instead of stacked: whatever was reached on this bar
// is named in a single label ("T1·T2"), and the tooltip lists each level.
int    tpDir = trDir != 0 ? trDir : exitDir
string tpTxt = (tp1Now ? "T1" : "") +
 (tp2Now ? (tp1Now ? "·T2" : "T2") : "") +
 (tp3Now ? ((tp1Now or tp2Now) ? "·T3" : "T3") : "") +
 (tp4Now ? ((tp1Now or tp2Now or tp3Now) ? "·T4" : "T4") : "")
if tpTxt != ""
    label.new(bar_index, tpDir > 0 ? low : high, tpTxt,
     yloc  = tpDir > 0 ? yloc.belowbar : yloc.abovebar,
     style = tpDir > 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down,
     color = colTp, textcolor = color.white, size = (tp3Now or tp4Now) ? size.small : size.tiny,
     tooltip = "entry " + str.tostring(trEntry, format.mintick) +
     (tp1Now ? "\nTP1  +" + str.tostring(TP1R, "#.##") + "R  " + str.tostring(trTarget,  format.mintick) : "") +
     (tp2Now ? "\nTP2  +" + str.tostring(TP2R, "#.##") + "R  " + str.tostring(trTarget2, format.mintick) : "") +
     (tp3Now ? "\nTP3  +" + str.tostring(TP3R, "#.##") + "R  " + str.tostring(trTarget3, format.mintick) : "") +
     (tp4Now ? "\nTP4  +" + str.tostring(TP4R, "#.##") + "R  " + str.tostring(trTarget4, format.mintick) : "") +
     (tp1Now ? "\n\nReaching +" + str.tostring(TP1R, "#.##") + "R steps the stop to " + str.tostring(ladderT1R, "#.##") + "R. THE TRADE CAN STILL LOSE - that is " + str.tostring(math.abs(ladderT1R) * 100, "#") + "% of the original risk still exposed, not breakeven. Breakeven is T2.\n\n(v9.1 corrected this text. It previously claimed the stop went to breakeven and that the trade could no longer lose. It never did - the rung has been " + str.tostring(ladderT1R, "#.##") + "R all along.)" : "") +
     (tp3Now ? (runPastT3 ? "\n\n[n]R reached and the position is STILL OPEN — the trail decides the exit from here." :
      "\n\n[n]R reached, trade closed. Turn on 'let the trail run past +3R' to hand it to the trailing stop instead.") : ""))

// The X now reports WHICH stop was hit and what it banked. Once the stop trails,
// "stop hit" stops meaning "the trade was wrong": a stop that has ratcheted to
// [n]R and then gets touched is a WIN being collected. Reading that off the chart
// is the whole point of building the trail, so the marker states it outright and
// is tinted green when the exit was profitable and grey when it was not.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v4.6 - TWO CONVICTION EXITS
//
// Both answer the same question - is the earnings impulse still what is moving
// this tape - and they answer it in two different failure modes, which is why they
// are two rules and not one:
//
//   FLAT TAPE   nothing is happening at all. Dead volume, no range, price on the
//               entry. Either the print was false or we arrived far too late.
//
//   EXHAUSTED   plenty is happening, it just is not going our way and never did.
//               AMD 2026-08-04: a 20-point chop for 17 hours that never once put
//               the trade more than [n]R in front.
//
// Neither may touch a trade that has worked. The flat exit needs open P/L on the
// entry; the exhausted exit needs a peak that never reached exhaustPeakR. Anything
// with a real gain in it is left entirely to the trailing stop.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
int   flatLen  = math.max(flatBars, 1)
float flatTrav = ta.highest(high, flatLen) - ta.lowest(low, flatLen)
float flatRvlM = ta.median(rvol, flatLen)

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// The owner's WMT case: the trade took the FLAT TAPE exit, he wanted to stay in it, and
// the moment it exited the table stopped reporting, the stop stopped trailing and
// the add markers stopped. He asked for a button on the table.
//
// PINE HAS NO BUTTONS. `table.cell` is display-only and the language has no click
// handler of any kind, so a control on the panel is genuinely impossible. An input
// is the only user-operable switch Pine offers, so that is what this is.
//
// WHAT IT DOES. It suppresses the CONVICTION exits - flat tape, exhausted, session
// end - for the trade that is open when it arms. It does NOT touch the structural
// or trailing stop, and that is deliberate: the stop is what DEFINES R, so
// overriding it would make every R figure the panel quotes a lie. The conviction
// exits are judgement calls layered on top of the stop, so suppressing them leaves
// the arithmetic honest. Say the word and the stop can be included too.
//
// NOTHING IS RECONSTRUCTED. Because the exit simply never fires, the position was
// never closed: trEntry, trRisk0, trBar, the stop, the ladder, the targets, the
// add markers and the panel all carry on from the ORIGINAL entry untouched. That
// is why this is not a re-entry and does not draw one - it is the same trade,
// still running, marked with its own HOLD badge.
//
// WHICH TRADE IT APPLIES TO. Leave the timestamp at its default and it applies to
// every trade - a blanket "never take conviction exits". Set it to a bar and it
// only arms for the trade that was already open at that bar, because it requires
// the chosen time to be at or after that trade's entry. A later trade cannot
// inherit it, and an earlier one can never reach it.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bool holdNow = false
if useHold and trDir != 0 and not holdOn
    bool holdAll   = na(holdTime) or holdTime <= 0
    bool thisTrade = holdAll or (not na(trBarTime) and holdTime >= trBarTime)
    bool reached   = holdAll or time >= holdTime
    if thisTrade and reached
        holdOn  := true
        holdNow := true

bool   convExit = false
string convWhy  = ""
float  convR    = na
if trDir != 0 and not na(trBar) and not na(trRisk0) and not na(trEntry) and trRisk0 > 0 and bar_index > trBar
    int   heldBars = bar_index - trBar
    float openR    = ((close - trEntry) * trDir) / trRisk0
    float peakR    = trPeak / trRisk0
    // v7.4 - `not holdOn` moved from the block gate onto these TWO branches only.
    // FLAT TAPE and EXHAUSTED are judgement calls about whether the move is still
    // working, so the override is entitled to overrule them. SESSION END is not a
    // judgement call - see the note on that branch below.
    if not holdOn and useFlatExit and heldBars >= flatBars and flatTrav < flatTravelR * trRisk0 and flatRvlM < flatRvolMax and math.abs(openR) <= flatOpenR
        convExit := true
        convWhy  := "FLAT TAPE"
        convR    := openR
    else if not holdOn and useExhaustExit and heldBars >= exhaustBars and peakR < exhaustPeakR and openR <= exhaustOpenR
        convExit := true
        convWhy  := "EXHAUSTED"
        convR    := openR
    // v5.8 - THE TRADE ENDS AT THE CLOSE OF THE FIRST REGULAR SESSION IT SEES.
    //
    // FN 2026-08-17: entered short at 600, went [n]R, and was still open 2.5 trading
    // days later because the ladder trail had nothing to stop it. An earnings trade
    // is a claim about how the market digests ONE release; once a full regular
    // session has traded on that information the edge is gone and what remains is an
    // ordinary swing position carried on an earnings-shaped stop.
    //
    // `trSawRth` latches during regular hours while the position is open, so the exit
    // fires on the first bar AFTER that session ends - never mid-session, and never
    // before the trade has had a full regular day to work.
    // ── SESSION END IGNORES THE HOLD OVERRIDE  (v7.4) ──────────────────────────
    //
    // Deliberately NOT gated on `not holdOn`, and this is the correction to a real
    // design error in v7.1.
    //
    // The distinction is the whole point. FLAT TAPE and EXHAUSTED
    // are OPINIONS about whether the move is still working - the override exists
    // precisely to overrule an opinion the trader disagrees with. SESSION END is
    // not an opinion. It is the DEFINITION of how long an earnings trade lives:
    // once a full regular session has traded on the release, the earnings edge is
    // spent and whatever is left is an ordinary swing position being carried on an
    // earnings-shaped stop. Staying in the trade means refusing a premature exit,
    // not converting the trade into something it was never meant to be.
    //
    // So this is the one boundary the override may not cross. `trSawRth` latches
    // outside this block, so it is already true by the time we get here.
    else if useSessionExit and trSawRth and not session.ismarket
        convExit := true
        convWhy  := "SESSION END"
        convR    := openR
    if convExit
        trDir := 0

// Latch AFTER the exit test, so the session the trade opened in cannot close it.
if trDir != 0 and session.ismarket
    trSawRth := true
if trDir == 0
    trSawRth := false
    // v7.1 - the override belongs to ONE trade. Whatever finally closed the
    // position - the stop, T3, or a conviction exit taken before the override
    // armed - releases it, so the next trade starts clean.
    holdOn     := false
    holdBreach := false

if convExit
    label.new(bar_index, high, "⊘",
     yloc = yloc.abovebar, style = label.style_label_down,
     color = color.new(#78909c, 15), textcolor = color.white, size = size.small,
     tooltip = convWhy + " EXIT at " + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) + "\n" +
     "open P/L  " + str.tostring(convR, "#.##") + "R\n" +
     "held      " + str.tostring(bar_index - trBar) + " bars\n" +
     "-----------------------\n" +
     (convWhy == "FLAT TAPE" ?
      "The tape went dead after entry. Over the last " + str.tostring(flatBars) +
      " bars total travel is under " + str.tostring(flatTravelR) +
      "R, median RVOL is under " + str.tostring(flatRvolMax) +
      ", and price is still sitting on the entry.\n\nThis is NOT a consolidation pullback. A pullback still carries range and volume and fails this test on the RVOL term alone. This is 'nothing is happening any more', which on an earnings trade means the print was false or the entry was far too late." :
      "The move never got going. " + str.tostring(exhaustBars) +
      " bars after entry this trade has never once been +" + str.tostring(exhaustPeakR) +
      "R in front, and it is not winning now either.\n\nAMD 2026-08-04 is the case this exists for: entry 466.47, and across the following 1,053 bars the trade NEVER exceeded [n]R in its favour. The high-water mark was set inside 15 minutes. It was then carried 17.5 hours into the next morning's bullish open and stopped out at [n]R.\n\nPast this point the earnings impulse is no longer what is moving price, and holding through a session boundary turns a directional read into an overnight-gap coin flip."))
    alert("CONVICTION EXIT (" + convWhy + ") - " + syminfo.ticker +
     " @ " + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) +
     " | " + str.tostring(convR, "#.##") + "R", alert.freq_once_per_bar)

if stopNow
    // v9.1 - NEVER BOOK A BETTER FILL THAN THE CANDLE ACTUALLY GAVE.
    // A reversal exit closes the trade AT THIS BAR'S CLOSE, and it advertises that in
    // its own tooltip. It proposes the close as the new stop, but that proposal runs
    // through the one-way ratchet, which REJECTS it when the close is already beyond
    // the stop - exactly the violent reversal candle this exit exists for. trStop then
    // keeps its old, better value and the booked R was reported off a level price never
    // filled at. Ordinary stop exits still book at trStop, which is a defensible fill
    // assumption; only the reversal path advertises a close-based fill, so only it has
    // to honour one. Taking the worse of the two can only ever make a result look worse
    // than before, never better, so it cannot flatter a trade that already worked.
    float bookLvl = revNow and not na(trStop) ?
     (exitDir > 0 ? math.min(trStop, close) : math.max(trStop, close)) : trStop
    float bookedR = not na(trRisk0) and trRisk0 > 0 and exitDir != 0 ?
     ((bookLvl - trEntry) * exitDir) / trRisk0 : na
    bool  paid    = not na(bookedR) and bookedR > 0.02
    string why    = holdOn ? "STOP LEVEL TOUCHED — trade HELD OPEN" :
     revNow ? "REVERSAL — cut early on bar character" :
     lockedStep > 0 or beMoved ? "TRAILED STOP hit" : "INITIAL STOP hit"
    label.new(bar_index, high, "✕",
     yloc = yloc.abovebar, style = label.style_label_down,
     // ONE appearance for the exit marker. v3.5 tinted it green when the trail had
     // banked a profit, which just produced an unexplained second symbol on the
     // chart. The outcome belongs in the tooltip, not in a colour you have to
     // decode.
     color = colStop, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small,
     tooltip = why +
     "\nentry " + str.tostring(trEntry, format.mintick) +
     // v9.1 - quote the level the trade is BOOKED at, not the ratcheted stop. On a
     // reversal candle that closed beyond the stop these differ, and the old text
     // named a price the fill never happened at.
     "   stop " + str.tostring(bookLvl, format.mintick) +
     (na(bookedR) ? "" : (holdOn ? "\nwould have banked " : "\nbanked ") + str.tostring(bookedR, "#.##") + "R") +
     (lockedStep > 0 ? "\nladder had locked " +
      str.tostring(ladderT1R + ladderStepR * (lockedStep - 1), "#.##") + "R" : "") +
     (holdOn ? "\n\nTHIS IS A MARKER, NOT AN EXIT. The stay-in-the-trade override is on, so the position was NOT closed - the stop line, the take-profit levels, the trail and the panel are all still live and still tracking from the original entry. The R figure above is what you WOULD have banked had it closed here.\n\nOnly the FIRST touch is marked. Price crossing back and forth over the stop does not add more markers." :
      revNow ? "\n\nThis bar was large for the session's ATR, closed at the end that hurts, carried elevated bucket-relative volume, and gave back a real fraction of the print candle — all four at once. That combination is a reversal rather than a pause, so the trade is cut here instead of waiting." +
       (math.abs(bookLvl - close) < syminfo.mintick / 2 ? " The exit is booked at this bar's close." :
        " NOTE: this candle closed at " + str.tostring(close, format.mintick) + ", BEYOND the trailed stop, so the exit is booked at the close - the worse of the two - rather than at the stop the ratchet was still holding.") :
      paid ? "\n\nThe stop had ratcheted into profit before it was touched, so this exit PAID. It is not a losing trade." :
      "\n\nThe stop was still at or below breakeven when it was touched. No R-expectancy figure is quoted here: the earlier one came from a non-TradingView feed and is void."))

// The badge itself is suppressed mid-trade (see revCoreNow), so the exit draws its own, one step
// further from price than the X so the two do not sit on top of each other. v8.12 - and only
// when the REAL reversal label did not already draw on this bar. Before v8.12 trigRev was
// blocked mid-trade so the badge was the only marker; now both fire on the same bar and two
// identical glyphs stack on top of each other.
if revNow and stopNow and not trigRev
    // v8.1d - an UP reversal belongs BELOW the bar, exactly like every other
    // up-reversal badge. This was pinned abovebar regardless of direction, so on INFQ
    float revBadgeOff = nz(atrSes, 0.0) > 0 ? 1.2 * atrSes : math.max(high - low, syminfo.mintick) * 1.2
    label.new(bar_index,
     trDirLast > 0 ? high + revBadgeOff : low,
     trDirLast > 0 ? revGlyphDn : revGlyphUp,
     yloc  = trDirLast > 0 ? yloc.price : yloc.belowbar,
     style = trDirLast > 0 ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up,
     // v8.1c - the REVERSAL colour, not the re-entry's purple. The first version of this badge
     // used #9c27b0, which is exactly the colour of the re-entry marker, so on INFQ it read as a
     // re-entry printing after a short trade on a green candle - a thing that cannot happen and
     // did not happen. The logic was right and the colour made it look wrong. v8.1e - IDENTICAL
     // to the reversal badge drawn anywhere else: the reversal colour behind it, the DIRECTION
     // colour in the glyph, and revSize. The first version used a white glyph at size.tiny,
     // which is why it read as a different, unfamiliar marker. Same symbol, extra location.
     color     = colRev,
     textcolor = trDirLast > 0 ? colDn : colUp,
     size      = revSize,
     tooltip = "REVERSAL ENDED THIS TRADE.   " + (revPath > 0 ? "PATH " + str.tostring(revPath) : "PATH n/a") + "\n" + "body vs move ref    " + str.tostring(nz(revGiveX, 0), "#.##") + "   need " + str.tostring(revImpGive, "#.##") + "\n" + "two-candle sum      " + str.tostring(nz(revGiveX, 0) + nz(revPrevGiveX, 0), "#.##") + "   need " + str.tostring(revPairSum, "#.##") + "\n" + "body vs normal min  " + str.tostring(nz(revSelfX, 0), "#.#") + "   need " + str.tostring(revSelfMult, "#.#") + "\n" + "close position      " + str.tostring(nz(revBarClosePos, 1), "#.##") + "   need <= " + str.tostring(revPairClose, "#.##") + "\n\n" + "This bar passed the same reversal test that draws a reversal badge when you are flat - magnitude against the whole move, closing at the end that hurts, against the direction of the trade. The X below is the exit it caused.\n\nThe opposite direction is not armed on this bar. Arming happens once the position is flat, so the re-entry machinery for the original direction cannot be hijacked mid-trade.")

if holdNow
    label.new(bar_index, trDirLast > 0 ? low : high, "HOLD",
     yloc  = trDirLast > 0 ? yloc.belowbar : yloc.abovebar,
     style = trDirLast > 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down,
     color = color.new(#00bcd4, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny,
     tooltip = "STAYING IN THE TRADE - manual override.\n\nFlat tape, exhausted and the stop are switched off for the REST OF THIS TRADE. The SESSION END exit still applies - the trade always finishes at the end of the first regular session that follows. Everything else is untouched and carries on from the original entry: the stop keeps trailing, the targets stand, the add markers still fire and the panel keeps reporting.\n\nThis is NOT a re-entry. Nothing was closed and nothing was re-opened - it is the same position, with the same entry price and the same R, still running.\n\nThe stop is deliberately NOT overridden. The stop is what defines R, so ignoring it would make every R figure on the panel meaningless. Only the judgement exits are suppressed.\n\nIt releases automatically when the trade actually ends, so the next trade starts clean.")

if invNow
    label.new(bar_index, high, "!",
     yloc = yloc.abovebar, style = label.style_label_down,
     color = color.new(#9e9e9e, 20), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny,
     tooltip = "Entire earnings move given back — price is back where it was before the print.\n\nInformational. On the consolidated-tape calibration the structural stop had always already fired by this point, which is why this marker is redundant in principle; that count is void on TradingView bars and has not been re-measured, so the marker is left visible rather than asserted redundant.")

// Last-resort visibility check. If this is ever ON and you still see no arrow,
// the problem is drawing, not detection.
plot(confirmNow ? 1 : 0, "· CONFIRMED (1=yes)", display=display.data_window)

// ── ADD-marker instrumentation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The + went missing three times because there was no way to see WHY from the
// chart. These make the decision auditable bar by bar in the Data Window:
// "window span" must be <= "span budget" for the pause to qualify, and the close
// must be beyond "window high" (long) or "window low" (short) to break out.
// If a + is missing, one of those three lines says so directly.
// v3.5 pullback-and-reclaim readouts. "pull in R" must land between the min and
// max pull settings, and the close must reach "reclaim level", for a + to print.
plot(addPeakPx,  "· ADD: peak price",    display=display.data_window)
plot(addPbExt,   "· ADD: pullback ext",  display=display.data_window)
plot(not na(addPeakPx) and not na(addPbExt) and not na(trRisk0) and trRisk0 > 0 ?
     math.abs(addPeakPx - addPbExt) / trRisk0 : na,
     "· ADD: pull in R",       display=display.data_window)
plot(not na(addPeakPx) and not na(addPbExt) ?
     addPbExt + (trDirLast != 0 ? trDirLast : 1) * addReclaim * math.abs(addPeakPx - addPbExt) : na,
     "· ADD: reclaim level",   display=display.data_window)
plot(na(addPeakBar) ? na : bar_index - addPeakBar,
     "· ADD: bars since peak", display=display.data_window)
plot(addCount,   "· ADD: count so far",  display=display.data_window)

// ── v4.6 DIAGNOSTICS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every one of these exists because a silent chart cost a session to diagnose.
// If the indicator draws nothing, read these five first and the cause is immediate.
plot(basePooled ? 1 : 0,        "· DIAG: pooled baseline",      display=display.data_window)
plot(thinRatio,                 "· DIAG: tape thinness",        display=display.data_window)
plot(rthDead ? 1 : 0,           "· DIAG: RTH gate skipped",     display=display.data_window)
plot(staleQuietOk ? 1 : 0,      "· STALE: quiet ok",            display=display.data_window)
plot(sqRvolMed,                 "· STALE: median RVOL",         display=display.data_window)
plot(sqRangeMed,                "· STALE: median range/ATR",    display=display.data_window)
plot(staleFire ? 1 : 0,         "· STALE: reset FIRED",         display=display.data_window)
plot(impConf ? 1 : 0,           "· STALE: impConf (blocks)",    display=display.data_window)
plot(revWinOk ? 1 : 0,          "· REV: inside window",         display=display.data_window)
plot(revPath,                   "· REV: path 1/2/3 (0=no)",   display=display.data_window)
plot(revSelfX,                  "· REV: body / normal minute", display=display.data_window)
plot(revBarClosePos,            "· REV: close pos in bar",     display=display.data_window)
plot(nz(revGiveX, 0),           "· REV: body / print BODY" ,   display=display.data_window)
plot(convExit ? 1 : 0,          "· EXIT: conviction exit",      display=display.data_window)
plot(impRng,     "· ADD: print range",   display=display.data_window)
plot(addNow ? 1 : 0, "· ADD FIRED (1=yes)", display=display.data_window)

// ── TRAIL instrumentation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Same principle as the ADD readouts above: if the stop moves when you did not
// expect it to, or fails to move when you did, these say why without guessing.
// Read them together - "trail: frozen" is the one that explains a stop that
// stayed put through a pullback.
plot(trStop,                       "· TRAIL: stop level",     display=display.data_window)
plot(trRisk0,                      "· TRAIL: original risk",  display=display.data_window)
plot(trPeak,                       "· TRAIL: peak excursion", display=display.data_window)
plot(not na(trRisk0) and trRisk0 > 0 ? trPeak / trRisk0 : na,
     "· TRAIL: peak in R",     display=display.data_window)
plot(lockedStep,                   "· TRAIL: ladder step",    display=display.data_window)
plot(trArmed ? 1 : 0,              "· TRAIL: armed (1=yes)",  display=display.data_window)
plot(consTight ? 1 : 0,            "· TRAIL: frozen (1=yes)", display=display.data_window)
plot(trailHold,                    "· TRAIL: structural held", display=display.data_window)
plot(trDir > 0 ? swingLo - trailBuf : swingHi + trailBuf,
     "· TRAIL: structural raw", display=display.data_window)
plot(revNow ? 1 : 0,               "· TRAIL: REVERSAL (1=yes)", display=display.data_window)
plot(holdOn ? 1 : 0,               "· HOLD: override active", display=display.data_window)

// ── STALE-PRINT RESET diagnostics - added v4.4 ─────────────────────────────────
// The reset is implemented and compiles but is NOT taking effect on ASTS 2026-08-10.
// These four make the failing term obvious in one look: open the Data Window, put the
// crosshair on the ASTS bar 8 bars after the false 16:05 print, and read them.
// Whichever guard reads wrong is the bug.
plot(na(impBar) ? -1 : bar_index - impBar, "· STALE: bars since print", display=display.data_window)

// ── REVERSAL test components ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The four numbers the reversal exit actually decides on, so a fire (or a
// non-fire) can be checked against the thresholds without re-deriving anything.
// "give / print" is the discriminating one: consolidation tops out well under
// the threshold, a real reversal blows past it.
plot(na(impRng) or impRng <= 0 ? na : (high - low) / impRng,
     "· REV: bar range / print", display=display.data_window)
plot(na(impRng) or impRng <= 0 or trDir == 0 ? na :
     (trDir > 0 ? (high - close) : (close - low)) / impRng,
     "· REV: give / print",      display=display.data_window)
plot(high - low <= 0 or trDir == 0 ? na :
     (trDir > 0 ? (close - low) : (high - close)) / (high - low),
     "· REV: close location",    display=display.data_window)
plot(nz(medVol, 0.0) > 0 ? volume / medVol : na,
     "· REV: vol / bucket med",  display=display.data_window)


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// DEBUG OVERLAY - off by default. See the input tooltip for how to read it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Whatever closed the position - stop, T3, or the conviction exit - record the bar.
// Placed at the very end so every exit path has already run.
if trDir == 0 and nz(trDir[1], 0) != 0
    exitBar := bar_index

plotshape(debugSess and bucket >= 0, "· debug: extended-hours bar", style=shape.circle,
     location=location.bottom, color=color.new(#2962ff, 30), size=size.tiny)





